Let me be honest with you first: the "vector DB + hashmap of relationships" pattern fails because it's not a database — it's a federation. Each subsystem has its own writes, its own consistency model, its own index, its own query planner, and the only thing tying them together is application-level glue. The moment you want to ask "find me the top 10 documents semantically near X, where the author follows author Y in the graph, that mention 'derivatives', written in the last 30 days, ranked by a hybrid score" — you can't push that down. You're doing N round trips, post-filtering at the client, and the planner can't reorder by selectivity. That's the real problem. Not the lack of features, but the lack of a single execution model.

So before we talk about how to build it, let me give you what I think the actual thesis of an "AI-native database" should be, because most things calling themselves that are still federations with prettier wrappers.

The thesis: a database is AI-native when vectors, text, graph, and relational data share one storage engine, one transaction log, one query planner, and one cost model. Anything less is plumbing. Three properties follow from that thesis, and they're the hard part:

A unified physical record. A row in a "documents" table isn't a row plus a vector hanging off in another system. It's a single record whose columns can be scalars, vectors, posting-list-indexable text, or graph endpoints. They land in the same WAL entry. They're versioned together under MVCC. A failed embed rolls back the row.
A unified query plan. SELECT … WHERE vec_sim(emb, $q) > 0.8 AND text_match(body, 'derivatives') AND EXISTS (PATH(author, follows*1..3, $u)) compiles to one logical plan, gets reordered by a CBO that has cardinality estimates for vector search (recall vs. selectivity), text search (posting list size), and graph traversal (avg out-degree). This is where DataFusion is a good substrate — its logical/physical split lets you add custom operators without forking the planner.
A unified storage format. Arrow in memory, an extension of Parquet (or a custom columnar format) on disk, with extension types for HNSW pages, CSR-encoded adjacency, and FOR-compressed posting lists. One LSM tree, one compaction strategy that understands all four index types.
If you nail those three, the rest — SDK, API, managed deployment — is normal infra work. If you don't, you're building OriginChain v1 again with better marketing.

Concrete architecture I'd reach for given your background:

Storage layer in Rust on top of parquet + arrow with custom extension types. Use sled or a custom LSM (you've done this with vekta) as the metadata + LSM substrate. WAL using redb or roll your own — you've earned that — but one log for everything. RocksDB is the boring safe choice if you want to skip a year of work; pay the JNI/FFI tax and don't apologize.

Index layer with four primitives, all writing through the same WAL: HNSW (with provision to swap to DiskANN for >10M vectors per shard), FST + positional posting lists for BM25, CSR adjacency with property arrays for graph, and standard B+tree/LSM for relational. The non-obvious bit: HNSW graph edges are themselves a graph — store them in the same adjacency format your "graph" subsystem uses. You get shared compaction and traversal code essentially free.

Query layer on DataFusion. Custom LogicalPlan nodes for VectorSearch, TextMatch, GraphPath. Custom PhysicalPlan operators that can stream results. Critically: a HybridScore operator that takes K candidate streams and fuses them (RRF, weighted, learned ranker as pluggable strategies). Cardinality stats for vector indexes — you need to estimate "how many vectors fall within distance d of q" because that drives whether you start with the vector predicate or filter first.

AI-native-ness as schema features, not bolt-ons: columns declare EMBED USING model='...' ON CHANGE, the DB owns embedding generation lazily or eagerly; embedding model versioning is a first-class concept so you can dual-read during migrations; every record carries provenance (which model, which source, when) as system columns; recency-weighted scoring as a built-in.

The hard problems you should sign up for clear-eyed:

The cost-based optimizer is the thing that takes longest and is most invisible until it's wrong. Cardinality estimation for ANN under filters is a genuinely open research area — read up on Filtered-DiskANN and ACORN before you commit to an approach. Hybrid scoring fusion is mathematically not a solved problem; RRF is the safe default but isn't optimal. Transactional updates to HNSW indexes are painful — most production HNSW implementations are append-mostly with periodic rebuilds; if you want true MVCC over HNSW you're either doing copy-on-write at the page level or accepting that deletes are tombstones until compaction.

One concrete question worth deciding now, because it changes everything downstream: single-node first, or distributed from day one? Single-node lets you build the unified storage/query story correctly and is a real, sellable product (think DuckDB-for-AI). Distributed multiplies the work by 5x and forces compromises in the storage layer (sharding key, cross-shard joins, distributed graph traversal). My honest take: do single-node first, write the storage and query layers so distribution is possible but not present, ship it, then layer distribution on top using something like FoundationDB or a Raft-based shard manager once you have customers pulling for it. OriginChain's single-tenant AWS deployment is actually a strength here — you don't need multi-tenant distribution to be commercially viable.

