This is a genuinely interesting instinct and I want to take it seriously rather than just validate it. Let me steelman it first, then be properly critical, because there are two distinct ideas tangled together here and one of them is much stronger than the other.

What you've actually said, untangled:

The unit of storage is an event-like thing — a record that exists at a point in time, with content.
Relationships are formed at write time, not query time — when the record lands, it eagerly connects to spatially-near (semantically similar) and temporally-near (co-occurring) records.
The physical layout is row-shaped, but the logical behavior is node-shaped — every record is implicitly a graph node whether or not the schema says "graph."
Idea 3 is the strong one. Ideas 1 and 2 have real problems. Let me go through them.

Why "row that behaves like a node" is actually a powerful frame.

In every existing system, "is this thing a row or a node?" is a schema-time decision the user makes, and it's almost always wrong in retrospect. You declare a users table and then six months later you wish users could traverse to other users without a join. Or you build a graph in Neo4j and then desperately wish you could do an analytical aggregation across all nodes without traversing.

If every physical record has a stable identity (a row ID that's also a node ID) and edges are simply typed references between row IDs, then "graph" stops being a separate storage modality. It becomes an access pattern. A foreign key is an edge. A vector neighbor is an edge. A temporal predecessor is an edge. The HNSW graph itself is just edges with type "vector_neighbor". You don't have a graph subsystem; you have a single record store with multiple edge indexes over it.

That's the insight worth keeping, and it's not what most people who say "unified database" actually build. It collapses a real category. Hold onto it.

Now the critical part. Where the memory metaphor leads you wrong.

First problem: eager relationship formation at write time doesn't scale and isn't even what you want.

The human brain is doing something specific: it has ~86 billion neurons, massively parallel hardware, no durability requirement (memories degrade, that's fine), and consolidation happens during sleep over hours. When you write "every block of memory automatically makes relationships with similar concepts," you're describing an O(N) operation per insert against the entire existing dataset. At 10M records that's 10M similarity computations per write. At 100M it's a non-starter.

You might say "but HNSW already does this approximately" — yes, but HNSW is doing it for the index, with a bounded number of comparisons (ef_construction, typically 200), not for the data model. The relationships HNSW forms are index entries for retrieval, not semantic facts about the record. Conflating those two is exactly the OriginChain v1 trap — relationships became a hashmap because they were neither real edges nor real index entries, they were a confused middle thing.

The deeper issue: which relationships should form eagerly? Semantic similarity in embedding space? Temporal proximity? Co-occurrence in some session? Causality? Each of these is a different graph, and pre-computing all of them at write time means you've decided the workload before you know it. That's the opposite of what a database should do.

The right move is: edges that the schema declares are eager (foreign keys, explicit graph edges); edges that come from indexes are lazy and approximate (HNSW neighbors, posting list co-occurrence); edges that come from queries are computed on demand and optionally materialized as views.

Second problem: "blocks of memory entering at a specific time" smuggles in an append-only event-log assumption.

This is seductive because event logs are clean and Kafka-shaped and immutable and easy to reason about. But it's the wrong abstraction for a database that has to support updates, deletes, GDPR erasure, schema evolution, and MVCC. The moment you say "well, updates are just new events that supersede old ones," you've just described Datomic, and Datomic is a real system worth studying but it's also a system that made specific painful tradeoffs (read amplification, query complexity) precisely because of that model.

I'd separate two things you're conflating: records are versioned through time (which is MVCC and is great) versus records are events (which is event sourcing and is a different beast). You want the first. You don't want the second.

Third problem: the metaphor hides where the work actually happens.

Human memory feels effortless from the inside because you're not aware of the consolidation, the forgetting, the reconstruction during recall. A database doesn't have that luxury — every cost is explicit and paid by someone (write throughput, query latency, storage cost, recall quality). The memory metaphor lets you wave at "relationships form automatically" without saying who pays for it and when. That's the question that has to be answered concretely before the metaphor is useful.

What I'd take from your instinct, and what I'd discard:

Keep: every physical record has a stable identity that doubles as a graph node ID. Edges are first-class and typed. The graph isn't a separate subsystem; it's a view over the record store enabled by typed reference columns and edge indexes.

Keep: time is a first-class dimension of the record. Every record knows when it was written, and the system can answer temporal queries cheaply. This is genuinely AI-native because LLM workloads are heavily recency-weighted.

Discard: eager all-to-all relationship formation at write time. This is the seductive part of the metaphor and it's a trap. Relationships are declared (schema), indexed (approximate, bounded work), or computed (queries). Pick which per relationship type; don't pretend they're all the same thing.

Discard: the event-log framing. Use MVCC for versioning. Records get updated; old versions are retained until GC; this is well-understood and correct.

A sharper formulation to chew on:

A physical record is a typed, versioned tuple with a stable identity. Its columns can be scalars, vectors, text, or typed references to other record identities. Indexes over the record store come in four flavors — B-tree (on scalars), HNSW (on vectors), posting list (on text), and adjacency (on typed references) — and all four are built from the same primitive: "given this column's values, give me an efficient way to find records." The graph isn't a thing you stored. It's a thing you index.

If you accept that framing, here's the sharper question it forces: should the adjacency index over typed references be physically separate from the records, or should it be inlined into the record itself (each record carries its outgoing edges)? Postgres-style would say separate (the index is its own structure). Neo4j-style would say inlined (each node has its edge list adjacent to it on disk for fast traversal). The choice has massive consequences for write amplification, traversal latency, and how compaction works.

