Good — you're right that Neo4j's traversal speed comes from inlining, and the reason is worth understanding precisely before we commit, because the same mechanism that makes Neo4j fast at traversal makes it slow at scans, and we need to know which trade we're actually making.

Why Neo4j is fast at traversal: the actual mechanism

Neo4j stores nodes in a fixed-size record file. Every node record is exactly 15 bytes (in their classic store format): a few flags, a pointer to its first relationship, a pointer to its first property, and a label reference. Because records are fixed-size, finding node N is pure arithmetic: offset = N * 15. No B-tree lookup. No hash. Just multiply and seek.

Relationships are stored in their own file, also fixed-size (~34 bytes), and crucially each relationship record contains four pointers: prev and next relationship for the source node, prev and next for the target node. So relationships form a doubly-linked list that both endpoints participate in. To traverse "all friends of user 42," you go to node 42 (arithmetic), follow its firstRel pointer (one seek), then walk the linked list (sequential pointer-chases).

This is sometimes called "index-free adjacency" in their marketing, which is misleading — there is an index, it's just that the index is encoded as pointer arithmetic instead of a B-tree. What's genuinely true is that traversal cost is proportional to the result size, not the dataset size. A 3-hop query in a 10-billion-node graph is the same cost as in a 10-thousand-node graph if the local neighborhood is the same size. That's the property worth keeping.

The cost of this design — what Neo4j gave up to get traversal speed

Three things, and you need to feel each one before deciding.

Fixed-size records destroy columnar efficiency. When you SELECT AVG(age) FROM users, Neo4j has to read every full node record plus chase property pointers to find the age field. A columnar store reads only the age column, sequentially, often compressed 10x. For analytical workloads Neo4j is between 10x and 1000x slower than DuckDB. This isn't a minor weakness; it's structural.

Properties are stored separately and indirectly. Because the node record is fixed-size, variable-length data (strings, vectors, JSON) lives in a separate property store reached by pointer. Reading a node's properties is a second seek. For vector embeddings — which are 1536 floats = 6KB — this is catastrophic. You cannot afford a pointer chase per vector when you're doing a similarity scan over 10M vectors.

Updates are painful. Fixed-size records mean you can't grow a node in place. Adding a relationship means appending to the relationship file and patching pointers in the linked list — multiple page writes per edge insert. Concurrent edge inserts on the same hot node contend on the same linked-list head.

So the honest question: do we actually want full Neo4j-style inlining?

I don't think we do. I think we want something more careful. Let me propose a hybrid that I'll defend, and you push back.

The proposal: PAX-style records with inlined edge stubs

PAX ("Partition Attributes Across") is a layout invented at CMU around 2001 that's now used in basically every modern analytical engine, including DuckDB and Snowflake. The idea: data is organized into row groups (say, 10,000 records per group), and within a row group the storage is columnar. So you get row-group-level locality (related records near each other on disk) plus columnar efficiency (within a row group, all values of column X are contiguous and compressible).

Here's what I'd do for our physical record:

Each row group on disk has a header listing column offsets, then the column data laid out columnar. The columns include the usual scalar types, plus three special column types: vector columns (stored as contiguous float arrays, ready for SIMD), text columns (stored as a dictionary + positions, ready for posting list construction), and edge columns. The edge column is the key idea.

An edge column stores, for each row in the row group, a small inline stub: the count of outgoing edges of this type, and either (a) the edges themselves if the count is below a threshold (say, ≤8), or (b) a pointer to an overflow page if the count exceeds the threshold. The edges themselves are pairs of (target_row_id, optional_edge_property_offset).

Why this works:

For the long-tail of nodes with few edges — which is most nodes in most real graphs, since edge distributions are power-law — the edges are inlined in the record's row group. Traversal cost is one row-group read, no pointer chase. You get Neo4j-class traversal speed for the common case.

For the heavy hitters — the celebrity nodes with millions of edges — the inline stub points to an overflow structure (a CSR-encoded edge block, possibly compressed with Roaring bitmaps if the edges are dense). One indirection, but only for the nodes that would have been a bottleneck anyway, and the overflow structure is itself columnar and scannable.

For analytical scans — SELECT AVG(age) — you read only the age column from each row group. The edge columns are skipped entirely. You retain DuckDB-class scan speed.

For vector similarity — the vector column within a row group is a contiguous float array. SIMD-friendly. The HNSW index points into row groups, not into individual rows, which means HNSW graph edges are themselves stored as an edge column on a synthetic "vector_index" table. The vector index is a graph, stored the same way as every other graph in the system.

The atomic record, in detail

When you write INSERT INTO documents (id, title, body, embedding, authored_by) VALUES (...):

The row lands in the currently-open row group for the documents table — which is in memory, not yet on disk. The id column, title column (compressed dictionary), body column (with a text-index entry queued), embedding column (a slot in the contiguous float array), and authored_by edge column (one outgoing edge stub) all get their values appended.

When the row group fills (say, 10K rows) or the transaction commits (depending on durability mode), the row group is flushed to disk as a single immutable unit. The HNSW index gets updated to point new vector entries at this row group + offset. The text index gets updated with the new posting list entries.

The row ID of a record is (table_id, row_group_id, offset_within_group). This is the stable node identity. Edges point to row IDs. Indexes point to row IDs. Vector neighbors are row IDs.

The thing this design buys us that Neo4j can't do

Because row groups are immutable once flushed, they're naturally snapshot-able. MVCC becomes trivial: a transaction sees the row groups visible at its start timestamp. Updates produce new row groups; old ones GC when no transaction can see them. This is exactly how DuckDB and Lance handle versioning, and it's why they have clean transactional semantics that graph databases struggle with.

You also get cheap analytical queries, cheap vector scans, cheap graph traversals — but with a real tradeoff I want to name: writes get batched. You can't have a transaction commit until its row group is durable. For high-throughput streaming inserts this means group commit (collect N inserts, flush together) which adds latency. For low-write workloads it's irrelevant. For our likely workload (AI applications writing embeddings, conversations, documents) it's fine, but if you imagine someone wanting OLTP-style 100K single-row inserts per second with sub-ms latency, this design will disappoint them.

The thing you should push on

The threshold for inlining edges vs. overflowing is a tuning parameter and it interacts badly with row-group boundaries. If a node has 7 edges inlined and you add an 8th, you need to either rewrite the row group (expensive) or transition to overflow (which means the inline stub now contains a pointer rather than edges, which means the row group's edge column has variable-shape entries). DuckDB handles variable-length data via vector-of-strings tricks but it's genuinely awkward.

The clean answer is: all edges go to a separate edge log, indexed by source row ID, and "inlining" is a cache effect — if a row group has few enough edges per row, we materialize them inline as a read optimization during compaction; if not, we don't. The edge log is the source of truth. The inline copy is an index.

That's slightly less aggressive than full Neo4j inlining and gives up some single-record traversal latency, but it cleanly separates correctness (the log) from optimization (the inlined copy), which is the discipline that keeps databases from going insane over time.

So my real recommendation: edges live in an append-only edge log keyed by source row ID; row groups contain a materialized edge column that's a cache of edges as of the row group's snapshot timestamp; compaction merges new edges from the log into refreshed row groups. You get Neo4j-class traversal latency on warm data, clean MVCC, clean recovery, and the edge log is itself a columnar structure that's cheap to scan.

Does that framing land? And the specific question I want your gut on: are you willing to pay the group-commit write latency for the structural benefits, or is sub-millisecond single-row insert latency a hard requirement for your imagined workload? Because that answer changes whether row groups are the right unit or whether we need something more like an LSM memtable that flushes into row-group format later.

