First, the OLTP terminology — and I'm going to be picky here because it matters.

You wrote OTLP twice. Those are two different things and the confusion is common enough that it trips people up:

OLTP = Online Transaction Processing. Row-at-a-time inserts/updates, sub-ms latency, high concurrency. Postgres, MySQL, Oracle. This is what you mean.
OTLP = OpenTelemetry Protocol. The wire format for shipping observability data. What Lalith's GreptimeDB fork at Middleware ingests. Completely unrelated.
You're thinking OLTP. I'll use that from here on.

Now, the real question: why did I say row groups are bad for OLTP when real OLTP systems achieve 100K+ inserts/sec?

Because real OLTP systems don't use row groups as their write path. They use a fundamentally different structure for writes, and only convert to columnar formats later — if at all.

Let me show you what Postgres actually does when you insert a row, because this is where the picture sharpens.

Postgres has 8KB pages. When you INSERT, it finds a page with free space in the target table's heap file, writes the row tuple into that page (variable-length, in-place), updates the page header, writes a WAL entry, and returns. The WAL flush is the durability moment — typically sub-ms with a fast SSD. The page itself might not hit disk for seconds; the buffer pool keeps it in memory. Indexes (B-trees) get updated similarly: navigate to the leaf page, insert the entry, log it, done.

The atomic write unit is a row in a page. Pages are mutable in place. There's no "wait for a batch to fill." There's no "the row group flushes when it's full." A single row commits in microseconds because all the system has to do is append to a WAL and dirty a page in memory.

This is the row-store world. It's optimized for: lots of small writes, point lookups, row-at-a-time updates, sub-ms commit latency. It's bad at: scanning a billion rows to compute an average (because you read all columns even if you want one), compressing data efficiently (rows mix types, so compression is per-row and weak), vectorized execution (SIMD wants contiguous arrays of the same type).

DuckDB-style row groups are the opposite design. They're optimized for: scanning a billion rows fast, compressing aggressively, SIMD execution. They're bad at: point writes with sub-ms latency, because the write unit is a 10K-row block.

Postgres-style and DuckDB-style live at opposite ends of a real tradeoff curve, and neither one is what we want, because we want both behaviors at once.

The thing real modern systems do — and what we should do — is have two write paths that converge.

This is called an LSM tree, or more generally a write-optimized-then-merged architecture, and it's what RocksDB, Cassandra, ScyllaDB, ClickHouse, and basically every system built since 2010 uses. Let me describe it the way it should work for us.

When you INSERT:

The row is appended to the WAL — a sequential file on disk. One write, sub-ms. This is the durability moment. The transaction can commit here.
The row is also placed in an in-memory memtable — a sorted structure (skiplist, B-tree, whatever) holding recent writes. Queries check the memtable first, then disk. The memtable is mutable; rows in it can be updated or deleted in place.
When the memtable hits a size threshold (say 64MB) or a time threshold, it's flushed to disk as an immutable row group (or "SSTable" in LSM jargon, but conceptually the same thing). The flush is a background operation. The foreground write path doesn't wait for it.
Background compaction merges small row groups into bigger ones, deduplicates updates, removes deleted rows, and (this is the part I'm adding for us) refreshes inlined edge columns, rebuilds local vector index pages, etc.
This is the standard answer to your question, and it dissolves the tension entirely. Writes commit at WAL speed (sub-ms). Reads hit the memtable for recent data and row groups for older data. Row groups are immutable, but the system is fully mutable because new versions of a row land in newer memtables/row groups and supersede old ones at read time. Old versions get GC'd during compaction.

So when I said "row groups make sub-ms inserts hard," I was being sloppy — I should have said "row groups as the write unit make sub-ms inserts hard, but row groups as the post-flush storage unit in an LSM are exactly how you get both."

Now, "this will not be editable, right?"

The honest answer is: row groups themselves are immutable, but the database is fully editable. The mechanism is what I just described — updates land in the memtable, become new versions of the row, supersede old versions at read time, and old versions get compacted away. From the user's perspective, UPDATE documents SET title = '...' WHERE id = 42 works exactly like Postgres. From the storage's perspective, the old version sits in a row group on disk and a new version sits in the memtable, both visible to the read path with the newer one winning.

This is also how Postgres actually handles updates internally, by the way — Postgres doesn't update rows in place either, it writes a new tuple and marks the old one dead. The difference is that Postgres does this within mutable pages and relies on VACUUM to reclaim space, while LSM systems do it across immutable row groups and rely on compaction. Different mechanisms, same logical semantics, similar amounts of pain.

The thing that's genuinely different in our system, and why this matters more than it does in Postgres:

Edges. In Postgres, an UPDATE on a row touches one heap page and a few index pages. In our system, if a row has an authored_by edge column with an inlined copy of the edge, updating that edge means the materialized inlined edge in an old row group is stale. That's fine — the read path knows the row group is shadowed by a newer version — but it means compaction has more work to do: when it merges row groups, it has to reconcile edge versions, which is more expensive than reconciling scalar columns.

The discipline that keeps this sane is what I said last turn: the edge log is the source of truth. Inlined edges in row groups are a cache. When we update an edge, we write to the edge log (cheap, sub-ms). The inlined copy becomes stale and gets refreshed at compaction time. Reads of recent edges go to the log; reads of cold edges go to the inlined copy in the row group. Compaction merges the two.

This is exactly the discipline RocksDB uses for everything, exactly the discipline ClickHouse uses for materialized columns, exactly the discipline modern systems converge to. We're not inventing it. We're applying it consistently across all four index types (B-tree, HNSW, posting list, adjacency), which is the part nobody has done well.

So the revised picture, top to bottom:

A write commits when it's in the WAL. Sub-ms. This handles your OLTP concern entirely.
A write is queryable immediately because it also lives in the memtable.
The memtable is a mutable row-shaped structure optimized for writes and point reads.
Periodically the memtable flushes to a row group on disk — immutable, columnar, PAX-laid-out, with the four index types we discussed.
Compaction merges row groups in the background, refreshing materialized indexes (inlined edges, local HNSW pages, local posting lists) as it goes.
The read path is a merge of memtable + row groups, newest version wins, with the cost model knowing which structure to hit first.
This gives you sub-ms inserts, full editability, columnar scan speed, fast graph traversal on warm data, MVCC for free, and a path to clean recovery (replay the WAL into the memtable on restart).

The remaining honest tradeoff is write amplification: a single logical write eventually gets rewritten multiple times as data moves through compaction levels. RocksDB has a typical write amp of 10-30x. That's the price for this architecture. It's why LSM-tree systems aren't right for pure write-once-read-never workloads, and why some folks still prefer pure B-trees. For our workload — embeddings, conversations, documents, mixed reads — it's the right tradeoff by a wide margin.

