Two clean questions, let me take them in order.

First — Cockroach Labs and Postgres.

No. Postgres predates CockroachDB by about 30 years. The actual history:

Postgres started in 1986 at UC Berkeley under Michael Stonebraker as the successor to Ingres (hence "post-Ingres"). It became open-source PostgreSQL in 1996 when SQL support was added. It's developed by the PostgreSQL Global Development Group — a distributed community of contributors, no single company owns it.

CockroachDB was founded in 2015 by three ex-Google engineers (Spencer Kimball, Peter Mattis, Ben Darnell) who had worked on Google's Colossus and Spanner. CockroachDB is wire-compatible with Postgres — meaning you can point a Postgres client at it and it speaks the same protocol — but the internals are completely different. CockroachDB is a distributed SQL database built on top of a Raft-replicated key-value store (originally RocksDB, now their own Pebble engine). Postgres is a single-node relational database with a heap+B-tree storage engine.

The wire compatibility is a marketing and adoption choice — it means you don't have to rewrite your application to migrate — but the storage, transactions, replication, and query execution are entirely Cockroach's own work. Conflating them is a common mistake because Cockroach leans hard into "Postgres-compatible" in their messaging. They didn't create Postgres, they cloned the protocol on top of a fundamentally different engine.

(Worth knowing: the same pattern exists with YugabyteDB, which is also Postgres-wire-compatible but distributed. And with Aurora Postgres, which is Postgres's actual code but with the storage layer replaced by Amazon's. Wire-compatible ≠ same system.)

Second — how Postgres and MongoDB actually do fast inserts and updates.

These are two genuinely different designs and they illustrate two ends of the spectrum. Let me walk through each in real mechanical detail, because the details matter for what we're building.

Postgres: heap + B-tree, mutable pages, MVCC via tuple versioning

A Postgres table is a heap file. Just an ordered sequence of 8KB pages. Each page has a header, a line pointer array (pointers to tuples within the page), and the tuples themselves growing from the bottom of the page up while line pointers grow from the top down. They meet in the middle.

When you INSERT INTO users (id, name, age) VALUES (1, 'Prajwal', 32):

Postgres consults the Free Space Map (FSM) — a separate small file that tracks which pages have room. It finds a page with enough free bytes for the new tuple. O(1) lookup.
It acquires a lightweight lock on that page (called an LWLock — not a row lock, just a brief latch to prevent concurrent writers from corrupting the page structure).
It writes the tuple into the page's free space, adds a line pointer in the header, updates the page's free-space accounting.
It writes a WAL record describing the change — "tuple inserted at page 42, offset 17, with these bytes" — to the WAL buffer in memory.
For each index on the table (primary key B-tree, any secondary indexes), it navigates to the appropriate leaf page, inserts the index entry, writes a WAL record for the index change.
At COMMIT, the WAL buffer is flushed to disk (fsync). This is the durability moment. The actual heap page might not hit disk for seconds.
The page is now "dirty" in the buffer pool. A background writer process eventually flushes it. A separate checkpointer process periodically forces all dirty pages to disk so the WAL can be truncated.
Total foreground work: a few memory writes plus one sequential WAL append. On a fast NVMe, that's tens of microseconds. The reason Postgres can do 100K+ inserts/sec isn't magic — it's that the durable write is a single sequential append, and everything else happens in RAM and lazily.

Now, updates. This is where Postgres gets interesting and where its biggest weakness lives.

When you UPDATE users SET age = 33 WHERE id = 1:

Find the tuple via the primary key index. Read its page into the buffer pool.
Do not modify it in place. Instead, write a new tuple with age = 33 somewhere — ideally on the same page (this is called a HOT update, "Heap-Only Tuple"), otherwise on another page.
Mark the old tuple as superseded by setting its xmax to the current transaction ID. The old tuple now says "I was valid until transaction N." The new tuple says "I became valid at transaction N."
Update the index entries — unless it's a HOT update where no indexed columns changed and the new tuple fits on the same page, in which case the index can still point at the old tuple and follow the HOT chain to find the new one. This optimization is huge for high-update workloads.
WAL the whole thing.
The reason for the new-tuple-instead-of-overwrite is MVCC. Other transactions running concurrently might still need to see the old version. Each tuple carries xmin (transaction that created it) and xmax (transaction that killed it), and a transaction with snapshot timestamp T sees a tuple iff xmin <= T < xmax. This gives you snapshot isolation for free, with no read locks.

The cost: dead tuples accumulate. Every update leaves a corpse. Deletes don't actually remove data, they just set xmax. Over time, pages fill with dead tuples and the table bloats. VACUUM is the cleanup process that scans pages, identifies tuples no transaction can see anymore, and reclaims their space. AUTOVACUUM runs this in the background based on heuristics. Tuning autovacuum is one of the perennial pains of running Postgres at scale — get it wrong and your tables bloat to 10x their actual data size, indexes get fragmented, and performance degrades.

So Postgres's "fast insert" story is: WAL is the durability hot path, everything else is lazy. Its update story is "every update is logically an insert plus a soft-delete, and VACUUM is the price you pay."

This is the design tradition our LSM approach descends from but reinterprets. Postgres does versioning within mutable pages and cleans up with VACUUM. LSMs do versioning across immutable row groups and clean up with compaction. Same logical semantics, different physical mechanism, different operational pain.

MongoDB: documents in BSON, WiredTiger underneath

MongoDB is more interesting than its reputation suggests because the storage engine isn't really "MongoDB," it's WiredTiger — a separate storage engine they acquired in 2014 (the same WiredTiger written by the original BerkeleyDB authors). Everything mechanical about MongoDB's writes happens in WiredTiger.

WiredTiger is essentially a B-tree (with an LSM option that almost nobody uses). Documents are stored in BSON format — a binary, length-prefixed encoding of the JSON document. Each collection is a B-tree keyed by _id. Indexes are separate B-trees.

When you db.users.insertOne({_id: 1, name: 'Prajwal', age: 32}):

The document is serialized to BSON in memory.
WiredTiger navigates the _id B-tree to the appropriate leaf page (in its in-memory page cache).
The document is inserted into the leaf page. WiredTiger uses a clever scheme called page reconciliation — it doesn't immediately modify the page on disk. Instead, the in-memory page has a "skiplist of updates" attached to it. New writes go into this skiplist.
The write is logged to WiredTiger's journal (their WAL). By default this happens every 100ms in batches, which is why MongoDB has weaker durability defaults than Postgres — you can lose up to 100ms of writes on a crash unless you specify j: true in the write concern, which makes the journal sync synchronous.
Indexes get updated.
Eviction or checkpointing eventually reconciles the in-memory page (base page + update skiplist) into a new on-disk page. WiredTiger writes the new page to a new location on disk — copy-on-write — and updates the parent pointer to point at the new location. This is similar to how ZFS or LMDB work.
So MongoDB's "fast insert" path: append to in-memory page's update skiplist, eventually journal it. Very fast, but with weaker default durability than Postgres.

Updates in MongoDB are simpler than in Postgres because of BSON and the copy-on-write design. When you update a field:

Find the document by _id (B-tree lookup).
Apply the change to the in-memory copy. If the document grew, it might need to be relocated — but WiredTiger handles this via the update skiplist abstraction, so the cost is amortized.
Journal the change.
The update skiplist now has the new version. Reads see it. Eventually reconciliation flushes the new state to a new page.
MongoDB does not keep MVCC versions the way Postgres does. WiredTiger has snapshot isolation, but it's achieved through its checkpoint/snapshot mechanism rather than per-tuple versioning. The skiplist of in-memory updates effectively gives you recent versions; older snapshots are reconstructed from checkpoints.

The interesting design choice MongoDB made: the document is the unit of atomicity. You can atomically update any number of fields within a single document with no transactional machinery, because the document is one B-tree value and the write is one atomic page operation. Multi-document transactions require the full transaction machinery and are slower — they're a relatively recent addition (4.0, 2018) and they're an order of magnitude slower than single-doc writes.

This is why MongoDB pushes you toward embedding-related-data-in-the-same-document. The data model is a forcing function for the storage engine's strengths.

The pattern across all three (Postgres, MongoDB, the LSM design):

Every fast OLTP system has the same fundamental structure:

A sequential append (WAL or journal) is the durability moment. Sub-ms because it's sequential I/O.
An in-memory mutable structure (buffer pool with dirty pages, or memtable with skiplist) absorbs writes and serves recent reads.
A background process (checkpointer, compactor, VACUUM, reconciliation) eventually moves data into the final on-disk format.
MVCC or snapshot isolation is implemented either by per-tuple versioning (Postgres), copy-on-write pages (WiredTiger), or immutable row groups with shadowing (LSM).
The variations are about where the mutability lives and how cleanup happens. Postgres puts mutability in pages and cleans with VACUUM. MongoDB/WiredTiger puts mutability in update skiplists and cleans with reconciliation. LSM puts mutability in the memtable and cleans with compaction.

For LL, we've effectively chosen the LSM variant. The reasons it's the right choice for us, restated now that you can see the alternatives:

Postgres-style mutable pages don't compose with the indexes we need. You can't update an HNSW graph in place on a mutable page efficiently — HNSW edges span the whole dataset, not a single page. Posting lists have the same issue.
WiredTiger-style update skiplists could work but they're a single-record optimization — they don't naturally extend to "this page also has 200 vectors that need to be SIMD-scanned." The skiplist would defeat columnar locality.
LSM-style memtable + immutable row groups lets the memtable be row-shaped (fast writes, fast point reads) and the row groups be columnar (fast scans, SIMD-friendly, compressible, with embedded indexes). The transition between them happens once, at flush time, and after that the data lives in its optimal form.
The discipline we should steal from each:

From Postgres: per-record version visibility via transaction IDs. Every record carries xmin/xmax (or equivalent). Reads filter by snapshot timestamp. This is the cleanest MVCC model and it composes well with LSM — old versions get GC'd at compaction time instead of VACUUM time.

From MongoDB: the document/record as the unit of atomic write. We should pick a "natural" atomicity boundary and make multi-record transactions explicit and slower. For us this is probably "a row plus all its inlined-at-write-time edges and indexed-at-write-time text/vector entries are one atomic unit," because they're all part of the same WAL record.

From WiredTiger: copy-on-write at the page level for the immutable row groups. We never modify an old row group in place. Compaction reads N old row groups and writes M new ones, then atomically swaps the catalog pointer. Recovery is trivial — if a crash happens mid-compaction, the old row groups are still intact and we just retry.

That's the design with the receipts. We're not inventing a new write path; we're picking the LSM tradition that's already proven in RocksDB, ClickHouse, Cassandra, and refining it for the specific shape of our data (columnar within row groups, four index types co-located, edges as first-class columns).

The question I want to ask back, now that you've seen the alternatives in detail: does the WAL design need to change for us? RocksDB has one log. Postgres has one WAL. But our writes have to land four different index structures plus the heap. Do we want one unified WAL with logical records ("insert this row into table T"), or do we want separate WALs per index type that get coordinated by transaction ID? The first is simpler and more like Postgres. The second is more parallelizable but introduces a distributed-commit problem within a single node. Which gut do you have on it?

