Good instinct, and you're right to be suspicious of the multi-WAL option — the WAL mismatch problem is genuinely hard and the solutions are all worse than just having one WAL. Let me walk through why multi-WAL is tempting, what the mismatch problem actually looks like, the techniques people have invented to solve it, and why one WAL wins anyway for us.

Why someone would even consider multiple WALs

The dream is parallelism. If your single WAL is a sequential append point, every transaction in the system serializes through that one file descriptor. On modern NVMe you can do maybe 500K–1M sequential appends per second per device, which sounds like a lot but starts to matter at scale. Worse, if your WAL fsync is on the critical path of every commit (it should be), then commit latency is bounded by fsync latency, and a single contended WAL means contended fsyncs.

So the seductive idea is: B-tree updates go to WAL-A, HNSW updates go to WAL-B, posting list updates go to WAL-C, edge log updates go to WAL-D. Each can be appended and fsynced in parallel by separate threads. You've theoretically 4x'd your write throughput.

This is essentially what some distributed systems do across nodes (each shard has its own log), and what some specialized systems do for separating control-plane from data-plane logs. There's real precedent.

What goes wrong: the WAL mismatch problem in detail

Imagine a transaction INSERT INTO documents (id, body, embedding, authored_by) VALUES (1, 'hello', [...], 42). This touches:

The heap (the row itself) → WAL-A
The HNSW index (new vector entry) → WAL-B
The posting list index (new tokens from 'hello') → WAL-C
The edge log (new authored_by edge to row 42) → WAL-D
Each WAL gets a record. Each gets fsynced independently. Now a crash happens. On recovery, we replay each WAL.

The problem: the four WALs may have crashed at different points. WAL-A might have durably recorded the row insert. WAL-B might have failed before fsync — the HNSW entry is lost. WAL-C might have the posting list entry. WAL-D might have the edge.

After recovery, you have a row in the heap with no vector index entry, partial text indexing, and a dangling edge. The database is structurally inconsistent. Queries will return wrong results. The vector search won't find the row that exists. A graph traversal will find an edge pointing to data the text index doesn't know about.

This is the violation of atomicity in its rawest form. ACID's A is broken. And not in the easy way (a transaction visibly fails) — in the hard way (some parts succeeded, some failed, and the database doesn't know which).

The techniques people have invented to solve this

There are essentially four serious approaches. Let me walk through each so you can see why none of them are free.

Approach 1: Two-Phase Commit across the logs

Treat each WAL as a "participant" in a distributed transaction. The protocol:

Coordinator writes a PREPARE record to a coordinator log.
Each participant WAL writes its records with a "tentative" marker, fsyncs, and acknowledges.
Coordinator writes a COMMIT record to its log and fsyncs.
Participants are notified to mark their records "committed" — but crucially, they don't need to fsync this; if a crash happens, recovery reads the coordinator log to determine which tentative records to honor.
Cost: you've added a coordinator log and at least two fsync rounds per transaction. You wanted to parallelize fsyncs and now you have more of them. The latency is strictly worse than a single WAL. The only win is throughput at high concurrency where the coordinator can batch.

This is what XA transactions and distributed two-phase commit do across nodes. Within a single node, it's almost never worth it. The complexity is enormous and the latency penalty is real.

Approach 2: Hierarchical logging with a "manifest" log

One small log (the manifest) records the transaction-level events: "transaction T123 begins, T123 commits at timestamp X." The big logs (per index) record the index-level changes with transaction IDs.

On recovery, replay the manifest first to learn which transactions committed. Then replay each index log, but only apply records whose transaction ID committed. Records from uncommitted transactions are skipped.

This works. It's roughly what some systems do (LMDB-like designs, some research databases). The challenges:

Each index log entry has to carry the transaction ID, which adds overhead per record.
Recovery is more complex: you have to read the manifest fully before you can interpret any index log.
The manifest log fsync is still on the critical commit path, so you haven't actually escaped the serialization point — you've just made it smaller. The big logs can be fsynced in parallel, but the commit can't return until the manifest has fsynced.
You get some parallelism benefit (the big logs can fsync concurrently with the manifest), but the commit latency floor is still one fsync.

Approach 3: Idempotent index rebuilding from a primary log

Designate one WAL as the "truth" — the heap WAL, typically. Index updates are written to their own logs but the heap WAL contains enough information to rebuild any index from scratch.

On recovery, replay the heap WAL fully. Then replay each index WAL up to the point where the heap WAL says they should be. If an index WAL is ahead of the heap, truncate it (those records are orphaned). If an index WAL is behind, rebuild the missing entries from the heap.

This is elegant in theory and brutally painful in practice. Rebuilding an HNSW index for the last 10 minutes of traffic might mean re-inserting 100K vectors with ef_construction=200 — that's 20M distance computations on the recovery path. Recovery time goes from seconds to hours.

Some systems do a variant of this for secondary indexes that are cheap to rebuild (B-trees, posting lists). For HNSW it's basically not viable.

Approach 4: Group commit with a single WAL, redesigned for throughput

This is the punchline and it's what I'd actually recommend. If your concern about a single WAL is throughput, the answer isn't multiple WALs — it's group commit.

Group commit means: when a transaction wants to commit, it writes its WAL record to an in-memory buffer and joins a "commit group." A dedicated WAL writer thread accumulates groups, does ONE fsync covering many transactions, then releases all of them. Each transaction sees its commit return as soon as the group's fsync completes.

Postgres does this. MySQL/InnoDB does this. RocksDB does this. The math:

Without group commit: 1 fsync per transaction, throughput = 1 / fsync_latency. On NVMe, ~10K txn/sec.
With group commit, batch size 100: 1 fsync per 100 transactions, throughput = 100 / fsync_latency. ~1M txn/sec.
The latency per transaction goes up slightly (you wait for the group), but throughput scales by 50–100x. This dominates the parallelism win from multiple WALs in basically every realistic scenario, and it has none of the atomicity problems.

Combine group commit with a well-designed in-memory WAL buffer (lock-free MPSC queue, for instance) and you can sustain millions of commits per second on a single WAL.

Why one WAL is correct for LL

Putting it all together:

Atomicity is non-negotiable for us. We have four index types that must stay consistent with the heap. A vector search returning a row whose body hasn't been text-indexed is a correctness bug, not a performance issue. Multi-WAL approaches all bolt some form of atomicity protocol back on top, at which point you've reinvented a single logical WAL with a more complicated implementation.
Recovery is dramatically simpler. One log, one cursor, one replay loop. We can prove correctness. With multiple WALs we'd be debugging recovery edge cases forever.
Throughput is solved by group commit, not parallelism. The single-WAL ceiling on modern NVMe is ~1M commits/sec with proper group commit design. We will not exceed that for years, if ever.
Logical logging fits our shape. The WAL record should be "insert this row" or "update this field" — a logical description of the change, not a physical description of which pages changed. The recovery code then deterministically applies the change to all four index structures. This is what Postgres calls "logical decoding," and it's the right level for us because our index structures are too varied for physical logging to make sense.
One concrete subtlety worth surfacing

The WAL has to be designed so that recovery can replay it correctly even if the in-memory state is partially built. Specifically:

The WAL record for an insert contains the row's data, the row ID assigned, and the timestamp.
Recovery reads the WAL, recreates the memtable, and re-derives the index entries from scratch.
For HNSW specifically: recovery doesn't need to re-run ef_construction insertions for vectors still in the memtable. They live in the memtable's mutable HNSW (a small one), not in the immutable row-group HNSW pages. Vectors in immutable row groups were already persisted as part of the row group flush, not the WAL.
This is the critical observation that makes single-WAL viable for vector data: the WAL only has to record the logical insert, not the HNSW graph state. The HNSW graph for committed-but-not-yet-flushed vectors lives in the memtable and is rebuilt from the WAL on recovery. The HNSW graph for flushed vectors lives in row groups and survives the crash directly because row groups are written atomically (write to new file, fsync, atomic rename, update catalog).

So the WAL stays small and logical. The expensive index structures live in the memtable (small, fast to rebuild) or in row groups (already durable, no recovery needed). The two together give us crash safety without any per-index logging.

The mental model to lock in

The WAL is a logical sequence of "what the user asked to happen." The memtable is all the derived structures for recent data. Row groups are all the derived structures for older data, made durable in one atomic flush. Recovery is "replay the WAL into a fresh memtable; row groups are already correct."

This is the clean design, and it falls out naturally from "one WAL, logical logging, group commit for throughput."

So your instinct is right and the alternative isn't worth the complexity. Multi-WAL solves a problem we don't have (throughput beyond what group commit provides) at the cost of a problem that's genuinely hard (atomicity across logs).

