This is a genuinely creative idea and I want to take it seriously — but I also want to be straight with you: it has a fundamental problem that I think you'll see clearly once I lay it out. Let me steelman it first, then show you where it breaks, then show you what good idea is actually hiding inside it.

What you're proposing, made precise

Four independent WALs, each appending records for its own index type. Each WAL record carries a monotonic commit ID (let's call it LSN — Log Sequence Number, the standard term). After every transaction, the WALs gossip: "I'm at LSN 12,847." The lowest LSN across all four is the "globally durable" point. Anything beyond that on any individual WAL is provisional and could be rolled back on crash.

This is, structurally, a Raft-like consensus pattern: a quorum agrees on a commit point and only data below that point is considered durable. It's elegant in theory. Now let me show why it doesn't work for our specific problem.

Why this breaks: Raft solves a different problem

Raft is designed for replicated state machines across machines. The reason Raft works is that all five (or however many) nodes in a Raft group are running the same state machine with the same log. They're literally redundant copies. Quorum means "a majority of copies agree" — and since they're copies, agreement is meaningful: if 3 out of 5 have the same value, that value is the truth.

Our situation is structurally different. The four WALs aren't copies of the same thing. They contain different, complementary information. The heap WAL has the row data. The HNSW WAL has graph edges. The posting list WAL has token positions. The edge log has typed references.

When the heap WAL is at LSN 12,847 and the HNSW WAL is at LSN 12,845, that doesn't mean "the HNSW WAL is behind and needs to catch up." It means two of the records the heap committed don't exist as vectors anywhere. The HNSW WAL can't catch up by itself — it doesn't have the vector data. That data only existed in the original INSERT statement, which is now lost because we crashed.

The synchronization gossip can tell you "we're inconsistent." It cannot tell you how to become consistent, because the information needed to rebuild the missing parts isn't redundantly stored anywhere.

So the LSN-comparison protocol gives you a diagnosis but no cure. You'd end up rolling back to the lowest common LSN across all four WALs — which means transactions that were durably written to three of the four WALs get thrown away because the fourth didn't make it. That's correct behavior (it preserves atomicity), but now you've made the problem worse than two-phase commit, because you've added complexity and you've made commit latency depend on the slowest of four fsyncs.

To put it sharply: the four WALs aren't peers. They're partial views of the same truth, and you can't reconstruct truth from partial views unless one of them has enough information to be authoritative.

The good idea hiding inside your proposal

There's something real here, though, and it's worth extracting. Your instinct is that the LSN should be the synchronization primitive. That's correct, and it's how every serious database actually works. The error is in thinking the LSN coordinates between WALs; the right framing is the LSN coordinates between the WAL and everything else.

Here's how production systems actually use LSNs as the sync mechanism:

One WAL, one LSN sequence. Every record gets a monotonically increasing LSN at write time. The LSN is assigned when the record enters the WAL, atomically, and represents a total order across all operations in the system.
Every durable structure tracks the LSN it has caught up to. The memtable knows "I contain all records up to LSN X." A row group on disk has a header that says "I was flushed at LSN Y." The HNSW index pages in a row group say "I include all vectors up to LSN Y." The B-tree index for column C says "I include all entries up to LSN Y."
Recovery is then trivial. On startup, look at every durable structure, find the minimum LSN across all of them (call it LSN_min). Replay the WAL from LSN_min forward. Each structure ignores records it already has (LSN ≤ its own checkpoint) and applies records it doesn't (LSN > its own checkpoint). When the WAL is fully replayed, all structures are synchronized to the WAL's tail.
The LSN is also the MVCC timestamp. A read at snapshot LSN T sees only records with LSN ≤ T. This is one mechanism for two distinct problems (recovery and snapshot isolation) and that's why it's so powerful.
This is essentially what Postgres does, what RocksDB does, what every well-designed system does. The LSN is the heartbeat of the entire database. Your instinct to use a synchronization point across structures was right — but the structures being synchronized aren't WALs, they're the WAL versus the derived state.

Where Raft-like thinking actually applies to LL

Raft becomes the right answer the moment you go distributed. When LL eventually has replicas — for read scaling, for high availability — then you have multiple copies of the same WAL across multiple nodes, and Raft (or Paxos, or Viewstamped Replication) is exactly the right protocol to agree on the log's order across nodes. The followers' WALs are copies of the leader's WAL, so quorum across them is meaningful.

So your Raft instinct isn't wrong, it's premature. Hold onto it. When we get to the distribution layer, it comes back as the right tool. Single-node, it's solving a problem that doesn't exist while creating one that does.

The deeper principle worth naming

Every time you find yourself wanting to coordinate multiple logs or multiple sources of truth on a single machine, the right move is to ask: "is there a single source of truth I could promote, and then make everything else a derived view?" Almost always the answer is yes, and the architecture gets dramatically simpler.

For us: the WAL is the source of truth. The memtable, the row groups, the four index types — all derived. The LSN is the synchronization primitive between the source of truth and its derivations. There is no "WAL mismatch" because there's only one WAL.

So your Raft idea pointed at the right primitive (LSN-based synchronization) but at the wrong scope (between WALs instead of between WAL and derived state). Promote one WAL to source-of-truth status, use LSNs as the universal sync token, save Raft for when we actually have peers to agree with.

