The constraint, stated honestly

A write hits the WAL, gets an LSN, and now needs to be in some in-memory structure such that immediately after commit, four different query patterns work:

Point lookup by ID — "give me row 47" — needs sub-microsecond latency. This is the OLTP path. Hash map or skiplist.
Vector similarity — "give me the 10 nearest vectors to query Q" — needs to return results that include just-inserted vectors. If a user inserts a doc and immediately queries, they expect to find it.
Text match — "give me rows whose body contains 'derivatives'" — needs to match against tokens in just-inserted documents.
Edge traversal — "give me all rows that row 47 has an authored_by edge to" — needs to follow edges written moments ago.
The constraint that makes this hard: all of this has to be cheap on the write path. Every microsecond we spend building in-memory indexes is a microsecond of write latency. So we can't just say "build a full HNSW in the memtable" — HNSW insertion is ~milliseconds per vector. That would destroy commit latency.

The cheating most systems do

Here's how the federation systems handle this and why it's wrong:

Postgres with pgvector: vector inserts go into a regular B-tree-indexed table. The HNSW index is separate and either (a) doesn't include the new row until a background process catches up, or (b) is rebuilt periodically. Recent vectors are invisible to similarity search until index catchup completes.
Elasticsearch: writes go to a per-shard "translog" (their WAL) and an in-memory buffer. The buffer is not searchable. Every ~1 second, the buffer is flushed to a new Lucene segment, which is searchable. So there's a 1-second visibility lag by default. This is why Elasticsearch is called "near-real-time" rather than real-time — there's a structural gap between commit and queryability.
Milvus, Weaviate, etc.: similar pattern. Writes land in a segment buffer that's not part of the search index. Visibility happens at flush boundaries.
MongoDB's text index: in-memory updates aren't reflected in text search until the index updater catches up.
This is the cheat. They have eventual queryability hiding behind "commit" because building full indexes on the write path is too expensive. Most users never notice because they don't insert-then-query within the visibility window. But it's a correctness gap, and it's the gap that bites you in agentic workflows where an LLM writes a doc and immediately retrieves it.

The principle I want to commit to

Read-your-writes consistency across all four access patterns. The instant a transaction commits, every query type sees its effects. No "near-real-time," no flush delays. This is a real differentiator and it's worth designing for explicitly.

That means the memtable has to be queryable across all four patterns, but the in-memory structures have to be cheaper than full disk-grade indexes — because they only need to handle the small fraction of data that's recent (between flushes, say up to 64MB or 100K rows).

The design: a memtable as a coordinated set of in-memory structures sharing one row store

Here's the architecture I'd propose, and then I want you to push on the specific choices.

The memtable's heart is a row store keyed by row ID. This is a skiplist (concurrent, lock-free) where the key is the row ID and the value is the full record — all columns, including the vector, text, and edge columns. This skiplist is the source of truth for memtable data. Point lookups by ID hit this directly. Range scans by ID are sorted-order traversal. MVCC visibility is checked at this layer.

Why skiplist and not hashmap: hashmaps don't support range scans, and we need them for table scans. Skiplists also have much better behavior under concurrent writes than B-trees in memory — there are well-known lock-free skiplist designs (Java's ConcurrentSkipListMap, RocksDB's inline skiplist) that scale to many writer threads.

Around this central row store, four lightweight in-memory indexes.

For point lookup by indexed columns (not just row ID — also unique keys, foreign keys), a secondary hash map per indexed column. New row arrives, hash maps get updated. Cheap.

For vector similarity in the memtable, the cheapest viable structure: a flat array of all vectors with their row IDs, plus a tiny per-memtable HNSW. The flat array is what's called a "brute-force" or "flat" index — for similarity search you just do SIMD dot products across all vectors. If the memtable holds 64MB of vectors at 6KB each, that's ~10K vectors. A SIMD dot product against 10K vectors with 1536 dimensions takes a few milliseconds — slower than HNSW but acceptable, and correct (100% recall). The tiny HNSW is optional and only kicks in if the memtable grows large; for typical memtable sizes, flat is fine.

This is the key insight for the vector case: the memtable can afford brute-force vector search because the memtable is small. The expensive HNSW index only needs to exist for the row groups (the bulk of the data). Recent vectors are searchable via flat scan; older vectors via HNSW. The query layer merges results.

For text matching in the memtable, a per-memtable in-memory inverted index. As rows are written, tokens are extracted from text columns and a hashmap-of-tokens-to-row-IDs is updated. This is small (memtable is small) and cheap to maintain. On query, this in-memory inverted index returns recent matches; the row groups' posting lists return older matches; results are merged.

For edge traversal in the memtable, two hash maps per edge type: forward (source row ID → target row IDs) and reverse (target row ID → source row IDs). When an edge is inserted, both get updated. Traversal queries hit these directly for recent edges. Older edges come from the edge log in row groups.

Why this works architecturally

The pattern is: for every disk-grade index structure, there's an in-memory analog optimized for the memtable's size. The disk version is optimized for billions of rows with logarithmic or sublinear lookups. The memory version is optimized for ~100K rows where simpler structures (flat arrays, hash maps, small skiplists) are actually faster than their fancier counterparts, because they fit in cache and have better constants.

This is exactly the same insight that makes LSM trees work in general — the memtable doesn't need to be a B-tree because at the memtable's size, a skiplist beats a B-tree on every metric (cache behavior, write contention, range scans). We're applying the same principle to all four index types.

What flushing looks like

When the memtable fills, it's frozen (a new memtable starts taking writes) and the frozen one becomes immutable. Now we can do expensive work: convert the row skiplist to columnar layout, build a real HNSW from the flat vector array, build a real posting list with FOR-compression from the inverted index, build CSR-compressed edge blocks from the edge hash maps. All of this happens in a background thread, no foreground latency impact.

The frozen memtable remains queryable using its in-memory structures until the flush completes and produces a row group. Then the catalog atomically swaps: queries now hit the row group instead of the frozen memtable, and the frozen memtable can be discarded.

This eliminates the visibility gap entirely. Data is queryable from the moment it commits to the moment it flushes (in-memory structures), and continues to be queryable after the flush (row group structures). No discontinuity.

The thing that's subtle and worth flagging

The in-memory HNSW (if we have one) is thrown away at flush time and a fresh HNSW is built in the row group. We don't try to "promote" the in-memory HNSW to disk. Why: HNSW construction quality depends on ef_construction and insertion order. A high-quality HNSW for 100K vectors built once with ef_construction=400 is better than an incrementally-built one. So we accept the rebuild cost for higher index quality. This is the same tradeoff Lucene makes — segments are built fresh from buffered docs, not by incrementally evolving a previous index.

The same logic applies to posting lists (rebuild with proper compression) and edge blocks (rebuild as CSR). The memtable's in-memory structures are write-optimized; the row group's structures are read-optimized. The flush is the conversion point.

The honest tradeoffs

Memory footprint per memtable is significant. The skiplist holds full rows. The vector array is 6KB × N vectors. The inverted index holds all tokens. The edge maps hold all edges. For 100K rows with 1536-dim embeddings, that's roughly 600MB just for vectors, plus everything else. Memtables are 0.5–1GB-ish. That's fine for a server, painful for an embedded use case.

Flat vector search in the memtable is slower than HNSW. If the memtable has 100K vectors and you're searching them with brute force, that's a few milliseconds vs. sub-millisecond HNSW. For most workloads this is fine (you're paying ~5ms for memtable vectors + ~1ms for row group HNSW = ~6ms total). For ultra-low-latency similarity search it's a constraint. We could add a small in-memory HNSW as the memtable approaches its size limit, but the complexity probably isn't worth it for v1.

Writes are not literally free. Each write updates the skiplist (one operation), maybe a hash map (one operation), maybe appends to the vector array (one append), maybe updates the inverted index (a few tokens), maybe updates edge maps (a few edges). On a modern CPU this is microseconds, but it's not zero. The write throughput ceiling is bounded by these in-memory updates plus the WAL fsync. With group commit, the WAL isn't the bottleneck; the in-memory updates are. I'd budget for ~500K–1M writes/sec per shard, which is plenty.

