Read amplification. Every query checks the active memtable, any frozen-but-not-yet-flushed memtables, and the row groups. The more memtables in flight, the more places a query has to look. Bigger memtables flush less often, which means fewer small row groups, which means less to merge. Smaller memtables flush more often and create more small row groups that need compaction.
Row group size at flush. When a memtable flushes, it becomes a row group of approximately the same size. Row group size matters for scan efficiency (bigger is better for analytical queries up to a point), compression ratio (bigger is better, more data to dictionary-encode), and index quality (HNSW built from 100K vectors is meaningfully better than HNSW built from 10K).
