Why we need our own format

There are excellent existing columnar formats: Parquet, ORC, Lance, Arrow IPC, DuckDB's native format. The honest question is whether we should extend one of them or build from scratch. I want to walk through this with you because the answer informs everything downstream.

Parquet is the de facto standard. It's columnar, compressed, has good ecosystem support, and is well-understood. But Parquet was designed for batch analytics — write once, read many. It has no concept of indexes embedded in the file. It can't store HNSW pages, posting lists, or graph adjacency. You can stuff binary blobs in a column (which is what some "Parquet + vector" systems do), but at that point the format is just a container; the database has to maintain external indexes that go stale relative to the Parquet files. That's the OriginChain v1 trap again.

Lance is the most relevant existing format — it's a Parquet-shaped format with native vector indexing. They store vectors as Arrow arrays and have a separate "manifest" that tracks vector indexes. It's much better than Parquet for our use case. But Lance was designed for ML training data lakes, not transactional databases. Its versioning is at the fragment level (file granularity), not the record level. It doesn't have built-in posting lists or graph adjacency. And critically, it doesn't have the LSN-tagged page metadata we need for our LSM recovery model.

Arrow IPC is in-memory format serialized to disk — no indexes, no compression beyond columnar, designed for fast deserialization not storage efficiency.

DuckDB's format is closed-by-design — it's tied to DuckDB's execution model and not stable across versions.

So: extending Lance is the closest existing path, but it would require so much modification (page format, MVCC, posting lists, graph adjacency, LSN tracking) that we'd be forking it anyway. Better to design our own from clean principles, taking inspiration from Parquet (footer-with-metadata layout), Lance (vector-native pages), RocksDB SSTables (LSN-tagged structure), and Lucene segments (embedded inverted indexes).

The format is genuinely the most consequential artifact we'll produce. Every byte of every row group goes through this format. It will outlive the rest of the codebase. Let's design it carefully.

What a "row group file" actually is

Let me clarify terminology first because I've been using "row group" loosely. In Parquet parlance, a row group is a horizontal partition within a file — a file contains multiple row groups. In our LSM design, I want to use the term differently: each row group is its own file on disk, produced by a single memtable flush. So "row group" and "file" are 1:1 for us. (This matches how RocksDB SSTables work — each SSTable is one file.)

So when a 256MB memtable flushes, it produces one file containing all the data and indexes for those 30K rows. That file is immutable from the moment it's written. Compaction reads N such files and produces M bigger files. The catalog tracks which files exist and which LSN range each covers.

The file's structural shape

I'd propose this top-level layout, and we can refine:

[Magic number + format version]
[File header: LSN range, row count, schema fingerprint, creation timestamp]
[Column chunks: one section per column, columnar data]
[Embedded indexes: one section per index type relevant to this file]
[Footer: offsets/sizes of everything above, statistics, bloom filters]
[Footer length + magic number]
The two magic numbers (one at start, one at end) bracket the file so we can validate it's not corrupted from either direction. The footer is at the end (like Parquet) because at write time you don't know the offsets until you've written everything — writing the footer last is the only way to do it in a single pass.

Reading the file: open it, seek to the end, read the footer length, read the footer, now you know where everything is, seek to whichever section you need. This means opening a file is one small seek, and reading a specific column or index is one additional seek to the known offset. This is the Parquet model and it's correct.

The column chunks section

For each column in the schema, this section contains:

A column header: type, encoding, compression algorithm, null count, min/max values, total bytes
The encoded column data: actual values, possibly dictionary-encoded, possibly compressed
Optionally a dictionary: if dictionary encoding is used
Per-page metadata: rows-per-page, page offsets within the chunk
Pages within a chunk are typically 8KB–1MB. Pages are the unit of decompression — to read row 47's column C, we read the page containing row 47 (using an offset index), decompress it, find the value. Pages are aligned and independently decompressible so we can do parallel decompression.

For our four "special" column types:

Vector columns: stored as a contiguous float array (no per-value compression by default — we want SIMD-friendly raw access). Optionally scalar-quantized (FP32 → FP16 or INT8) with a quality tradeoff. The column header records the quantization scheme and dimensions.

Text columns: stored as compressed strings (LZ4 or ZSTD per page), with optional dictionary encoding for low-cardinality text. The text content lives here; the inverted index over the text lives in the embedded indexes section.

Edge columns: stored as variable-length lists of (target_row_id, edge_property_offset) pairs. Most rows have few edges; we use a compressed sparse representation. The column tells you "for row 47, edges start at byte offset X." The actual graph traversal happens via the embedded edge index, but the inline copy is here for cheap lookup.

Scalar columns (int, float, string, bool, timestamp): standard columnar encoding — dictionary, RLE, delta, FOR — whichever fits the data best. We use ZSTD compression as the default at the page level.

The embedded indexes section

This is the part that makes this format not Parquet. We embed in the file itself:

A B-tree index over the primary key column — pages of (key, row_offset) pairs that let us do point lookups within this file. The B-tree is small because the file is bounded (at most ~100K rows); a fanout-128 B-tree has ~3 levels at this scale, so point lookup is 3 page reads.

HNSW pages for the vector column(s) — if a vector column has an HNSW index declared, we build the HNSW for just this file's vectors and store the graph here. The graph is laid out as: a header with entry point and max layer; layer-by-layer adjacency lists; the vectors are NOT duplicated (they're already in the vector column chunk — the HNSW just points to row offsets). This is critical: HNSW edges reference vectors by row offset, not by storing them inline. Saves a huge amount of space.

Posting lists for the text column(s) — if a text column has an inverted index declared, we build the inverted index for this file's documents. Layout: a term dictionary (FST — Finite State Transducer — for compact prefix-shared storage), per-term posting lists (delta-encoded row IDs with FOR compression), and optional positional data for phrase queries.

Edge index pages — for any edge column with an index, we build a CSR-style (Compressed Sparse Row) adjacency structure: an offset array (row_id → offset_into_edges_array) and the edges array itself. This complements the inline edges in the column chunk: the column chunk has edges-as-data, this section has edges-as-index for fast traversal queries.

The per-file index design has a critical property: each file is a fully self-contained queryable unit. To do a vector search, you can search each file's HNSW independently and merge results. To do a text query, you query each file's inverted index independently and merge. This is exactly how Lucene segments work, and it's what makes parallelism and compaction simple.

The footer

The footer is structured data (we'd encode it with FlatBuffers or our own format — definitely not JSON, we need fast decode) containing:

Schema definition (column names, types, indexes declared)
Per-column-chunk offsets and sizes
Per-index-section offsets and sizes
Per-page offsets (for fast skip-to-page)
Zone maps: min/max for each column per page (for predicate pushdown — skip pages whose range doesn't match the WHERE clause)
Bloom filters per indexed column (for fast "definitely not in this file" checks)
LSN range: [min_lsn, max_lsn] covering this file's records
Row count and tombstone count
Compression dictionary references
Checksums for each section
Critical detail: the footer includes page-level statistics, not just file-level. That's what lets the query layer skip pages without reading them. When the planner says "WHERE timestamp > X," the executor reads zone maps from the footer (small, cached) and skips pages where max(timestamp) < X. This is called predicate pushdown to the storage layer and it's a huge analytical performance win.

What's actually novel here vs. Parquet/Lance

Let me be honest about which parts are stolen and which are original:

Footer-with-metadata layout, magic numbers, page-based column chunks: stolen from Parquet. This is just how columnar formats are done now.
Vector columns as contiguous float arrays: similar to Lance.
Embedded HNSW per file: Lance has something similar; we're being more explicit about layout.
Embedded posting lists per file: stolen from Lucene segments, applied to columnar context.
Embedded edge index per file: not really done elsewhere — graph databases use external adjacency structures, not embedded ones.
LSN range in footer, MVCC visibility tags per row: stolen from RocksDB SSTables, but we're combining it with all the above.
The original contribution is the integration: a single file format where all four index types live alongside their data, all built atomically at flush time, all using the same page abstraction, all visible to the same query planner. Nobody has shipped this. Lance is the closest and they explicitly punt on text and graph.

The MVCC piece — how versioning lives in the file

Each row in the column chunks carries xmin (LSN at which this version was created) and xmax (LSN at which it was superseded, or ∞ if still live). These are stored as two extra columns (system columns). A reader at snapshot LSN T sees row R iff R.xmin ≤ T < R.xmax.

This means a single file can contain multiple versions of the same row — produced when a row is updated and the new version lands in a later memtable that flushes into a file alongside the old version. Compaction is what eventually removes old versions: when no live transaction can possibly see version V (T_min across all active transactions > V.xmax), compaction drops it.

The honest tradeoff worth naming

This format is more expensive to produce than a simple columnar file. Building the HNSW, posting lists, and edge indexes at flush time is real CPU work. For a 256MB memtable with 30K rows, flush might take 1–5 seconds of CPU work (mostly HNSW construction). That's fine because flush is a background operation — but it means compaction is expensive, because compaction reads N files and rebuilds the indexes for the merged output.

This is the same tradeoff Lucene makes and accepts. The alternative — not embedding indexes, maintaining them externally — is the federation trap. We're paying compaction CPU to keep the format self-contained, and it's the right trade.

The decisions hidden in this proposal that I want you to push on

Endianness, alignment, and structural encoding. I've been hand-wavy. Concretely: little-endian everywhere (matches x86/ARM, no swap cost), 8-byte alignment for vector data (SIMD needs this), FlatBuffers for footer (zero-copy read), raw bytes for column data (no parsing overhead). This is the kind of thing that's painful to change later — worth being explicit about up front.
Compression algorithm defaults. ZSTD at level 3 is the modern default — good ratio, fast decompress, fast compress. LZ4 is faster but worse compression. Snappy is older. I'd commit to ZSTD as default with LZ4 as the "low CPU" option.
Per-file index granularity. I've assumed indexes are per file. The alternative is per page (multiple HNSWs per file, one per page) or cross-file (HNSW spans multiple files). Per-file is the sweet spot: granular enough that small files have small indexes, coarse enough that you're not paying per-page overhead. Lucene segments work this way.
Vector quantization story. Do we quantize vectors at the file level (one quantization scheme per file) or per-column (declared in schema)? Per-column is more flexible — different vector columns can have different quantization. Per-file is simpler. I lean per-column.
Schema evolution. What happens when you add a column to a table? Old files don't have it. The reader has to know "column X is missing in this file, return null for it." This means each file embeds its own schema in the footer, and the reader projects between the file's schema and the table's current schema. This is what Iceberg and Delta Lake do; we should do the same.
