HNSW in 90 seconds, so we're on the same page

HNSW (Hierarchical Navigable Small World, Malkov & Yashunin 2018) is a multi-layer graph. Each vector is a node. Edges connect each node to its approximate nearest neighbors. The graph has layers: layer 0 contains all vectors, layer 1 contains a sample (~1/M of them), layer 2 a sample of that, and so on. Higher layers are sparse, used for coarse navigation. Lower layers are dense, used for fine-grained search.

Search starts at the entry point in the top layer, greedily walks toward the query (move to the neighbor closest to the query, repeat until no neighbor is closer), descends to the next layer, repeats, until you're at layer 0 where you do a more thorough beam search with parameter ef_search (the size of the candidate queue). Higher ef_search = more accurate, slower.

Two construction parameters matter: M (max neighbors per node, typically 16-48) and ef_construction (search width during insert, typically 100-400). At query time, ef_search (typically 50-200) trades latency for recall.

Key facts that drive layout decisions:

Each node has at most M outgoing edges per layer it appears in (2M at layer 0, by convention)
Most nodes appear only at layer 0 (probability of appearing at layer L is exp(-L/m_L) for some constant)
Search is dominated by distance computations, not graph traversal — typical search touches ~1000 candidates at ef_search=100
Search is random-access heavy — you jump around the graph following neighbor pointers; spatial locality is poor
That last point is the one that breaks in-memory implementations when you put them on disk. Random access to disk is 100,000x slower than random access to RAM. The layout has to fight this.

The disk constraint, named clearly

If we naively put HNSW on disk — vectors in one place, graph edges in another, traversal jumps around — then a single search touching 1000 candidates becomes 1000+ disk reads at 100μs each = 100ms search latency. That's catastrophic. SOTA vector search is 1-5ms.

Three techniques exist to handle this, and we'll combine all three:

Caching — keep the hot parts of the graph in RAM. Buffer pool. Standard.
Layout for locality — put data that's accessed together near each other on disk so one read fetches multiple useful things.
Compression — make vectors smaller so a single page fits more of them; SSD reads a page (4-16KB), not a vector.
DiskANN (Microsoft, 2019) is the canonical paper on disk-resident graph indexes. The key insight: store each node together with its neighbor list and its full-precision vector, all on one page (or contiguous bytes). One read gets you the vector for distance computation AND the neighbors to traverse. So search becomes O(candidates) disk reads instead of O(candidates × 2).

We're going to steal this aggressively. But we're not going to be DiskANN — we're keeping the HNSW algorithm (it's better for our scale, simpler to implement, and the hierarchy helps under updates). We're just borrowing DiskANN's page layout discipline.

The page layout decisions, in order

Decision 1: What's the unit of storage — node or page?

In-memory HNSW thinks in nodes. Disk HNSW has to think in pages, because the OS reads pages.

The right unit: a fixed-size page that contains multiple HNSW nodes plus their neighbor lists. Typically 4KB or 16KB. A page holds, say, 4-32 nodes depending on vector dimensionality.

Why fixed-size: O(1) page lookup from node ID. page_id = node_id / nodes_per_page. No B-tree to navigate. No fragmentation. Buffer pool can use a simple slab allocator.

Why multiple nodes per page: most HNSW traversals touch nodes that are similar (greedy search goes to nearby neighbors). If we cluster nearby nodes on the same page, traversal becomes more sequential. We'll come back to this clustering question.

Decision 2: What's actually inside a page?

Each node entry on a page contains:

- node_id: u32 (4 bytes) — the row offset, links to the row in the column chunks
- layer_count: u8 (1 byte) — how many layers this node appears in
- neighbor_count_per_layer: u8[layer_count] — how many neighbors at each layer
- neighbors_layer_0: u32[2M] — fixed-size, padded to 2M entries (unused = sentinel)
- neighbors_layer_1+: variable, follows
- vector: f32[D] or quantized — the full vector for distance computation
The vector lives on the page with the node. This is the critical DiskANN-style decision: one read brings the vector AND the neighbors. We pay storage cost (vectors are duplicated in the vector column chunk and in the HNSW pages — yes, this is intentional) for a 2x reduction in I/O during search.

Actually, let me push back on my own design here. We have a choice:

Option A: Duplicate the vector in the HNSW page. Vectors live twice — once in the vector column chunk (for full scans, for serving the actual vector to clients), once in the HNSW page (for fast traversal). ~2x vector storage.

Option B: Store only a pointer (the row offset) in the HNSW page; do a second read to fetch the vector from the column chunk. 1x vector storage, 2x disk reads during traversal.

DiskANN chose A. Lucene/HNSW chose A. Pinecone chose A (I believe). The storage doubling is the cost of fast search. We should choose A.

But — there's a refinement. The HNSW page only needs enough of the vector to do distance computation during search. If we quantize the vector aggressively in the HNSW page (say PQ — Product Quantization, or scalar quantization to int8), we can fit many more nodes per page. The full-precision vector lives in the column chunk; the HNSW page has a compressed version used for fast candidate selection.

The standard pattern (used by DiskANN's "rerank" mode): use the quantized vector in the HNSW page for the greedy search and ef_search filtering. Then for the top-K candidates returned, fetch the full-precision vectors from the column chunk and rerank with exact distance. This gives you HNSW speed with full-precision accuracy.

This is the pattern I'd commit to. It means:

HNSW pages contain quantized vectors (say int8, 4x smaller than f32)
Column chunks contain full-precision vectors
Search algorithm: traverse HNSW with quantized distances → get top-K candidates → fetch full vectors → rerank → return top-K
Storage cost: 1.25x (full precision in column chunk + quantized copy in HNSW page). Search quality: nearly identical to full-precision HNSW. Search speed: meaningfully faster because pages are smaller and more nodes fit per cache line.

Decision 3: Quantization scheme for HNSW pages

The options:

Scalar quantization (int8): each f32 → int8 via min-max scaling. 4x compression. Very fast. ~1-2% recall loss at sensible parameters. Implementation: trivial.
Product Quantization (PQ): split vector into M subspaces, quantize each via codebook lookup. Compression: configurable, 8-32x typical. Recall loss: 2-5%. Implementation: requires codebook training during index build, more code.
Binary quantization: f32 → bit (sign of value). 32x compression. Massive recall loss alone, but useful for coarse filtering with rerank.
I'd go with scalar quantization (int8) as the default, with PQ as an option for vectors that warrant it (high dimensionality, lots of vectors, willing to pay codebook complexity for storage savings).

The rationale: scalar quantization is essentially free to implement and gives 4x storage reduction. PQ is more code, more complexity, and the marginal compression vs scalar quantization isn't always worth it unless you're at billion-vector scale. We can add PQ later as an option; we shouldn't ship without it eventually, but it doesn't need to be in v1.

Decision 4: How are layers laid out?

HNSW has multiple layers. Layer 0 has all nodes; higher layers have decreasing samples. Two layout strategies:

Strategy A: Layer-major. All of layer 0 in one section, all of layer 1 in another, etc. Good for: starting search at the top (jump to top-layer section, do upper-layer traversal there). Bad for: descent across layers requires jumping between sections.

Strategy B: Node-major. Each node's data for ALL layers lives together. Good for: a node's full data (vector + all layer neighbor lists) fits in one read. Bad for: top-layer-only operations have to skip a lot.

DiskANN doesn't have layers (it's flat). Lucene's HNSW uses layer-major. The HNSW paper doesn't take a position.

I'd go with node-major, with one caveat. Reasoning: most search time is spent at layer 0 (descent through upper layers is fast — they're tiny). The dominant access pattern is "read a node's layer-0 neighbors and its vector." Node-major puts these together. The upper layers are small enough (layer 1 is ~1/M of layer 0, so ~6% with M=16) that having them inline doesn't bloat layer-0 pages much.

The caveat: the entry point (a single specific node, top layer) should be in the footer for instant access. We don't want to seek into the HNSW section to find the entry point. So the footer carries: entry_point_node_id, max_layer, num_layers. Search starts by reading the entry point's page, which gives us its upper-layer neighbors, and we descend from there.

Decision 5: Node clustering — does node_id correspond to spatial position?

Nodes are assigned IDs when inserted into the memtable. Without intervention, ID is roughly insertion order — semantically arbitrary. Putting node 0, 1, 2 on the same page means random vectors end up adjacent. Traversal hits random pages.

Smart layout: at flush time, when we're building the HNSW for this file, reorder the nodes such that nearby-in-graph nodes get nearby IDs. This is a graph reordering problem. Standard techniques: BFS ordering from a centroid, Reverse Cuthill-McKee, or domain-specific schemes like the GraphIndex paper's approach.

Concrete recommendation: at flush time, do a BFS from the highest-degree node, assign sequential IDs in BFS order. This is cheap (linear in graph size) and improves locality meaningfully. Nodes that are graph-neighbors get page-neighbors. A search traversal that walks to a node's neighbor likely finds that neighbor on the same page or an adjacent page already in cache.

This adds CPU cost at flush time (one full BFS over the HNSW graph). For a 30K-vector flush that's milliseconds. Worth it.

A subtlety: the node IDs in the HNSW are file-local. They're not the global row IDs. The HNSW page header has a translation table: local_id → row_offset_in_column_chunk. This lets us reorder for graph locality without disturbing the row order in column chunks (which has its own constraints — primary key order, for example). Search returns local IDs, we translate to row offsets for the rerank step.

Decision 6: Deletion handling

In an immutable file, you can't delete. So how do we handle deleted vectors?

The MVCC story applies: a row with xmax < ∞ is logically deleted. During search, we filter candidates by visibility — if a candidate's xmax is less than our snapshot LSN, we discard it.

But HNSW search itself doesn't know about MVCC. It just traverses the graph and returns candidates. The filtering happens at the rerank step (when we fetch full vectors, we also fetch xmin/xmax and filter).

The cost: graph traversal still visits dead nodes. If 50% of nodes in a file are dead (because lots of updates), search is doing 2x the work for nothing. Compaction is what fixes this — compaction rebuilds the HNSW without the dead nodes, restoring search speed.

This is the same pattern as Lucene segment merging. It's correct. The performance is fine as long as compaction is keeping up.

Decision 7: Concurrent reads during compaction

This isn't strictly a page layout decision but it affects layout. If compaction is reading file F1+F2 to produce F3, and queries are simultaneously reading F1+F2, the buffer pool handles this — pages are cached. F3 is being written as a new file; once written, atomic catalog swap. Queries that started before the swap see F1+F2; queries after see F3. F1+F2 are dropped when no live transaction needs them.

The layout makes this trivial because files are immutable and named uniquely. Compaction never touches an existing file.

