Good. Posting lists. This is the one section where there's a clear external authority — Lucene — and the right move is to understand exactly what they do, why they do it, and decide where we deviate. Twenty-five years of production search at Google, Elastic, and everyone else has shaped this format, and reinventing it would be hubris. But we have one constraint they don't have, and one opportunity they don't take, and those are the interesting parts.

The problem, stated plainly

A text column has rows containing documents. We want queries like "find rows where the body contains 'derivatives' and 'volatility'." The naive approach is to scan every row and check — O(N) per query, hopeless at scale.

The inverted index inverts the relationship. Instead of "row → tokens," we store "token → list of rows." For each unique term in the corpus, a posting list: the row IDs that contain that term. A query for "derivatives" is then: look up the term, get the posting list, return those rows. A query for "derivatives AND volatility" is: intersect the two posting lists.

The interesting questions are: how do we store the term-to-posting-list mapping (the term dictionary), how do we encode posting lists efficiently (they can be huge), and what extra information do we carry per posting (positions for phrase queries, term frequencies for relevance ranking).

The term dictionary: FST and why

The term dictionary maps terms (strings) to posting list locations. Naive options:

Hash table: O(1) lookup, no prefix scans, no range queries, bad cache behavior at large sizes
Sorted array: O(log N) lookup, good cache behavior, no prefix sharing (every term stored in full)
B-tree: O(log N) with better cache behavior, still no prefix sharing
Trie: prefix sharing (great compression), bad cache behavior in naive implementations
The winning data structure, used by Lucene since 2012, is FST (Finite State Transducer). It's a trie compressed into a directed acyclic graph — terms with common prefixes share states, terms with common suffixes also share states. The output of traversing the FST is the offset into the posting list section.

Why FST is dominant:

Prefix and suffix sharing gives 5-10x compression vs storing terms in full. For a corpus with 10M unique terms averaging 12 chars, that's the difference between 120MB and 15MB of term dictionary.
Cache-friendly traversal. The FST is laid out as a contiguous byte array; following a transition is pointer arithmetic.
Range and prefix queries fall out for free. "All terms starting with 'crypt'" is one traversal.
Memory-mappable. You don't deserialize the FST; you mmap the bytes and traverse in place.
The implementation we'd use is essentially Lucene's. The fst Rust crate (BurntSushi's) implements this and is well-tested — we can use it directly. It's a Sorted-State Transducer in his terminology, and it's the right tool.

Decision lock: FST for the term dictionary, using the existing Rust fst crate as the implementation. This is one of those places where I genuinely don't think we should innovate. Lucene got it right; the Rust ecosystem has a battle-tested implementation; let's use it.

Posting list encoding: this is where it gets interesting

A posting list for a common term might contain millions of row IDs. For a rare term, just a few. The encoding has to handle both efficiently.

Three things go into a posting list:

Document IDs (row IDs in our case): the rows containing this term
Term frequencies: how many times the term appears in each row (used for BM25 scoring)
Positions: where in the document each occurrence is (used for phrase queries)
Modern systems split these into separate streams so queries that don't need positions don't pay for them. We should too.

Encoding the doc IDs

Doc IDs are stored in sorted order (this enables intersection by merging sorted lists, which is the core of multi-term queries). Sorted IDs allow delta encoding: instead of storing [100, 105, 120, 145], store [100, 5, 15, 25] — the deltas. Deltas are smaller on average and compress much better.

The standard delta encoding in modern posting lists is PFOR-Delta (Patched Frame of Reference, Delta-encoded), or its evolution FastPFOR. The idea: for each block of (typically) 128 doc IDs, find the number of bits B needed to represent most of the deltas; pack each delta into B bits; for outliers (deltas that don't fit in B bits), store them separately in a "patch" array.

Lucene 9+ uses this. Concretely, blocks of 128 doc IDs, find the bit width that fits 95%+ of deltas, pack with bit-packing, patches at the end. Decompression is SIMD-friendly because all values in a block are the same bit width.

Alternative: Roaring Bitmaps. Roaring is brilliant for dense doc ID sets (terms that appear in most documents). It uses different encodings depending on density: bitmap for dense, sorted array for sparse, run-length for runs. For typical search workloads, posting lists are not dense enough for Roaring to win — but for some boolean filter columns it absolutely is.

My recommendation: PFOR-Delta as default, with the framework to support Roaring for boolean filter columns later (e.g., "is_active = true" stored as a bitmap is way better than a posting list). We commit to PFOR-Delta for the text posting list; we leave room in the format for alternative encodings per column.

Encoding term frequencies

Term frequencies are small integers (most terms appear 1-3 times in a document). They're parallel to the doc IDs — one frequency per doc.

Standard approach: store frequencies in a parallel stream, encoded with the same bit-packing as doc IDs but on the raw values (not deltas — frequencies aren't sorted). Most frequencies are 1, so the bit width is tiny.

A useful optimization: skip frequencies when they're all 1. If a posting list has no doc with frequency > 1, store a flag and skip the frequency stream entirely. This is common for rare terms and saves storage.

Encoding positions

Positions are the expensive part. They're needed only for phrase queries ("derivatives volatility" with these words adjacent). Most queries don't need them. Many search systems make position storage optional per field.

When stored, positions are: for each doc, a list of positions where the term occurs. Position lists are typically small (a few positions per doc per term) but accumulate across many docs.

Encoding: positions within a doc are sorted, so delta-encoded. Then bit-packed. Standard.

Decision lock: PFOR-Delta for doc IDs and positions, bit-packed frequencies, positions optional per text column declared in schema. Same blocks of 128 values; same bit-packing technique throughout.

Skip lists for fast intersection

When you query "derivatives AND volatility," you need to intersect two posting lists. If both have 10M entries, naive intersection is 10M comparisons. Too slow.

The trick: skip lists. Within each posting list, store skip pointers every K entries (typically every 128). The skip pointer says "skip ahead to this offset in the posting list; the doc ID there is at least X." During intersection, when one list is far behind the other, you skip forward using the pointers instead of decoding every block.

Lucene stores skip data in a parallel stream. The skip stream is small (one entry per 128 docs) and lets you logarithmically skip through a posting list.

This is essential for production search. Without it, multi-term queries on large indexes are painfully slow.

Decision lock: Skip lists with skip points every 128 entries, stored as a parallel stream alongside the posting list.

Now the part where we deviate from Lucene

Lucene was designed before SIMD was ubiquitous in software, before SSDs, before we cared about embedding similarity ranking. We can make two improvements that aren't necessarily novel but aren't standard in the Lucene format.

Improvement 1: SIMD-aware block layout

We make every block exactly 128 elements, padded if necessary, and align block boundaries to cache lines. This lets us use SIMD bit-unpacking — instead of decoding 128 values one at a time, we use AVX2/AVX-512 instructions to decode 8-16 values per cycle.

The streamvbyte and fastpfor literature (Lemire et al.) has done this work. The Rust ecosystem has pco (Pcodec) and various bit-packing crates that do SIMD decode. We use them.

Cost: ~no additional development cost (the libraries exist), some constraint on block layout (must align), small storage overhead from padding (~1%). Benefit: 2-4x faster posting list decoding, which matters because text queries are often decode-bound.

Improvement 2: Score-quantized posting lists (the AI-native bit)

Here's where we differ from Lucene in a way that's genuinely useful for AI workloads.

Standard inverted index: posting lists are sorted by doc ID. To get the top-K results by BM25 score, you have to scan the entire posting list (or large portions of it) and rank.

Modern search engines use MaxScore and WAND algorithms to skip portions of posting lists that can't contribute to top-K. These work but are complex.

The AI-native move: at index-build time, score-quantize the posting list. For each term, pre-compute a coarse BM25-contribution-score for each doc and store posting lists sorted by score descending in addition to (or instead of) sorted by doc ID. Now top-K queries can short-circuit after reading the top few hundred entries.

This is what some specialized text-search systems do (early termination indexes). It's not Lucene's default because Lucene needs to support arbitrary boolean queries efficiently, which require doc-ID-sorted lists for merge-based intersection.

The right answer for us: store posting lists doc-ID-sorted (for boolean intersection), but also store a separate "top doc IDs by score" auxiliary array per term (just the top 1000 entries, by impact score). For top-K relevance queries, we hit the auxiliary array first; for boolean queries, we hit the main list. The auxiliary array is small (1000 × 4 bytes = 4KB per term) and only worth storing for terms with substantial posting lists (say, >10K entries).

This is a mild deviation but it gives us much faster top-K ranked queries, which is the dominant query pattern in AI/RAG workloads. People searching "find me documents about derivatives" mostly want top-K-by-relevance, not boolean precision.

Decision lock: SIMD-aware 128-element blocks, score-augmented auxiliary arrays for terms with large posting lists. Main lists remain doc-ID-sorted for boolean correctness.

The constraint Lucene doesn't have

Lucene segments are segment-local. A Lucene segment has its own term dictionary, its own posting lists. Multi-segment queries hit each segment and merge results. This is exactly our per-file model — and it's the right design for an LSM. So we don't deviate here.

But we have one extra constraint Lucene doesn't have: MVCC. Every posting in our index points to a row ID, and that row has xmin/xmax. The text index can return doc IDs whose rows are no longer visible to the current transaction.

Two approaches:

Approach A: Filter at query time. Posting list returns candidates → fetch xmin/xmax from row columns → filter. This is the same pattern as HNSW. Simple, correct, but text queries can return many candidates that get filtered out.

Approach B: Bake visibility into the index. Store xmin and xmax alongside each posting. Filter during posting list decode. More storage (8 bytes per posting) but no rejected candidates.

I'd go with Approach A. Storage savings matter; the filter step is cheap; we'd duplicate what's in the row columns. The HNSW pattern works for text too.

The opportunity I'm not taking, and why

You might expect that since we're an AI-native database, we'd integrate vector similarity directly into the inverted index — e.g., expand query terms via embedding similarity before searching. This is "neural sparse retrieval" — systems like SPLADE produce sparse embeddings that look like learned posting lists.

This is interesting research but I don't think it belongs inside the storage layer. It's a query-time concern: at query time, expand the query using an embedding model, then run a standard text query. The storage format doesn't need to know about it.

If we want to ship a SPLADE-style index later, we can — the column would just be a sparse vector column with its own index (which we haven't designed yet, but it's a natural extension of the text posting list with float weights instead of frequencies).

The full text section layout

Text Section (per text column):
  Section Header:
    - magic, version
    - num_terms, num_docs_with_field
    - has_positions: bool
    - has_score_augmented_lists: bool
    - field_stats: avg_field_length, etc. (for BM25)

  Term Dictionary:
    - FST (BurntSushi crate format)
    - Output values are offsets into the posting list region

  Posting Lists Region:
    For each term:
      - Header: doc_freq, has_positions, has_score_aux
      - DocID stream: PFOR-Delta blocks of 128
      - Frequency stream: bit-packed (or omitted if all 1)
      - Position stream: PFOR-Delta blocks (if has_positions)
      - Skip stream: skip points every 128
      - Score-augmented list: top-1000 doc IDs by impact score (optional)

  Field Statistics (for BM25 scoring):
    - Per-doc field length (compressed array indexed by row ID)
    - Used by BM25 to penalize long docs

The footer points to:
  - Text section offset and length per text column
  - FST root offset (for traversal start)
Storage budget check

For 30K docs averaging 200 unique terms each, with ~100K unique terms in the corpus:

FST: ~2-5MB (depends on term overlap, prefix sharing)
Posting lists (no positions): ~10-20MB (rough estimate, deltas + bit-packing get great compression on small int IDs)
Posting lists (with positions): ~30-60MB
Field statistics: 30K × ~2 bytes = 60KB
Score-augmented lists: assume 1000 terms qualify × 4KB = 4MB
Total per file: 20-80MB of text indexing on top of the text column data itself. For a 256MB memtable that's heavy but acceptable — text indexing is the most expensive index type per byte of source data. Worth knowing.

The query-time picture

A text query against a file: "body MATCH 'derivatives volatility'"

Read footer → get text section offset, FST root
Traverse FST for "derivatives" → posting list offset → decode → list of (doc, freq, positions)
Traverse FST for "volatility" → same
Merge-intersect the two posting lists, using skip pointers when one is far behind
For each candidate, compute BM25 score using term frequencies + field length
(If top-K query) check score-augmented lists first; if they cover the K we need, skip step 4-5
Return top-K by score
Apply MVCC visibility (fetch xmin/xmax for top candidates, filter)
Page reads: footer (cached), FST (cached after first access, ~1-2 pages per term), posting lists (proportional to result size with skip lists making it efficient), field stats (cached). Typical query: ~5-20 page reads, mostly cached on warm system.

The honest tradeoffs

Storage cost: text indexing roughly doubles storage of the text column data itself. Standard cost; everyone pays it.

Build time: FST construction is fast (~milliseconds for 100K terms). Posting list construction is bounded by tokenization speed — tokenizing 30K documents takes a couple seconds. Total flush overhead from text indexing: maybe 3-10 seconds for the text section.

Phrase query cost: position storage adds significant data. If you don't need phrase queries, you opt out per column and skip positions entirely.

Score-augmented list maintenance: these are static at flush time. If documents get deleted via MVCC, score-augmented lists may contain dead docs that get filtered at query time. Compaction rebuilds them.

