The only scenarios where we'd genuinely outgrow it: we want per-term precomputed BM25 statistics in the dictionary itself (max impact score, IDF, etc.), or we want term-level skip data inline. Both can live in a small parallel array indexed by the FST's output position rather than encoded in the u64. So the FST crate's interface is fine; "outgrowing" was me being paranoid about a non-problem. We use the crate, we encode what we need in u64, we keep auxiliary per-term metadata in a parallel structure if we want it. No forking, no rewrite.

On "reasonable people disagree" about score-augmented lists — what I meant is that this is a genuine architectural fork in the IR world, not a clear win.
The IR-orthodox approach is WAND (and its descendants BlockMaxWAND, MaxScore). These are query-time algorithms: you precompute per-block maximum impact scores during indexing, and at query time you use these maxes to skip blocks of the posting list that provably can't contribute to top-K. Lucene uses BlockMaxWAND. Elasticsearch uses it. It's the academic consensus.
Score-augmented auxiliary lists are simpler: just store the top-1000 doc IDs by impact score per term, ranked. Top-K queries hit the auxiliary list first; if we get K confident answers there, we never touch the main posting list.
The disagreement looks like this:
The WAND camp says: don't duplicate data; the algorithm is well-understood; it composes properly with complex boolean queries (A AND B AND NOT C with relevance ranking); the implementation cost is one-time. Auxiliary lists are a hack that wastes storage and only works for simple top-K.
The auxiliary-list camp says: WAND is 500-1000 lines of careful code with subtle bugs; auxiliary lists are 50 lines and obviously correct; for RAG/AI workloads, 95% of text queries are single-term or simple-AND with top-K — exactly the case auxiliary lists handle perfectly; the 4KB-per-qualifying-term overhead is trivial; you can add WAND later when someone needs it.
My honest take: for what we're building, auxiliary lists are the right call for v1. AI workloads don't do complex boolean queries against text the way enterprise search does. We can implement WAND in v2 if a customer pulls us toward it. The risk of starting with WAND is that we'll spend two months on it before shipping; the risk of starting with auxiliary lists is that we'll spend two weeks ripping them out later. I'd take that trade.
But it's a fork where I want you to know there's a real alternative, not a single right answer.

On positions opt-in per column — let me make this concrete.
A position is "where in the document does this term appear" — token index 0, 1, 2, etc. You need positions for phrase queries like body MATCH PHRASE 'machine learning', where the two terms have to be adjacent. Without positions, the same query degrades to body MATCH 'machine' AND body MATCH 'learning' — finds docs containing both words anywhere, not as a phrase.
Position storage is expensive. For a typical text column, positions roughly double the posting list size (sometimes triple). Most columns don't need phrase queries — tags, categories, short identifiers, names. Some columns do — body text, comments, descriptions.
So "opt-in per column" means schema declares which text columns have positions indexed:
body         TEXT INDEX (positions = true)   -- supports phrase queries
title        TEXT INDEX (positions = true)   -- supports phrase queries
tags         TEXT INDEX (positions = false)  -- term match only, no phrases
description  TEXT INDEX (positions = false)
Three complications this creates:
First, the schema gets one more knob. Not a huge deal but it's a knob users have to understand. We need good defaults — I'd default to positions = true and let users opt out when they know they don't need them. Storage-conscious users will opt out for short-text columns.
Second, query validation. If someone runs a phrase query against a positionless column, what happens? Three options: silently fall back to AND (wrong results, bad), silently return no results (also wrong), or fail loudly with an error. We pick the third — phrase query against positionless column raises a clear error at planning time.
Third, schema evolution. If you start without positions and later want them, you either rebuild old files (expensive — full reindex of text column) or accept partial coverage (positions for new files, no positions for old, phrase queries work only against recent data). I'd surface this honestly to the user: "this rebuild will take N hours" and let them choose. Compaction can be opportunistic — when it rewrites an old file, it adds positions if the schema now requires them.
The alternative is "always store positions" which is a 2-3x waste on columns that don't need them, or "never store positions" which means no phrase queries ever (acceptable for some workloads but a real limitation). Opt-in is the right answer; it just costs a bit of schema surface area.

