Why this section is the novel one
Every other section has prior art. HNSW pages have DiskANN. Posting lists have Lucene. Even the row group format has Parquet and Lance to learn from. Graph storage in an LSM/columnar context has essentially no production precedent. Neo4j is row-store with mutable pages. JanusGraph and TigerGraph are distributed but built on top of key-value engines that don't share our architecture. The Postgres extension age shoehorns graph onto rows. None of them have the constraints we have — immutable files, embedded indexes, MVCC via xmin/xmax, integration with columnar scans.
So we're doing original work here, and the trap is that original work is where we're most likely to overdesign. I'll try to keep this grounded by deriving each decision from a concrete query pattern rather than aesthetics.
The core data structure: CSR
Compressed Sparse Row is the standard graph representation in numerical computing and it's exactly what we want for an immutable, read-optimized file. Two arrays:
offsets[] — indexed by source node, contains the starting position in edges[] for that source's neighbor list. Length = num_sources + 1 (the +1 is a sentinel so offsets[i+1] - offsets[i] gives the count for node i).
edges[] — flat list of target row IDs, sorted by source then by target within each source.
To find all out-edges of source S: read offsets[S] and offsets[S+1], slice edges[start..end]. That's it. Two array reads and a slice. Cache-friendly because each source's neighbors are contiguous in memory. SIMD-decodeable because the slice is a packed array of target IDs.
This is read-optimal. It's also mutation-impossible — you can't insert a new edge into the middle of edges[] without shifting everything. Which is fine, because files are immutable. New edges land in new files; compaction rebuilds the CSR.
The reason CSR is correct here and not in Neo4j: Neo4j has to support edge inserts on existing nodes. We don't — at the file level. We support edge inserts at the system level by writing to memtables that eventually become new files. The CSR is the post-flush representation, and at that point it's static.
The cross-file edge problem, named honestly
Here's the part that has no clean precedent. Edges are pairs (source_row_id, target_row_id). Both source and target are global identifiers — each resolves to (file_id, local_offset). The source side is easy: when we build the CSR for file F, we include only edges whose source is in F. The CSR's offset array is indexed by F's local source IDs.
But the target can be anywhere. An edge written today from a source in file F_today might point to a target that was inserted three months ago and lives in file F_old. F_old is immutable and was written before this edge existed. It cannot contain this edge.
This breaks the symmetry between forward and reverse traversal. Forward — "what does S point to?" — is one file lookup: go to S's file, read its CSR. Reverse — "what points to T?" — could require scanning every file in the system, because the source of an incoming edge could live anywhere.
This is the fundamental problem of edge storage in an LSM. Let me lay out the four approaches and pick one.
Approach 1: Forward-only indexing. Each file stores only outgoing edges of in-file sources. Reverse traversal scans all files. Simple, half the storage, but reverse traversal scales with the number of files in the LSM. Unacceptable for any workload that does real reverse traversal — and "find all users following @prajwal" is a reverse traversal.
Approach 2: Forward + per-file reverse indexes. Each file stores both forward CSR (sources in this file, their outgoing edges) and a reverse index (for each target row ID referenced by edges in this file, which in-file sources point to it). The reverse index is keyed by target row ID — but target row IDs are global and sparse from any single file's perspective, so this isn't a dense CSR; it's a sorted map or hash. Full reverse traversal of T scans all files, but each per-file lookup is O(log N_edges_in_file) or O(1). Storage cost: ~2x edge data.
Approach 3: Global reverse index outside files. Maintain a system-level reverse index (target → sources) updated as edges are inserted. This violates the "everything in the file" principle but gives O(1) reverse traversal. The reverse index is mutable, lives outside the LSM, becomes a separate consistency problem to manage.
Approach 4: Compaction-built reverse indexes. Forward only at flush time. Compaction periodically builds reverse indexes over merged file sets. Smaller files have no reverse index; bigger compacted files do. Reverse traversal hits the big files efficiently and scans the few small recent files.
The right call is Approach 2, with bloom filter accelerators. Here's why.
Approach 3 is tempting but it's a different consistency model — a mutable structure that must be updated atomically with the immutable files. We'd be reintroducing the federation problem we're trying to eliminate. Reject.
Approach 4 is clever but it makes reverse traversal performance depend on compaction state, which is opaque to users. "Why is this query suddenly slow? Because compaction hasn't caught up yet." Bad UX. Reject.
Approach 2 keeps everything in the file, has predictable performance, and accepts a 2x edge storage cost. The cost is real but bounded — edges are typically a small fraction of total storage (vectors and text dominate), so 2x of a small thing is still small.
The bloom filter accelerator: each file has a bloom filter over target row IDs (or row ID ranges). Reverse traversal of T queries every file's bloom filter first; only files where the bloom filter says "maybe" actually get their reverse index consulted. For a system with 1000 files where T appears in 5, we do 1000 cheap bloom checks and 5 real lookups. The bloom filter is ~1KB per file; trivial.
Forward and reverse as separate structures within the section
Now within a file, forward and reverse have different shapes.
Forward index: dense CSR. Offset array indexed by file-local source ID (a small integer, 0 to num_sources_in_file). The translation from local source ID to global row ID lives in a parallel array. We have N_sources entries in the offset array; we have total_edges_count entries in the edges array.
Reverse index: sparse map. Target row IDs are global and discontiguous — no point in a dense offset array. Use a sorted array of (target_row_id, list_of_source_local_ids) pairs, binary searchable. Or an FST over target row IDs (interesting reuse of the FST machinery — FST outputs are u64 offsets into a parallel "sources" array). Honestly an FST is overkill here; a sorted array with a small B-tree-style fanout index for fast binary search is fine. Let's commit to that.
The reverse index entries point to local source IDs, then we resolve to global row IDs through the same translation table as the forward index. This keeps the reverse index tight.
Edge properties
Edges aren't just pairs of row IDs. They often carry properties: a follows edge might have since: timestamp. A weighted_recommendation might have weight: f32. A mentions edge might have position_in_text: u32.
Two patterns from the literature:
Inline properties: each edge in the CSR is a fat tuple (target_row_id, prop1, prop2, ...). Pro: locality, one read gets edge + properties. Con: bloats the CSR, breaks SIMD on target ID decode.
Parallel columnar properties: the CSR's edges array stores only target row IDs (tight, SIMD-friendly). Each property lives in a parallel array indexed the same way as edges[]. Reading edge at position K means reading edges[K] and prop1[K] and prop2[K]. Pro: columnar — if you don't need properties, you don't pay for them. SIMD-friendly. Compression-friendly per property.
We go parallel columnar. Same reasoning as the rest of our column store: queries that don't touch a property don't read it. Properties get appropriate compression (delta encoding for timestamps, dictionary for low-cardinality labels, FOR for small ints).
One concrete design: each edge column in the schema declares its property schema upfront. The file's edge section header records the property layout. At query time we know which parallel arrays to read for the requested properties.
The inline-edge-column-vs-CSR relationship
Earlier we said the row's edge column contains an inline stub. Let me make that concrete now that we have the CSR.
The edge column for row N at storage time:
{
  count: u16,
  // Either inline or pointer, based on count
  if count <= INLINE_THRESHOLD (8):
    targets: [target_row_id; count]   // inline, packed
  else:
    csr_offset: u32  // index into the edge section's offset array
}
For most rows (low degree, count ≤ 8), the row's edge column is self-contained. Reading the row's page gives you both the row data and its outgoing edges. One page read for the most common traversal pattern.
For high-degree rows, the inline stub holds only the count and a pointer into the CSR. Reading the row gives you the count; if you want the edges, you do one more read into the edge section.
The threshold of 8 is tunable. The principle: it should be small enough that inline storage doesn't bloat the row chunk for high-degree-tail nodes, large enough that the common case (most nodes have ≤ N edges) is inline. For typical graphs N=4 to N=16 works; 8 is a reasonable default and we make it configurable per column.
This gives us Neo4j-class single-hop traversal latency for the common case (one page read) and graceful degradation for high-degree nodes (one extra read into the CSR, which is itself well-cached).
MVCC for edges
Edges are versioned the same way as rows. Each edge carries xmin (LSN at which the edge was created) and xmax (LSN at which it was deleted, or ∞ if live).
Two storage choices:
Per-edge xmin/xmax: 16 bytes per edge. Honest, simple, expensive at scale. For a graph with a billion edges, that's 16GB just for versioning.
Compressed version metadata: edges in a file share a common creation LSN range. Store the file's LSN range in the header; per-edge xmin is a delta from the base. Most edges have xmax = ∞ (live), so encode that with a single bit and only store xmax when set. With these tricks, version metadata compresses to ~1-3 bytes per edge for typical workloads.
Go with the compressed version. Specifically: a parallel xmin_delta array (1-2 bytes per edge typically, encoded as PFOR-Delta from a per-file base LSN), and a parallel xmax array that's sparse — only present for edges that have been deleted at some point. Most files have a tiny xmax array because most edges in a fresh file are still live.
Visibility check at read time: for each edge candidate, check xmin_delta[k] + file_base_lsn ≤ snapshot_lsn and either xmax[k] is unset or xmax[k] > snapshot_lsn. One comparison, two array reads, fast.
When edges are deleted, the deletion writes a new tombstone in a future memtable that lands in a future file. The old file still has the edge with xmax=∞ in its storage — we never modify it. The new file has a tombstone entry. Query layer merges both files' edges, sees the tombstone, drops the edge. Compaction eventually merges the old file with the tombstone and drops both. Same pattern as everywhere else.
Concrete encoding details
Forward CSR:

offsets[]: PFOR-Delta encoded. The offsets are monotonically increasing, so deltas are small and bit-packable. SIMD decode in blocks of 128.
edges[] (target row IDs): within each source's neighbor list, target IDs are sorted; encode as PFOR-Delta within each source's slice. Across sources, the values reset (no delta across source boundaries).
Property arrays: parallel to edges[], encoded per property type (delta for timestamps, dictionary for labels, FOR for small ints, raw for floats).

Reverse index:

Sorted array of target row IDs (one entry per distinct target referenced by this file's edges).
Parallel array of source pointer lists. Each entry is a small CSR-style sublist: count and either inline source local IDs (if count ≤ threshold) or pointer into a packed source-ID region.
Fanout index: binary-search-friendly index over the target row ID array, every ~128 entries.

Bloom filter:

1KB filter over target row IDs in this file. Sufficient for typical false positive rates at typical file edge counts.

Section header:

magic, version
num_sources (forward index size)
num_targets (reverse index size)
total_edges
property schema (column names, types, encodings)
base_lsn (for compressing per-edge xmin)
file-local-to-global row ID translation table (or pointer to it; this might be shared with HNSW)

Section layout summary
Edge Section (per edge column):
  Section Header (magic, schema, stats, base_lsn)
  
  Forward Index:
    Offsets array (PFOR-Delta, indexed by file-local source ID)
    Edges array (target row IDs, PFOR-Delta per source)
    Property arrays (parallel to edges, one per declared property)
    xmin_delta array (parallel, PFOR-Delta from base_lsn)
    xmax array (sparse, present only if any edges deleted)
  
  Reverse Index:
    Sorted target row IDs array
    Source list array (parallel, sublists of source local IDs)
    Fanout index (every 128 entries)
  
  Bloom Filter (over target row IDs, ~1KB)
  
  Translation Table (file-local source ID → global row ID)
    [may be shared with HNSW section]
Footer points to the edge section's offset, length, and the offsets of the major sub-regions.
Query-time picture
Forward traversal of S (single hop):

Read S's row (in column chunks). The edge column gives count and either inline edges or a CSR offset.
If inline: we have the edges, done. One page read total.
If overflowed: read the offset array at S's local ID position, then read the edges array slice. One additional page read.
Apply MVCC visibility filter on each edge.
Apply property filters if any (read parallel property arrays).
Return target row IDs.

Reverse traversal of T (single hop, single file):

Check the file's bloom filter for T. If negative, skip the file.
Binary search the sorted target row ID array for T. Use fanout index to narrow.
Read the source list at T's position. Returns local source IDs.
Translate local source IDs to global row IDs via the translation table.
Apply MVCC visibility.

Reverse traversal of T (across all files):
For each file, do steps 1-5 above. Most files are skipped at step 1. Merge results across files. The cost scales with the number of files containing edges to T, not with the total number of files.
Multi-hop traversal: Implemented at the query layer as iterated single-hop. Out of scope for storage.
Honest tradeoffs
Storage cost. The 2x cost of forward + reverse indexing is real. For an edge-heavy schema, edges become a meaningful fraction of file size. For workloads where reverse traversal isn't needed on a particular edge column, we should allow REVERSE INDEX = false at schema time to skip the reverse half. Not all edges need reverse indexes.
Compaction cost. When compacting N files, we read N edge sections, merge edges (with MVCC version resolution), and rebuild forward + reverse CSR for the merged set. The rebuild is the dominant CPU cost during compaction for graph-heavy data. It's not optional — we want a single clean CSR per output file. Acceptable.
Cross-file reverse traversal performance. Even with bloom filters, reverse traversal cost grows with the number of files. For a system with 10,000 files and modest bloom filter discrimination, we might check 100 files per reverse query. That's 100 bloom checks (cheap) plus, say, 5-20 actual reverse-index lookups. Probably acceptable, definitely worse than Neo4j's pointer-chase. The mitigation is aggressive compaction for graph-heavy workloads, reducing file count.
Property schema rigidity. I've assumed properties are declared at schema time. Variable per-edge properties (some edges have a weight, others don't, others have a weight and a confidence) don't fit the parallel-columnar model cleanly. We could support a JSON-blob property column for flexibility but it'd be much worse for query performance. The right call is to require schema declaration; users with truly variable properties pay the JSON cost explicitly.
Local source ID space. I've assumed file-local source IDs are small (file has up to ~100K sources). This is fine for our 256MB memtable sizes. For very large compacted files (10M+ rows), local IDs need to be 32 bits, which is fine.
Three places to push back
The 2x storage cost vs. reverse-traversal performance. I committed to forward + reverse per file. The alternative is forward-only with a slower reverse path (scan all files). For workloads that genuinely never do reverse traversal, the 2x cost is pure waste. Schema-level opt-out (REVERSE INDEX = false) addresses this for the user who knows, but I'm making forward+reverse the default. Reasonable to flip this default to forward-only and require explicit opt-in to reverse indexing. My instinct is reverse-by-default is correct because graph databases without fast reverse traversal feel broken — but it's a real choice.
Inline edges threshold of 8. I picked 8 essentially by feel. For a graph with average degree 3 and a long tail to 1000, threshold 8 catches the common case. For a denser graph with average degree 20, threshold 8 spills everyone to the CSR and the inline mechanism is dead weight. The right answer is probably to make this per-column with a default of 8, and possibly auto-tune based on degree distribution observed during the memtable's life. Auto-tune is v2; per-column with default 8 is v1.
Bloom filter as the cross-file reverse acceleration. Bloom filters work but they're coarse — they tell you a file might have edges to T but not how many or which sources. An alternative is per-file min_target_row_id / max_target_row_id range (cheaper than bloom, less discriminating). Or hierarchical: range + bloom. For v1 I'd go with bloom only; ranges can be added in the footer trivially if we need them. Reasonable to disagree and want both from the start.

That's the edge section. Locking the decisions explicitly:

Forward CSR with PFOR-Delta encoded offsets and edges arrays, parallel columnar property arrays
Per-file reverse index as sorted target row ID array + parallel source lists + fanout index
Compressed MVCC: xmin_delta from per-file base, sparse xmax array
Inline edge stubs in row's edge column for ≤ threshold (default 8), CSR pointer above
Bloom filter per file over target row IDs for cross-file reverse traversal pruning
Schema-level opt-out for reverse indexing per edge column
Parallel columnar properties with declared schema (no JSON blob in v1)

Two things left: the footer schema (the FlatBuffers glue), and then we're done with the file format and we can move up the stack to compaction, the buffer pool, the catalog, the query plan, all the layers we still haven't touched.
