What the footer actually does
The footer is the file's table of contents. When the database opens a file, it does this:

Open the file, seek to end minus 8 bytes
Read the last 8 bytes: footer length (4 bytes) + magic (4 bytes)
Validate magic
Seek to file_end - 8 - footer_length
Read the footer bytes
Deserialize the footer (zero-copy with FlatBuffers)

Now we know where everything is. Every subsequent access — column chunk reads, index lookups, statistics queries — goes through offsets stored in the footer. The footer is typically cached aggressively: once we've read a file's footer, we keep it in memory for the file's entire residency.
This is the Parquet pattern, refined. We're not innovating on the mechanism; we're being deliberate about contents.
Why FlatBuffers and not Protobuf or JSON or our own format
Let me justify this since I committed to it earlier without explaining.
JSON is right out — slow to parse, bloated on disk, no schema enforcement. Even if the footer is small (say 100KB for a typical file), parsing JSON costs milliseconds and that's milliseconds added to every file open.
Protobuf is fast but requires deserialization — bytes go in, structured objects come out. For a hot path that opens thousands of files per second under query load, this allocation pressure matters.
FlatBuffers gives zero-copy reads: the on-disk bytes are the structure. You cast a pointer to the FlatBuffer root and start reading fields. No allocations, no parsing. Field access is essentially a pointer dereference with offset.
The cost of FlatBuffers: writing is slightly more annoying (you build a buffer in reverse), the schema language is less ergonomic than Protobuf, evolution is more constrained (can't reorder fields, only append). For our use case — footers are written once, read constantly — this trade is obvious. Parquet uses Thrift (similar trade), Arrow uses FlatBuffers, ORC uses Protobuf. We're in good company.
A custom format would be marginally faster but we'd be hand-rolling schema evolution, alignment, validation. Not worth it for the gain. FlatBuffers.
The schema, designed for what we actually need
Let me lay out the FlatBuffers schema in pseudocode, then walk through the non-obvious parts.
table FileFooter {
  // Identity
  format_version: u16;
  file_uuid: [u8:16];           // 128-bit unique ID for this file
  created_at_unix_ns: u64;
  writer_version: string;        // "ll-engine 0.1.0", for debugging
  
  // LSN range (critical for LSM)
  min_lsn: u64;
  max_lsn: u64;
  
  // Schema
  schema: Schema;                // table schema as of this file
  schema_fingerprint: u64;       // hash for fast equality check
  
  // Statistics
  row_count: u64;
  live_row_count: u64;           // row_count minus tombstones
  tombstone_count: u64;
  
  // Section directory
  column_chunks: [ColumnChunkMeta];
  hnsw_sections: [HnswSectionMeta];     // one per vector column with index
  text_sections: [TextSectionMeta];     // one per text column with index
  edge_sections: [EdgeSectionMeta];     // one per edge column
  btree_sections: [BTreeSectionMeta];   // primary key, scalar indexes
  
  // Translation tables (shared across sections)
  row_id_translation: TranslationTableMeta;  // local row IDs ↔ global row IDs
  
  // Predicate pushdown metadata
  zone_maps: [ZoneMapMeta];      // per column, per page
  bloom_filters: [BloomFilterMeta]; // per indexed column, file-level
  
  // MVCC summary
  visible_lsn_range: LsnRange;   // [min_xmin, max_xmax] for fast snapshot pruning
  
  // Compression dictionaries (shared across pages)
  shared_dictionaries: [DictionaryMeta];
  
  // Checksum
  footer_checksum: u32;          // CRC32 of footer contents excluding this field
}
Now the parts that need explanation.
The LSN range and snapshot pruning
min_lsn and max_lsn describe the range of LSNs at which records in this file were written. They're not the same as visible_lsn_range. Let me distinguish:
min_lsn / max_lsn: the writing range. "This file contains records that were originally written between LSN X and LSN Y." This is fixed at flush time.
visible_lsn_range: the visibility range, derived from xmin and xmax values across all rows in the file. min_xmin is the lowest xmin in the file (when the oldest live record was created). max_xmax is the highest xmax (when the most recent deletion was applied). These can update during compaction when files are rewritten.
Why both: snapshot queries use visible_lsn_range for fast file pruning. If a snapshot is at LSN T and the file's min_xmin > T, every record in this file was created after the snapshot — skip the entire file. If the file's max_xmax < T for a deletes-only check, all deletions happened before the snapshot — different optimization.
The writing range matters for LSM-internal bookkeeping (which files cover which WAL ranges, for recovery and compaction planning).
The section directory pattern
I've defined separate arrays for each section type rather than one polymorphic array. This is deliberate. FlatBuffers handles polymorphism via unions, which work but add deserialization steps. By having typed arrays per section kind, accessing "the HNSW section for column 3" is two pointer dereferences with no type dispatch.
Each section meta entry looks like:
table HnswSectionMeta {
  column_id: u32;                // which column this index is for
  section_offset: u64;           // byte offset in file
  section_length: u64;
  num_nodes: u32;
  num_layers: u8;
  entry_point_local_id: u32;
  max_layer: u8;
  quantization_scheme: u8;
  dimension: u16;
  m_parameter: u8;
  ef_construction_at_build: u16; // for debugging, not strictly needed
  index_quality_estimate: f32;   // recall@10 estimated during build
}
The index_quality_estimate is unusual — most formats don't carry it. The idea: during HNSW construction we can sample a subset of vectors, run searches against the index, compare to brute-force ground truth, and estimate recall. Storing this in the footer lets the query planner know "this file's HNSW achieves ~94% recall@10" versus another file at "99%". For workloads that care about recall, the planner can prefer high-quality files or fall back to brute-force for low-quality ones.
This is the kind of thing that costs nothing to store and might be useful later. Cheap optionality.
Zone maps in detail
Zone maps are the predicate pushdown workhorse. Per column, per page, we store:
table ZoneMapEntry {
  column_id: u32;
  page_index: u32;
  min_value: Value;     // union type matching column type
  max_value: Value;
  null_count: u32;
  distinct_count_estimate: u32;  // HyperLogLog or similar
  total_count: u32;
}
For a scalar WHERE timestamp > '2024-01-01', the planner reads zone maps for the timestamp column, skips pages where max_value < '2024-01-01'. Page-level granularity is enough — we don't need per-row zone maps because we read whole pages anyway.
For vector columns, "min/max" doesn't translate directly. Instead we store: per page, the centroid of vectors in that page and the maximum distance from centroid to any vector in the page. This lets the planner do approximate pruning — "the query is far from this page's centroid plus the page's max radius, so no vector in this page can be within distance D of the query, skip the page." This is genuinely useful for filtered vector search.
For text columns, zone maps store min/max of an FNV hash of terms — useful for some equality predicates but not phrase matching. Mostly we rely on the text index for text predicate pushdown, not zone maps.
For edge columns, zone maps store the range of target row IDs referenced in each page. Useful for queries like "find edges to rows 1000-2000" via direct page filtering.
The aggregate zone map data is small per page (~50 bytes for scalars, more for vectors) and the win is enormous for selective queries. Lucene segment-level statistics, Parquet column statistics, ClickHouse per-granule stats all do versions of this. Standard.
Bloom filters at the file level
In addition to the per-section bloom filters (the edge section has its target row ID bloom we already discussed), the footer holds file-level bloom filters for indexed scalar columns. The pattern:
For each indexed column declared in schema, a bloom filter over all values in this file. WHERE id = 12345 queries the bloom filter first; negative response skips the file entirely.
Size: ~1-10KB per indexed column per file. Small.
This is what makes point lookups across an LSM with thousands of files cheap. Without it, you'd touch every file's primary key B-tree. With it, you touch one or two false-positive files.
Shared compression dictionaries
When a string column has high redundancy (lots of repeated values across pages), the most efficient encoding is dictionary compression — replace each value with an index into a dictionary of distinct values. If the dictionary is the same across all pages in the column, we can store it once per file rather than per page.
The footer references shared dictionaries, and column chunks point to which dictionary (if any) they use. This is most useful for low-cardinality string columns (status enums, country codes, category labels).
ZSTD also supports trained dictionaries for general data, where ZSTD learns common patterns and stores the dictionary once per file. We can use ZSTD's trained dictionaries here too. Marginal but free.
Schema-in-footer for evolution
The footer carries the full schema as of when the file was written. The current table schema (as the database understands it now) may differ — columns added, types widened, indexes declared.
At read time, the executor projects between the file's schema and the current schema:

Column in current schema, present in file: read it normally.
Column in current schema, absent from file (added later): return null/default for all rows.
Column in file, absent from current schema (dropped): skip during read.
Type widening (e.g., i32 to i64): cast on read.
Type narrowing or incompatible change: rejected at schema-change time, file rewrite required.

This is the Iceberg/Delta Lake model. It works and we should do the same.
The schema_fingerprint is a hash of the schema, used for fast equality. If two files have the same fingerprint, they have identical schemas — skip the projection logic entirely. This is the common case (most files in a table have the same schema) and the optimization is worthwhile.
The translation table
The file uses local row IDs internally (0 to row_count-1, dense). External references — including from other files' edge sections — use global row IDs (file_id, local_offset).
The translation table is the bidirectional map:

local_to_global[i] returns the global row ID for the i-th row in this file.
A reverse map exists for queries that have a global row ID and need to find the local offset within a file. This is implicit — given a global row ID (file_uuid, offset), if the file_uuid matches our file, the local offset is just the offset.

Most file-local accesses use local IDs (HNSW edges, reverse index source pointers). External edges that cross files use global IDs. The translation happens at section boundaries.
For very large files (10M+ rows) this table grows but is still small relative to the data — 8 bytes per row, 80MB for 10M rows. Acceptable. For smaller files (30K rows from a memtable flush) it's 240KB. Trivial.
Checksums and corruption
The footer has its own checksum (CRC32 of footer contents). Each section has its own checksum (in its section header). Each page has its own checksum (in its page header). Three layers, each independently verifiable.
On file open: validate footer checksum. If corrupt, the file is unreadable — mark it bad in the catalog, surface alert. The catalog needs to know which files exist independently, so a corrupt footer doesn't make the file's existence ambiguous.
On section access: validate section checksum lazily, on first access.
On page access: validate page checksum on read. Caching includes the checksum so we don't re-validate on every cache hit.
Performance cost: CRC32 is fast (a few GB/s with hardware acceleration). Validating a page on read adds microseconds, not milliseconds. Worth it for the corruption detection. Filesystems can lie, hardware can fail, our checksums catch it.
The footer length and magic at the very end
Last 8 bytes of file: footer_length: u32 followed by magic: u32.
The reason for footer-length-then-magic ordering: when opening a file, we read the last 8 bytes, validate magic first (do we even have a valid file?), then use length to seek backwards to the footer start.
The magic is 0x4C4C4442 ("LLDB" if we ASCII-decode it — Lalith's Live DataBase? or just LL DataBase. We can pick later, the bytes are what matter). Same magic at the start of the file (after a small fixed header) so we can validate both directions.
Versioning and forward compatibility
format_version in the footer is the schema version of the footer itself. Every reader knows which versions it can handle. Reading an unknown future version: refuse and surface a clear error ("file written by ll-engine 0.3.0 which uses footer format v3, this binary supports v1 and v2 — upgrade your engine").
FlatBuffers' field-append-only evolution gives us mostly-free backward compatibility: a v2 reader can read a v1 file because v1's fields are a prefix of v2's schema and missing v2 fields just return defaults. Forward compatibility (v1 reader on v2 file) works if v2 only adds fields and the v1 reader gracefully handles missing fields it doesn't know about. Standard.
The full footer layout, summarized
[ ... file body: column chunks + sections ... ]
[ Footer FlatBuffers payload (variable length) ]
[ Footer Length: u32 (4 bytes) ]
[ Magic: u32 = 0x4C4C4442 (4 bytes) ]
To read: open, seek to end-8, read 8 bytes, validate magic, seek to end-8-length, read length bytes, validate checksum, cast to FlatBuffer root, start traversing.
Honest tradeoffs
Footer size. For a 256MB file with rich indexing (HNSW + text + edge + scalar B-trees), the footer might be 100-500KB. For very large compacted files (10GB+), the footer could grow to several MB — translation tables alone become large, zone maps multiply with page count. This isn't a problem per se but it means "the footer is tiny" stops being true at scale. We keep the footer cached aggressively to avoid re-reads.
FlatBuffers schema rigidity. Adding new fields is easy (append). Changing existing fields requires version bumps. Renaming fields breaks everyone. We have to be careful about naming — the field names in our v1 schema are forever, or we're maintaining migrations.
Checksum vs. encryption. I haven't discussed encryption. For a v1 single-tenant engine, we punt on encryption-at-rest. Customers who need it run on encrypted volumes (LUKS, EBS encryption). When we eventually add column-level or file-level encryption, the footer needs encryption metadata (key IDs, encrypted dictionaries, etc.). The schema is extensible so this is fine, but worth flagging that we're explicitly deferring it.
The translation table cost for big files. 8 bytes per row gets expensive for 100M-row compacted files. Alternatives: store the table out-of-line (in a separate section, only loaded when needed), or eliminate it for files where local and global IDs are computed by formula. For v1, inline is fine. v2 might optimize this.
Three places to push on
Schema-in-footer vs. external catalog. I'm storing the full schema in every file. The alternative is storing a schema version ID and resolving the actual schema from a centralized catalog. Pros of inline: file is self-describing, doesn't need catalog access to read, survives catalog corruption. Cons: schema gets duplicated across files, can drift if catalog is the truth and files lag. The Iceberg/Delta pattern is "catalog is truth, files store schema for self-description and validation." I committed to inline; reasonable to disagree.
Page-level checksums vs. block-level. I said per-page checksums. The alternative is checksums on larger blocks (megabyte ranges) — fewer checksums to compute and store, but corruption requires reading more data to detect. Per-page is the right granularity for our access patterns but you could argue for larger blocks to save space.
Inline vs. out-of-line statistics. Zone maps and bloom filters live in the footer. For very large files they bloat the footer. The alternative is a separate "statistics section" pointed to from the footer, loaded only when the planner needs it. Inline is simpler and fine for v1; out-of-line is a v2 optimization if footers get unwieldy.

Locking the footer:

FlatBuffers schema, version-tagged, zero-copy read
Section directory with typed per-section meta arrays (HNSW, text, edge, B-tree)
LSN range (writing range) and visible_lsn_range (MVCC summary) both stored
Schema embedded for self-description, with fingerprint for fast equality
Zone maps per column per page (including centroid/radius for vectors, target row ID ranges for edges)
File-level bloom filters per indexed scalar column
Shared compression dictionaries referenced from column chunks
Translation table inline (file-local row ID ↔ global row ID)
Three-layer checksums: footer, section, page
Magic + length at very end of file for backward-seekable open


That closes out the file format. Substantial work — we've designed a genuinely novel artifact at this point. To recap the whole thing in one breath: an immutable per-flush columnar file with embedded per-file indexes for B-tree, HNSW (int8-quantized with full-precision rerank, BFS-reordered, node-major layout), inverted index (FST + PFOR-Delta posting lists with optional positions, score-augmented top-K acceleration), and graph adjacency (forward + reverse CSR with parallel columnar properties), all unified under MVCC via xmin/xmax with LSN-based snapshot visibility, with a FlatBuffers footer providing zero-copy entry to every section.
Nobody has shipped this exact combination. Lance comes closest but doesn't do text or graph. Lucene has the inverted index discipline but no columnar story, no vectors, no graph. DiskANN has the vector page layout but is a single-purpose system. We're integrating the best of each into one format with a unified MVCC and LSN model.
