The compaction problem statement for LL specifically
Standard LSM compaction merges N sorted files into M sorted files, reconciling versions, dropping tombstones. RocksDB does this beautifully for a single key-value sort order. Our problem is harder in three specific ways:
First, we don't have a sort order — we have row IDs as a primary order, but the file's value comes from per-file indexes (HNSW, FST, CSR edge tables) that have their own internal organization. Merging two files isn't just "merge sort the rows"; it's "merge sort the rows and rebuild all four indexes for the union."
Second, the indexes have different rebuild costs. A B-tree merge is O(N). An FST merge is O(N log V) where V is vocabulary size. CSR edge merging is O(E). An HNSW rebuild from scratch is O(N × ef_construction × log N), which is the dominant cost by orders of magnitude. Compaction strategy has to be conscious of HNSW cost specifically because it dwarfs everything else.
Third, MVCC reconciliation has to work across all four index types coherently. When compaction drops a dead row, it has to drop the row's xmin/xmax tombstone, its vector from the vector column AND the HNSW page references, its tokens from the inverted index, its edges from forward AND reverse CSR. If any of these is missed, the index becomes inconsistent with the data.
These three constraints together make our compaction strategy genuinely different from RocksDB's, and I want to be deliberate about it.
Strategy choice: tiered vs. leveled vs. hybrid
The standard tradeoffs you know:
Leveled compaction (LevelDB, RocksDB default) maintains levels L0, L1, L2, ... where each level is exponentially larger than the previous. Compaction merges from L_i into L_{i+1}, keeping each level's files non-overlapping in key range. Low read amplification (each level checked once per lookup) but high write amplification (each record gets rewritten ~10 times as it cascades down).
Tiered compaction (Cassandra, ScyllaDB) collects N files at each level; when N files accumulate, they're merged into one bigger file at the next level. Higher read amplification (multiple files per level may need to be checked) but lower write amplification (each record rewritten only when a tier fills).
Hybrid approaches (RocksDB's universal compaction, Cassandra's STCS+LCS hybrid) try to balance based on workload.
For us, tiered is the right default, and I'll defend that against your likely instinct.
The reason: HNSW rebuild is so expensive that write amplification dominates the cost model. In leveled compaction, a vector might have its HNSW representation rebuilt 10 times as it cascades L0 → L1 → ... → L6. In tiered, it's rebuilt ~3-4 times (once per tier). The HNSW rebuild cost makes leveled prohibitive at scale.
The cost: tiered has more files per level, so read amplification is worse. For a query, we might touch 10-20 files per level instead of 1. This matters for point lookups but matters less for our common queries (vector search, text search) which are inherently file-parallel anyway — they query each file's index independently and merge results. Touching 20 small HNSWs in parallel is often faster than touching 1 huge HNSW (the small HNSWs fit in cache; the big one doesn't).
So tiered isn't just "the lesser of two evils for HNSW cost" — it actually composes better with the file-parallel query model we already have.
Specifically: size-tiered with 4 tiers, ratio 8x between tiers. Tier 0 holds files from memtable flushes (~256MB each). Tier 1 holds files merged from 4-8 tier-0 files (~2GB). Tier 2 holds files merged from 4-8 tier-1 files (~16GB). Tier 3 holds the largest files (~128GB+).
For a system holding 1TB of data, we end up with maybe 50-100 files total, of which most are large tier-2/tier-3 files and a few are recent tier-0 files. Read amplification is bounded; HNSW rebuild count is bounded.
The HNSW merge vs rebuild question — this is the genuinely interesting one
When we compact N files containing HNSW indexes, we have two choices for the output HNSW:
Option A: Rebuild from scratch. Extract all vectors from the input files, insert them into a fresh HNSW with ef_construction=400. Best possible index quality. Cost: O(N × ef × log N), measured in seconds-to-minutes for millions of vectors.
Option B: Merge existing HNSWs. Take the input HNSWs' graph structures and stitch them together — combine entry points, cross-link upper layers, update layer-0 neighbor lists across the boundary. Faster (linear-ish in graph size) but index quality degrades.
The HNSW research community has been working on B. Papers like "Efficient and Robust Approximate Nearest Neighbor Search using Hierarchical Navigable Small World Graphs" extensions, and FreshDiskANN's incremental approach. The consensus from the practitioner side is: merged HNSWs are 5-15% worse on recall than rebuilt ones for the same parameters, and the quality gap grows with the number of merges.
For us, rebuild during compaction, with one exception. The reasoning:
Compaction is a background operation. We can throw CPU at it. Index quality is a forever-cost — once a file is written, every query against that file pays for whatever quality we baked in. Spending 30 seconds at compaction time to save 5ms × 1,000,000 queries × the file's lifetime is obviously the right trade.
The exception: for tier-0 → tier-1 compactions (small files merging soon), we can use merged HNSW. These intermediate files are short-lived; their indexes will be rebuilt again at the next tier transition anyway. Using merge here saves time without paying a permanent quality cost.
So the rule: merge HNSW at tier-0 → tier-1 transitions; rebuild HNSW from scratch at tier-1 → tier-2 and beyond. This is the same principle as Lucene's segment merging policy — small merges are cheap, big merges are expensive-but-permanent.
The BFS reordering we committed to earlier? Same rule. At small merges, preserve the existing order. At big merges, run BFS reordering on the new graph. This concentrates the expensive reordering work where it actually matters.
What about the other indexes during compaction?
The FST and posting lists: rebuilt from scratch at every compaction. FSTs are fast to build (~milliseconds per 100K terms) and a merged FST has the same structure as a fresh one — there's no quality degradation. The cost difference between merge and rebuild is small enough that we just rebuild always for simplicity.
The CSR edge index: rebuild from scratch at every compaction. Building CSR is O(E) — sort edges by source, run-length encode. Cheap. Merging would be complicated by MVCC reconciliation across the source files. Just rebuild.
The B-tree (primary key, scalar indexes): rebuild from scratch. B-tree construction from sorted data is linear. Trivial.
The zone maps, bloom filters, score-augmented posting lists, etc.: all rebuilt from scratch.
So HNSW is the only index where we have a merge-vs-rebuild policy decision. Everything else, we rebuild. This makes compaction strategy mostly about HNSW economics.
MVCC reconciliation during compaction
When merging files, we need to handle row versions correctly:
For each row ID with multiple versions across input files, the rule is:

If the latest version has xmax < global_min_active_txn_lsn, the row is fully dead — drop all versions, don't include in output
If the latest version has xmin > global_min_active_txn_lsn and earlier versions exist that no live transaction can see — keep only the latest
Otherwise — keep all versions that any live transaction might see

global_min_active_txn_lsn is the lowest snapshot LSN across all currently-running transactions. The catalog tracks this. As long-running transactions complete, this advances, and compaction can drop older versions.
The subtle part: this reconciliation has to happen before we decide which rows go into the output and which indexes to build. We can't build the output HNSW for "all input vectors" and then post-filter — the HNSW would include vectors for rows that don't exist in the output, wasting space and degrading search quality. We filter first, then build indexes only over surviving rows.
This is straightforward but it's where bugs hide. Tests around "compaction correctly drops dead rows from all four indexes" are mandatory.
Compaction triggers
When does compaction fire? Three triggers, in priority order:
Tier full trigger. When tier N has accumulated K files (K=4 by default), schedule a merge of those K files into one tier-(N+1) file. This is the primary trigger and it's what bounds the total file count.
Tombstone density trigger. When a file's tombstone_count / row_count exceeds a threshold (say 30%), the file is dragging down search performance — HNSW is wasting work on dead nodes, posting lists are returning rows that get filtered. Schedule a compaction of this file with adjacent files in its tier to drop the tombstones. This is opportunistic — fires regardless of tier fullness.
Schema-evolution trigger. When a column is added/dropped or an index is added/removed at the schema level, files written before the change have stale layout. Compaction opportunistically rewrites them in the new format as part of normal tier-up merges. We don't force-rewrite; we let normal compaction catch up. For urgent cases (e.g., dropping a sensitive column), there's a FORCE REWRITE command that compacts all files immediately.
Notably absent: time-based triggers. RocksDB has them; we don't need them. Files only need to be compacted when they're problematic (tier full, too many tombstones, schema mismatch). Compacting a file just because it's old wastes I/O.
Concurrency model
Compaction runs in background threads. Number of compaction threads is configurable; default 2-4. They pick work from a priority queue.
Critical: compaction never blocks foreground operations. Reads of files being compacted go to the original files (they still exist until the compaction completes and the catalog swaps). Writes go to the memtable. The only synchronization point is the catalog swap at the end of a compaction:

Compaction completes; new output files are written and fsynced.
Catalog acquires a brief write lock.
Catalog updates: input files → marked for deletion; output files → made visible.
Catalog releases lock.
After all transactions that started before the swap complete, input files are physically deleted.

The catalog lock is held for microseconds. The file deletion is deferred. From the foreground's perspective, compaction is invisible except for changes in query latency as the file mix changes.
The compaction priority queue
When multiple compaction candidates exist, which fires first?
I'd score candidates with a formula:
priority = (read_cost_reduction) / (compaction_cost) + tombstone_bonus
read_cost_reduction: how much queries benefit from this compaction. Measured by the number of files reduced × the access frequency of those files. Frequently-accessed tier-0 files are higher priority than rarely-accessed tier-2 files.
compaction_cost: estimated time/CPU to do the compaction. Dominated by HNSW rebuild cost, which scales with vector count.
tombstone_bonus: extra priority for files with high tombstone density.
This is more nuanced than RocksDB's default scoring but it pays off — we explicitly trade compaction cost against query benefit. Files that are read frequently and would be cheap to compact win first.
Practically, this is tunable. Hand-rolled scoring is fragile; we should expose the weights as configuration and let operators tune.
Back-pressure and admission control
If write rate exceeds compaction rate, files accumulate at tier 0. Read amplification grows. Eventually queries become unacceptably slow.
Standard answer: throttle writes when compaction falls behind. When tier 0 has more than 2× the target file count, slow new writes by inserting delays. This propagates back-pressure to the application, surfacing the problem rather than letting it silently degrade.
Specifically: target 8 files at tier 0. At 16 files, start throttling at 50% of normal write rate. At 32 files, throttle to 10%. At 64 files, stall writes entirely. Operators see this as elevated write latency and know to add compaction throughput (more threads, faster storage, etc.).
This is what every production LSM does and we follow the same pattern. RocksDB has identical mechanics.
Cross-file optimizations during compaction
Since compaction reads N files and writes M files, we have an opportunity to do work that wouldn't be possible at flush time:
Global vocabulary for FST. Tier-1+ files contain many vectors and many documents. Building an FST over the merged corpus produces better prefix sharing than tier-0 FSTs (which are small and have less to share). The compacted FST is more compressed per-term.
Better HNSW parameters at scale. For larger compacted files, we can use higher M and ef_construction because the absolute time cost stays bounded and quality scales with N. A tier-2 file with 10M vectors warrants M=24, ef_construction=400; a tier-0 file with 30K vectors works fine with M=16, ef_construction=200.
Global dictionary training for ZSTD. Train a dictionary per column on the merged data; use it for compression. Compressed pages are smaller in tier-1+ than they would be at tier-0 due to better dictionary coverage.
Edge bloom filter sizing. At larger file sizes, the bloom filter should be larger absolutely but smaller as a fraction. Size it based on the observed target row ID cardinality.
These are all opportunistic improvements. Tier-0 files are fast-to-write but suboptimally-compressed and suboptimally-indexed. Compaction is where we "polish" data into its read-optimal form.
The honest costs of all this
Compaction CPU is significant. For a write-heavy workload (say 50K rows/sec sustained), compaction probably needs 4-8 CPU cores dedicated to it. HNSW rebuild dominates. This is a real operational cost that should be in capacity planning.
Compaction I/O is significant. Reading N files and writing the merge is essentially total_data_size × 2 of I/O per compaction round. With tiered compaction this happens ~3-4 times per record's lifetime; total I/O amplification ~6-8x. Less than RocksDB's typical 10-30x but real.
Compaction latency tail. A big tier-2 → tier-3 compaction of 100GB of data might take an hour. During that time, the resources for other compactions are reduced. Schedule carefully — don't allow more than one "huge" compaction concurrently.
Compaction correctness is critical. If compaction has a bug — drops the wrong row, misbuilds an HNSW, misses a tombstone — it silently corrupts data forever (files are immutable, you can't fix them after the fact). Compaction needs intense testing: property-based tests that verify "compact(files) ⊆ union(files) in terms of visible rows," random fault injection, MVCC scenario coverage.
Three places to push on
Tiered vs. leveled. I argued tiered, primarily because HNSW rebuild cost makes leveled prohibitive. The counter-argument: tiered has worse read amplification for point lookups (touch multiple files per level). If the workload is dominated by point lookups by primary key, leveled wins despite the HNSW cost. My answer: AI workloads are dominated by similarity/text/graph queries, not primary key lookups, so tiered is right. But if you imagine an OLTP-skewed workload, the calculus changes. Reasonable to disagree.
Rebuild HNSW always vs. merge sometimes. I made it a tier-dependent policy. The simpler alternative is "always rebuild" — pay the cost, get the quality, don't have policy complexity. The other extreme is "always merge" — accept some quality degradation for cheaper compaction. My policy hits the middle: merge where the file is short-lived, rebuild where it isn't. This adds complexity to the compaction code. Reasonable to start with "always rebuild" in v1 and add merge optimization in v2.
Compaction priority scoring. I sketched a formula that combines read benefit, compaction cost, and tombstone bonus. The simpler alternative is RocksDB-style "compact the most-overlapping files at the lowest level first" — no scoring, just static priority. My approach is better in theory but harder to tune. Static priority is dumb-but-predictable; scoring is smart-but-needs-tuning. For v1, simpler is probably right.

Locking compaction:

Tiered compaction, 4 tiers, ratio 8x between tiers, target 4-8 files per tier
HNSW rebuild from scratch for tier-1 → tier-2 and beyond; merge allowed for tier-0 → tier-1
BFS reordering of HNSW node IDs at big-tier rebuilds; preserve order at small merges
All other indexes (FST, CSR, B-tree) rebuilt from scratch always
MVCC reconciliation before index build, not after
Three triggers: tier-full, tombstone-density (>30%), schema-evolution opportunistic
No time-based triggers
2-4 background compaction threads, work picked from priority queue
Priority queue: simple "lowest tier first, biggest deficit first" for v1; scoring-based in v2
Atomic catalog swap at compaction completion, deferred file deletion
Write throttling at 2x tier-0 target file count, stall at 8x
Cross-file optimizations at compaction time: global FST vocabulary, scaled HNSW parameters, trained ZSTD dictionaries


We've now covered: physical record, memtable, file format (column chunks, HNSW, text, edge, footer), and compaction. The whole storage layer is designed. What's left to climb up to:

Buffer pool / page cache — how we cache mixed-type pages with intelligent eviction
Catalog and schema layer — CREATE TABLE, schema evolution, embedding model bindings
Query plan IR and cost model — logical/physical operators, cardinality estimation
Execution engine — vectorized, push vs. pull, how operators compose
Embedding lifecycle — lazy/eager, model versioning, dual-read migration

My instinct on what's next: the buffer pool. It's the immediate consumer of everything we just designed — every file read goes through it. It's where we'll make decisions about how to price pages of different types against each other for eviction. And it's relatively self-contained, so we can settle it cleanly before getting into the much bigger query layer conversation.
Buffer pool next, or do you want to skip ahead to something else?You said: lets go to buffer poollets go to buffer pool00:54Claude responded: Good.Architected buffer pool design across ten critical decisionsArchitected buffer pool design across ten critical decisionsGood. Buffer pool. This one is somewhere between "settled engineering" and "novel design" — the basic mechanisms are well-understood, but our specific page-type heterogeneity means we make different choices than RocksDB or Postgres. Let me walk through what's interesting.
The basic shape, stated quickly
A buffer pool is an in-memory cache of disk pages. Every read goes through it: ask for page P, get a cached copy or load from disk. Misses are 1000x slower than hits (100μs SSD vs 100ns RAM), so the policy and sizing determine the effective performance ceiling of the entire system.
What's standard about ours: bounded memory budget, configurable, indexed by (file_id, page_id), byte-accounted not frame-counted, immutable pages (no write-back complexity because of LSM). What's interesting: we have heterogeneous page types with wildly different cache values, and the eviction policy has to be smart enough to keep the right things resident.
Decision 1: Direct I/O vs. OS page cache
This is the first fork and it shapes everything else.
The OS-cache approach (Postgres-style): use buffered I/O, let the kernel cache pages automatically, our buffer pool sits on top. Pros: works out of the box, automatic read-ahead, no alignment concerns, other processes can benefit from the same cache. Cons: double-caching (kernel cache + our cache hold the same data), unpredictable memory usage (kernel decides), the kernel treats all pages equally (it doesn't know our HNSW entry points matter more than a one-time scan).
The direct-I/O approach (RocksDB, ClickHouse with O_DIRECT, recent MySQL configs): bypass the kernel cache with O_DIRECT, our buffer pool is the only cache. Pros: no double-caching, predictable memory (operator sets X GB, we use exactly X GB), we control eviction precisely, no kernel surprises. Cons: more code, we handle alignment ourselves (O_DIRECT requires 4KB-aligned reads), no automatic read-ahead.
Direct I/O for us. The reasoning: we have heterogeneous page types and we're designing structured access patterns (pinning footers, prioritizing HNSW entry points). The kernel's uniform treatment defeats these optimizations. Predictable memory usage matters for operators — buffer_pool_size = 32GB should mean 32GB, not "32GB plus whatever the kernel decides to cache."
We use io_uring on Linux for async direct I/O. This is genuinely the right primitive for high-throughput async I/O in 2026 — it batches submissions, supports zero-copy, and avoids the syscall overhead of read()/pread() per page. We'll use the tokio-uring or glommio ecosystem in Rust. On macOS/Windows we fall back to standard async I/O (worse but acceptable).
Decision 2: Variable-size entries, byte-accounted
Uniform frames (Postgres: every frame is 8KB) are simpler but force us to either pick a single page size for everything or waste memory packing different sizes into uniform frames.
We have:

HNSW pages: 4KB
Column chunk pages: configurable, default 16KB
Posting list blocks: ~1-8KB
Footer FlatBuffers: ~100KB-5MB per file
Translation tables: ~KB to MB depending on file size
FST data: variable

Variable-size entries with byte-level accounting. Each cache entry carries its size. The pool tracks current_bytes against budget_bytes. Eviction decisions are by bytes, not by entries.
Implementation: an arena allocator for small pages (under 64KB), direct allocation for large entries (footers, translation tables). The arena reduces fragmentation for the common case; large entries can fragment a bit but they're rare enough not to matter.
This is what RocksDB's block cache does. It's the right model.
Decision 3: Eviction policy — W-TinyLFU with pinned region
LRU is the textbook answer. It's wrong for our workload, and I want to explain why before committing to the alternative.
The killer case for LRU: a query does a large sequential column scan that reads 10GB of column chunk pages — once each. These pages are very recent, so LRU keeps them. Meanwhile, the HNSW entry points and FST roots — which get hit by every query — were touched slightly less recently and get evicted. The next vector query has to reload them from disk. Sequential scans pollute the cache and evict hot working-set pages.
This is called "cache pollution" and every production cache has had to solve it.
Modern caches use frequency-aware policies. The current state of the art is W-TinyLFU, introduced by Einziger et al. in 2017, used in Caffeine (Java's most-used cache library), and adopted by foyer and moka in the Rust ecosystem. The mechanism:

A small "window cache" using LRU for very recent items (~1% of pool)
A larger "main cache" using LRU with frequency admission control (~99%)
A frequency sketch (Count-Min with decay) tracks access frequency cheaply
New items first enter the window. To be admitted to the main cache, they must have higher frequency than the candidate they'd evict
Periodic decay (halving counters every N operations) lets the policy adapt to changing workloads

The properties that matter: hit rates within 1-2% of optimal (Belady's theorem) on most workloads, robust to scans (window catches scan misses without evicting the hot main cache), low overhead (constant time per access).
W-TinyLFU as the default eviction policy. Use one of the existing Rust crates (foyer is the most mature for our needs; moka is also solid).
But W-TinyLFU isn't enough alone — some pages should literally never be evicted while their files are active. That's where pinning comes in.
Two-tier pool structure:
Pinned region: small (~1-5% of pool). Holds explicitly-pinned pages with no eviction. Used for:

File footers (one per active file)
FST root nodes (one per active text-indexed column per file)
HNSW entry points and upper-layer pages (top 1-2 layers — these are tiny but hit on every search)
Translation tables (small, accessed via every cross-section reference)

Pinning is an explicit API: pin(file_id, page_id, priority). When a file is opened, the file manager pins its essential pages. When a file is dropped (compaction or close), pins are released.
Dynamic region: everything else (95-99% of pool). W-TinyLFU eviction.
The pinned region's footprint: roughly 200KB per file (footer 100KB, FST roots 50KB, HNSW upper-layer pages 50KB, translation table varies). For 1000 active files, that's 200MB pinned. For a 32GB pool that's 0.6% — well within budget.
Decision 4: Concurrency model
Every query thread touches the buffer pool. Contention here is fatal.
Options:

Global lock: rejected, dies above 4 cores
Sharded pool with per-shard locks: simple, scales well, what RocksDB does
Fully lock-free with atomic operations: complex, marginal gains

Sharded pool, 64 shards by default. Page key (file_id, page_id) hashes to a shard; the shard has its own lock, its own W-TinyLFU instance, its own LRU window. Pinned region is shared and uses fine-grained locking (it's mostly read-only after warmup).
Within a shard:

Read access: brief read-lock acquired, page handle returned with reference count, read-lock released
Eviction: write-lock acquired only when capacity is exceeded
Page contents are read-only (immutable files), so multiple readers don't conflict on data — only on cache metadata

Reference counting on pages prevents eviction during use. A page being read holds a refcount; eviction skips pages with non-zero refcounts and looks for the next candidate.
64 shards is overkill for small systems and the right number for big ones. Configurable.
Decision 5: Prefetching strategy
Direct I/O means we own read-ahead. The kernel won't do it for us.
Three useful prefetch patterns:
Sequential column scans. When reading page K of a column chunk during a scan, prefetch pages K+1, K+2, K+3 asynchronously. The query layer indicates "this is a sequential scan, prefetch aggressively." For non-sequential access, don't prefetch.
File-open warming. When a file is opened (catalog opens it, or a query needs its first page), eagerly load: footer, all translation tables, FST roots, HNSW entry-point pages. These will be needed soon; load them in parallel via io_uring batched submission. File-open becomes one batched async I/O round-trip instead of many sequential reads.
HNSW: no automatic prefetch. HNSW traversal is fundamentally random — there's no predictable next page from looking at the current one. Prefetching neighbors is wasteful (we won't visit most of them). The right optimization here is the BFS reordering we did at flush time, which gives locality so adjacent pages tend to be on adjacent disk locations. Don't add explicit prefetch.
Posting list scans: bounded prefetch. When decoding a posting list, prefetch the next block (we'll need it next). One-block-ahead is enough; don't go further.
This is the bare minimum that captures the main wins. We can add more later if profiling shows specific patterns.
Decision 6: Memory layout — huge pages and NUMA
For pools above 16GB, huge pages (2MB) reduce TLB misses. The Linux kernel can be configured to provide huge pages via hugetlbfs or transparent huge pages. We'd want our pool memory backed by hugepages when available.
For multi-socket NUMA systems, ideally the pool is split per-NUMA-node and queries are scheduled to threads on the same node as their primary data. This is significant work and probably v2. For v1, allocate the pool on whichever node we boot on and accept some cross-node traffic.
The implementation move: define the pool's memory as an mmap-backed region with MAP_HUGETLB if available, fall back to regular allocation. Page slots come from this region.
Decision 7: Cache key structure
The cache key is (file_id, page_id). Both are integers — file_id is a u64 catalog identifier, page_id is a u32 within-file offset (page number, not byte offset).
For composability we use a single u96 (or pack into u128 for alignment). Hash function: FxHash or xxHash — both fast and good enough for cache keys.
Worth noting: when a file is dropped (compaction completed, old file no longer needed), we need to invalidate all its cache entries. Cheapest way: maintain a per-file list of cached page IDs, walk it on file drop. Alternative: lazy invalidation, file IDs include a generation number, mismatched generation means cache miss. I'd go lazy — simpler and avoids the bookkeeping.
Decision 8: Sizing recommendations
For an N GB machine, target pool size:

Reserve 4-8GB for OS, networking, connection overhead
Reserve 1-4GB per concurrent heavy query for execution memory (sorts, hash builds)
Allocate ~60-70% of remaining to buffer pool

So on a 64GB box: 4GB OS, ~8GB query memory for 4 concurrent heavy queries, ~52GB available, ~36GB buffer pool. Configurable; this is just the default we suggest.
Critical: respect the budget. If the OS is under memory pressure, that's an operator-misconfiguration problem, not the pool's job to solve. Don't dynamically shrink. Predictable memory usage is more valuable than adaptive cleverness.
Decision 9: Cold-start warmup
After process restart, the pool is empty. For a few minutes, every query is cold-cache slow. Production systems sometimes serialize the working set to disk and reload at startup.
For v1: skip warmup. After restart, performance ramps as the cache fills. Operators see this in their metrics.
For v2: snapshot the W-TinyLFU "main cache" key list to disk every minute. On startup, asynchronously load those pages into the cache before opening for queries (or open and warm in parallel). This recovers most of the hit rate in seconds.
What's novel vs. what we're stealing
Stolen:

W-TinyLFU policy: from Caffeine
Sharded pool with per-shard locks: from RocksDB
Variable-size byte-accounted entries: from RocksDB
Direct I/O bypass: from RocksDB, ClickHouse
Reference counting for eviction safety: textbook
io_uring for async I/O: modern Linux best practice

Original to us:

Two-tier structure (pinned region + dynamic) with explicit pinning of footers, FST roots, HNSW entry points
File-open warming with batched io_uring submissions of "always needed" pages
Type-aware priority hints (FST > posting list block > sequential column scan page)
Generation-based lazy file invalidation that integrates with our LSM file lifecycle

Most of these are 80%-stolen-20%-novel. That's appropriate — the buffer pool is well-trodden ground. We're being deliberate about the few places our workload is different.
The full architecture, summarized
BufferPool {
  shards: [Shard; 64],
  pinned_region: PinnedRegion,
  total_budget_bytes: u64,
  
  io_subsystem: IOUring,
  prefetch_coordinator: Prefetcher,
  file_invalidation_generation: AtomicU32 per file,
}

Shard {
  policy: WTinyLFU,
  entries: HashMap<(FileId, PageId), CacheEntry>,
  current_bytes: u64,
  budget_bytes: u64,    // total_budget / 64
  lock: RwLock,
}

CacheEntry {
  data: Arc<[u8]>,
  size: u32,
  refcount: AtomicU32,
  generation: u32,      // for lazy invalidation
}

PinnedRegion {
  pages: HashMap<(FileId, PageId), CacheEntry>,
  per_file_pins: HashMap<FileId, Vec<PageId>>,
  lock: RwLock,
}
Query interface:

get(file_id, page_id) -> PageHandle — returns cached or loads, refcounted
get_async(file_id, page_id) -> Future<PageHandle> — async version using io_uring
prefetch(file_id, page_ids: &[PageId]) — async prefetch, returns immediately
pin(file_id, page_id, priority) — explicit pin
unpin(file_id, page_id) — release pin
invalidate_file(file_id) — increments generation, lazy invalidation

Honest tradeoffs
Direct I/O complexity. We're doing alignment, batching, async submission ourselves. This is real code that has to be correct under concurrent load. Bugs here cause correctness issues (corrupted reads from misaligned I/O) or performance issues (synchronous fallbacks when io_uring submissions fill up). The Rust ecosystem is good enough now (tokio-uring, glommio, monoio) that this is manageable but not trivial.
W-TinyLFU memory overhead. The frequency sketch is ~10 bytes per cached page. For a 36GB pool with 4KB average page size, that's ~9M entries × 10 bytes = 90MB of sketch memory. Acceptable but real. We size the sketch appropriately.
Pinned region growth with file count. Each active file contributes ~200KB to the pinned region. For 10,000 active files, that's 2GB pinned. For systems with many small files this becomes significant. Mitigation: compaction reduces file count over time. If a workload has pathologically many tiny files, that's a compaction tuning problem, not a buffer pool problem.
Cold start. I deferred warmup to v2. For production systems with strict uptime SLAs this is a real gap. Operators on those workloads will need to pre-warm via synthetic query traffic at startup before exposing the system to real traffic.
NUMA punt. Single-NUMA-node assumption for v1 leaves performance on the table for big servers. For 2-socket machines (most production servers today) we accept 10-30% performance loss vs. a NUMA-aware pool. Real but not catastrophic.
Three places to push on
W-TinyLFU vs. simpler LRU. I argued strongly for W-TinyLFU. The counter: LRU is dead simple, well-understood, easy to debug. W-TinyLFU is a black box from operations' perspective — when hit rates are low, why? Sketch decay? Window cache size? Reasonable to ship LRU in v0.1 (it's adequate), upgrade to W-TinyLFU in v0.2 when we have telemetry to know what's actually happening. My recommendation stands but the simpler path is defensible.
Pinning explicitly vs. trusting the policy. I argued for explicit pinning of footers/FST roots/HNSW entry points. The counter: if W-TinyLFU is really as good as advertised, those pages will be kept hot by their access frequency without manual intervention. Explicit pinning is added complexity that might be redundant. My answer: pinning is cheap to implement and gives us guarantees the statistical policy can't provide (in adversarial workloads, even W-TinyLFU can be wrong). Cheap insurance.
Direct I/O vs. buffered. This is the biggest one. Direct I/O is more code, more responsibility, more chances for bugs. Buffered I/O with our cache on top would work, with the cost of double-caching and OS unpredictability. For v1 specifically, buffered I/O would ship faster. We'd lose some performance and some predictability. Reasonable to defer direct I/O to v2 if shipping speed matters more than benchmark wins. My instinct is direct I/O from day one because rebuilding the I/O stack later is painful — but I want to flag that we could justify either choice.

Locking buffer pool:

Direct I/O via io_uring on Linux (with fallback to standard async I/O on other platforms)
4KB page alignment throughout; entries variable-size with byte-level budget accounting
W-TinyLFU eviction policy via foyer or moka crate, 64 shards by default
Two-tier structure: small pinned region (footers, FST roots, HNSW entry points, translation tables) + dynamic region
Reference counting on pages to prevent eviction during use
File-open warming: batched io_uring loads of "always needed" pages
Sequential prefetch for column scans (3 pages ahead); one-block prefetch for posting list scans; no automatic HNSW prefetch
Generation-based lazy file invalidation
Hugepages support when available; NUMA-awareness deferred to v2
Cold-start warmup deferred to v2
Default pool size 60-70% of available RAM after OS and query-memory reservations, configurable
No dynamic shrinking; budget is honored strictly


