I'm going to organize this around four questions, because the catalog answers four distinct things and conflating them leads to confusion:

What does the user declare? (DDL: CREATE TABLE, schema syntax)
What does the system track? (metadata: tables, columns, files, transactions)
How is it stored? (persistence: the catalog itself is a database problem)
How does it evolve? (schema changes, online migration)

Let me work through each.
Question 1: What does the user declare?
The DDL is the public face of the system. It's the API everyone learns first. Getting it right matters more than most internal decisions because it's how we communicate what we are.
The principle I want to commit to: declare intent, not implementation. The user says "this column has a vector embedding generated from this text using this model"; we figure out the rest. They shouldn't have to know about HNSW parameters or quantization schemes unless they want to tune.
Concrete syntax proposal:
sqlCREATE TABLE documents (
    id              UUID PRIMARY KEY,
    title           TEXT,
    body            TEXT INDEX (positions = true),
    body_embedding  VECTOR(1536) EMBED FROM body USING 'openai/text-embedding-3-small',
    author          ROW REF users,
    cites           ROW REF documents [],
    created_at      TIMESTAMP DEFAULT now(),
    
    INDEX btree (created_at),
    INDEX vector (body_embedding) WITH (m = 16, ef_construction = 200),
    INDEX edge (author, cites) WITH (reverse = true)
) WITH (
    embedding_concurrency = 4,
    primary_key_clustering = true
);
Several things to notice:
VECTOR(1536) as a first-class type. Not FLOAT[] or BYTEA or JSONB. The system knows it's a vector and treats it accordingly — alignment, SIMD distance, HNSW indexing eligibility.
EMBED FROM body USING 'model_id': this is the AI-native schema feature. The vector column is derived from another column via a named model. Inserts and updates to body trigger embedding generation. The system owns the embedding lifecycle. We'll come back to this.
ROW REF users: a typed reference to another table's row. This is the edge column. Single-valued by default; [] makes it a collection (multi-edge). At the storage level, this is an edge column with the schema we already designed.
INDEX vector ... WITH (...): declarative indexes with optional parameters. Most users don't specify parameters; defaults are sensible. Power users tune.
INDEX edge (author, cites) WITH (reverse = true): declares which edge columns get reverse indexes (the 2x storage cost we discussed). Default is reverse = true for any column declared as ROW REF, but tunable.
Table-level options like embedding_concurrency control system-wide behaviors specific to this table.
What I'm not doing in the DDL:

No HNSW page size, no quantization scheme exposure (defaults; tunable via session variables for power users)
No memtable size at table level (system-wide)
No file format version (managed by the engine)
No tier configuration (system-wide)

The DDL is for user intent, not implementation details. Implementation details live in configuration files and admin commands.
On column types
The type system is a real design question. I'd propose:
Scalar types: BOOLEAN, INT8/16/32/64, FLOAT32/64, DECIMAL(p, s), TEXT, BLOB, TIMESTAMP, UUID, DATE, INTERVAL. Standard set.
Compound types: ARRAY[T] (homogeneous list), STRUCT{...} (named fields), MAP<K,V>. These exist in Arrow already and we get them mostly for free.
Specialized types: VECTOR(dim), SPARSE_VECTOR(dim) — distinct from ARRAY[FLOAT] because they enable specific indexing.
Reference type: ROW REF table_name — typed reference, stored as edge column.
JSON: JSONB column type for semi-structured. Common ask. Implementation: store as compressed CBOR or BSON; expose via JSON path expressions in queries. This is a v2 feature; v1 just stores it as a blob with no special operators.
On primary keys and row IDs
Every row needs a globally unique identifier. Two designs:
User-declared primary key + system-assigned row ID. User says PRIMARY KEY (id). System maintains a separate internal row ID for storage. PK is a uniqueness constraint enforced via B-tree.
Primary key IS the row ID. User-declared PK directly identifies the row in storage.
We go with the first design. Reasoning: PKs are often composite, or strings (UUIDs), or values the user wants to update (rare but possible). Internal row IDs are fixed 64-bit integers, never change, perfect for edge references and HNSW page references. Decoupling these gives us flexibility without exposing internal IDs to users.
Internal row IDs are (file_id: u64, local_offset: u32) for files, and (memtable_id: u64, local_offset: u32) for memtable rows. The catalog tracks file_id → file metadata.
When an edge column references another row, what's stored? Not the user's PK (could be long, could change) but the internal row ID. We resolve user PKs to internal row IDs at insert time via the target table's PK index.
This means inserts that reference foreign rows have a small lookup cost. Acceptable.
Question 2: What does the system track?
The catalog's runtime data model. Let me enumerate the objects.
Tables. Name, columns (with full type info), schema fingerprint, indexes declared, table options, creation/modification timestamps, owner. Each table has a stable internal table_id (u64).
Columns. Name, type, position, default value, constraints, indexing flags, EMBED FROM linkage if applicable, column_id (u32 within table, unique forever — never reused even if column is dropped).
Indexes. Type (btree, vector, text, edge), columns covered, parameters, status (building, active, dropping). Each index has its own index_id.
Files. file_id, table_id, tier level, LSN range, file path on disk, size, state (active, compacting, deleting). The catalog is the source of truth for which files exist and belong to which table.
Schemas. The full schema definition for each table, versioned. Schema_version increments on every schema change. Files reference a schema_version; the catalog can reconstruct any historical schema.
Models. For EMBED FROM columns, the embedding model registry. Model name, provider, dimension, API endpoint or local path, version, status. We'll come back to this.
Transactions. Active transaction list, their snapshot LSNs, used for MVCC garbage collection. The min snapshot LSN is what compaction needs.
Statistics. Per-table, per-column statistics for the cost-based optimizer — row counts, distinct value estimates, histograms, vector distribution centroids. Updated by compaction (compactions naturally produce these as they pass over data).
Settings. System-wide and per-table configuration — compaction thresholds, embedding concurrency, buffer pool tunables.
Audit log. Schema changes, model registrations, compaction events. For debuggability and compliance.
These objects have relationships: tables have columns, files belong to tables, files cover LSN ranges, transactions have snapshots, etc. The catalog enforces these.
Question 3: How is the catalog stored?
This is the part where catalogs get architecturally interesting. The catalog itself is a database. Where does it live?
Three approaches:
Catalog in our own engine. The system stores catalog data in tables of itself. Bootstrapping problem (how do you read catalog before catalog is loaded?), but recursively elegant.
Catalog in an embedded KV store (RocksDB, sled, redb). Catalog is a separate small database, our engine reads it at startup. Simple, robust, well-understood.
Catalog in a flat file (JSON/FlatBuffers). Tiny, simple, works for v1.
I'd propose catalog in redb (a Rust-native embedded KV store with ACID transactions). Reasoning:

Battle-tested, well-maintained, MIT-licensed
Real ACID transactions — our catalog updates need transactional semantics
Tiny operational footprint (single file)
Bootstrap is trivial: open redb file, read tables, done
Avoids the recursive bootstrapping headache of using our own engine for catalog
Scales to the catalog sizes we'll ever see (a system with 10K tables and 1M files has a catalog of maybe 100MB)

sled was an alternative but it's effectively unmaintained at this point. rocksdb works but is heavier and not Rust-native (JNI tax for non-Rust bindings doesn't apply since we're Rust, but the build complexity is real). redb is genuinely the right choice in 2026.
The catalog is a single redb database file (catalog.db) in the system data directory. On open, we read the necessary in-memory representations. On change, we write to redb transactionally.
On catalog caching
Catalog reads happen on every query (to resolve table/column names, look up indexes, get statistics). They must be fast. We cache the catalog aggressively in memory:

Full table list, schema for every active table, index list — all in memory at all times
File metadata for active files — in memory (this is the largest set; for 100K files maybe 50MB of metadata)
Statistics — in memory, refreshed when compaction updates them
Settings — in memory, refreshed on change

The redb database is essentially write-through persistence; reads almost never touch it. Reads touch redb only on cold start.
Update path: catalog change writes to redb in a transaction, then invalidates/updates the in-memory cache. The in-memory cache uses RwLock for concurrent reads with occasional writes.
On catalog transactions
Catalog operations must be transactional. Some examples:

CREATE TABLE: atomic — either the table fully exists with all metadata or it doesn't
DROP INDEX: atomic — either the index is gone from catalog and execution skips it, or it's still active
Compaction completion: atomically swap input file IDs for output file IDs in the file list — no query should see a transient state where input files are deleted but output files aren't visible

We use redb's native transaction support for this. Each catalog change is a single redb transaction.
Question 4: How does it evolve?
Schema evolution is where catalogs earn their keep. The basic operations:
Add column. ALTER TABLE documents ADD COLUMN summary TEXT;

Catalog updates: schema_version increments, new column added to column list
Existing files: don't have this column; reads return NULL/default for it
New writes: include the column
New files: have the column in their column chunks
Storage cost: ~zero — existing files are untouched

This is online and free. The Iceberg/Delta pattern works perfectly with our footer-carries-schema design.
Drop column. ALTER TABLE documents DROP COLUMN summary;

Catalog updates: column marked as dropped in catalog, but column_id is not reused
Existing files: still have the column data, but it's invisible to queries
Storage reclamation: lazy — old data sits in files until compaction rewrites them
For urgent cases (compliance): FORCE REWRITE TABLE documents triggers immediate rewrite of all files

The column_id never being reused is important. If you drop column 7 and add a new column, the new column gets column_id 8. Old files have column 7 data; we can ignore it. If column_ids were reused, old files' column 7 data would be misinterpreted.
Add index. CREATE INDEX ... ON ...

Catalog: new index entry, status=building
Background job: scans existing files, builds the index per-file, atomically marks files as having the index
During building: queries that could use the new index don't yet; queries proceed without it
Completion: status=active, index now used by query planner
Failure: index marked failed; can be retried

The per-file index addition pattern: the file's index section is stored outside the file in some cases, since we declared files as immutable. Two options:
Option A: Build the new index in a separate file (file_abc.idx_summary_btree) that's logically attached to the original file. Catalog tracks the side-file.
Option B: Rewrite the file to include the new index. More work but cleaner — files remain self-contained.
I'd go with Option B for v1. Side-files are operational complexity (more files to manage, more partial-failure states). Rewriting is just a special case of compaction with N=1 input. It's slower but cleaner.
Drop index. Catalog updates immediately. Files retain the index data until compaction; queries simply ignore the now-dropped index. Lazy reclamation again.
Change column type. The hard one. Two cases:

Widening (INT32 → INT64, FLOAT32 → FLOAT64): allowed online. Old files have narrow data; readers cast on read. No file rewrite needed.
Narrowing or incompatible (INT64 → INT32, TEXT → INT): rejected unless FORCE specified, which triggers full table rewrite.

Change embedding model. This is the genuinely novel schema evolution problem. ALTER COLUMN body_embedding SET MODEL 'openai/text-embedding-3-large'. We'll discuss the full lifecycle in the embedding section but at the catalog level:

Catalog: new model linked to the column, old model retained until migration completes
All future writes use the new model
Background migration: re-embed existing data with new model
During migration: dual-read pattern (search both old and new embedding indexes, merge results)
Completion: old model unlinked, old embedding column data eligible for cleanup

This requires the catalog to support a column having multiple active embedding bindings simultaneously. Schema evolution at its most interesting.
The model registry — a sub-system within the catalog
Embedding models are first-class catalog objects. Each registered model has:

model_id (u64, internal)
name ('openai/text-embedding-3-small')
provider type (http_api, local_onnx, local_pytorch, etc.)
endpoint config (URL, API key reference, local path)
output dimension (must match VECTOR(dim) of bound columns)
max input length
pricing/rate-limit metadata
status (active, deprecated)

Models are registered via CREATE MODEL DDL:
sqlCREATE MODEL openai_small AS HTTP_API (
    endpoint = 'https://api.openai.com/v1/embeddings',
    model = 'text-embedding-3-small',
    dimension = 1536,
    api_key = SECRET('openai_key')
);
The SECRET() reference points to a secrets store — we don't put API keys in the catalog directly. Catalog stores the reference; the actual key is resolved at use time from a secrets backend (env var, AWS Secrets Manager, HashiCorp Vault, file).
This decoupling matters for security and ops.
The model registry is the catalog's most "AI-native" feature. It's where we differentiate from generic databases — embedding is a first-class operation, not a layer above.
Catalog API
What does code interact with? The catalog exposes a Rust API:
rust// Reads — fast, in-memory
catalog.get_table(table_name: &str) -> Option<TableMetadata>
catalog.get_files_for_table(table_id) -> Vec<FileMetadata>
catalog.get_indexes_for_table(table_id) -> Vec<IndexMetadata>
catalog.get_statistics(table_id, column_id) -> ColumnStatistics

// Writes — transactional
catalog.create_table(stmt: CreateTableStatement) -> Result<TableMetadata>
catalog.alter_table(stmt: AlterTableStatement) -> Result<()>
catalog.register_file(file: FileMetadata) -> Result<()>
catalog.swap_files(inputs: &[FileId], outputs: Vec<FileMetadata>) -> Result<()>

// Transactions
catalog.begin_txn() -> CatalogTxn
txn.create_table(...).register_file(...).commit() -> Result<()>
Catalog operations are transactional but coarse-grained. Most catalog updates are infrequent (schema changes are rare); the high-frequency operation is file registration during compaction, which uses a streamlined fast path.
Question I anticipate: distributed catalog?
For a single-node v1, the catalog lives in the local redb file. For eventually-distributed v2, the catalog becomes a distributed object — possibly via Raft over the catalog state, possibly delegated to an external system like FoundationDB.
The design lets us swap implementations later. The catalog API is what code interacts with; the redb backing is hidden behind it. v2 swaps the backing for a distributed implementation; the API stays the same.
This isn't free — there are subtle assumptions (e.g., "catalog reads are sub-microsecond") that change when the catalog becomes distributed. But the broad shape works.
Honest tradeoffs
redb dependency. We've added a runtime dependency on redb. It's well-maintained and stable but it's another moving part. If redb has bugs, we have bugs. Mitigation: the catalog is small enough that we could re-implement on raw files in a week if needed. Acceptable.
All-in-memory caching. The catalog is fully in memory. For systems with millions of files this matters. For 100K files (a system with ~25TB at our typical file size of 256MB) it's ~50MB of metadata — fine. For 10M files it would be 5GB and we'd need to reconsider. We aren't there now; deferred.
Schema evolution complexity. Online ADD COLUMN is trivial. Online DROP COLUMN is trivial (lazy). Online CHANGE TYPE is constrained. Online ADD INDEX requires background work. CHANGE EMBEDDING MODEL requires dual-read. Each gets more complex. We ship v1 with ADD, DROP, ADD/DROP INDEX, type widening, model migration. Incompatible changes require explicit table rewrite.
Secrets storage. I punted on actually implementing secrets — just specified the indirection point. Real systems integrate with secrets managers; we'll need to do that work but it's bounded.
Transactionality limits. Schema changes are transactional but expensive transactions (rebuilding indexes) aren't atomic — they're long-running with intermediate states. Users see "building" status. This is the same as Postgres' CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY model.
Three places to push on
Catalog in redb vs. flat file. I argued redb. The flat-file alternative is simpler — write a FlatBuffers catalog to disk, load on startup, rewrite the whole thing on every change. Works fine for catalogs under 100MB. The argument for redb is ACID transactions for fine-grained updates (file registration during compaction). The argument for flat file is: it's 10x less code and we control everything. For v1 specifically, flat file might actually be the right call given simplicity matters. My instinct says redb but I want to flag the alternative.
Internal row IDs vs. PK-as-row-ID. I argued for internal row IDs. The counter: it adds a layer of indirection that every edge insert pays for. If PKs were the row ID, edge inserts wouldn't need to resolve PK → row ID; they'd just store the PK. Cost: edges become variable-size (PKs can be strings), and PK updates (rare but possible) cascade through edge updates. I think internal row IDs are correct but the tradeoff isn't trivial.
Model registry as catalog object vs. external. I made models first-class catalog objects. Alternative: models are external configuration, the column DDL just references a model name and the system resolves at query time from external config. Pros of external: cleaner separation, models can be reused across multiple databases. Pros of catalog-resident: schema evolution can change models atomically, catalog audit log records model changes. I lean catalog-resident; both work.

Locking catalog:

DDL declares intent: VECTOR types, EMBED FROM columns, ROW REF for edges, declarative INDEX with optional WITH params
Internal row IDs distinct from user PKs; edge columns store internal row IDs
Catalog persisted in redb, fully cached in memory after open
Catalog tracks: tables, columns, indexes, files, schemas (versioned), models, transactions, statistics, settings, audit log
Schema evolution: ADD COLUMN free, DROP COLUMN lazy, ADD INDEX background-build, type widening allowed, narrowing requires explicit rewrite, model change triggers dual-read migration
column_ids and schema_versions never reused
Model registry as first-class catalog objects; secrets via indirection to external secrets store
File rewrite for index addition (Option B, no side-files)
Per-file schema fingerprints for fast equality
API exposes typed Rust interface; backing implementation hidden for future distribution


