The five layers of a query lifecycle
Before any specifics, let me name the layers a query traverses so we know what we're designing:

Parser: text → AST
Logical planner: AST → logical plan (algebraic, no execution details)
Logical optimizer: logical plan → better logical plan (rewrites, predicate pushdown)
Physical planner: logical plan → physical plan (operator choices, index selection)
Executor: physical plan → results

This turn covers layers 2 and 4: what the logical plan looks like, and what physical operators exist. Layer 3 (the optimizer) and the cost model come next turn — they're inseparable.
On the query language
Quick note before diving in. We need to pick a surface language. Three options:
SQL with extensions. The boring choice. PostgreSQL-style SQL plus our additions for vector/text/graph. Pros: every engineer knows SQL, tooling ecosystem (BI tools, ORMs), familiar mental model. Cons: SQL is verbose for graph traversal, vector queries require awkward function calls.
A new query language. Custom syntax designed for our four-modal world. Pros: clean, expressive for our use case. Cons: nobody knows it, no tooling, adoption tax.
GraphQL-style or natural-language frontend. Higher-level than SQL. Cons: maps to SQL anyway under the hood, just adds a layer.
SQL with extensions for v1. Reasoning: the query language is the surface area users interact with most. Reinventing it is the single biggest adoption barrier we could create. SQL is ugly for some of our cases but it's the universal language of databases, and every BI/ORM/tool in the world speaks it. We extend it where needed (vector operators, graph path syntax) but stay recognizably SQL.
Examples of the syntax extensions:
sql-- Vector similarity in WHERE clause
SELECT id, title FROM documents
WHERE body_embedding <-> EMBED('quarterly earnings impact', 'openai/text-embedding-3-small') < 0.3
LIMIT 10;

-- Or with KNN syntax
SELECT id, title FROM documents
ORDER BY body_embedding <-> EMBED('quarterly earnings impact') ASC
LIMIT 10;

-- Text matching
SELECT id, title FROM documents
WHERE body MATCH 'derivatives AND volatility';

-- Graph traversal (Cypher-inspired path syntax embedded in SQL)
SELECT u.name FROM users u
WHERE EXISTS (PATH (u) -[:follows*1..3]-> (target) WHERE target.id = $user_id);

-- Hybrid: vector + text + graph + scalar
SELECT d.id, d.title,
       hybrid_score(
           text_score(d.body MATCH 'derivatives'),
           vector_distance(d.body_embedding <-> EMBED('hedging strategies'))
       ) AS score
FROM documents d
WHERE d.created_at > now() - INTERVAL '30 days'
  AND EXISTS (PATH (d) -[:authored_by]-> (a) WHERE a.id = $author_id)
ORDER BY score DESC
LIMIT 20;
The <-> operator is distance (lower = closer). MATCH is the text predicate. PATH ...  is the graph traversal subexpression. EMBED(text, model?) is the runtime embedding function. hybrid_score(...) is a built-in scoring fusion.
This is recognizably SQL. Anyone who knows Postgres pgvector reads this immediately. The graph syntax is the only genuinely new thing, and it's bounded.
OK, moving to the actual plan structures.
The logical plan IR
A logical plan is an algebraic expression — a tree of operators describing what to compute, not how. Each operator takes input relations and produces an output relation.
The base operators are textbook:

Scan(table, projection, predicate): read from a table, optionally projecting columns and filtering rows
Filter(input, predicate): filter rows by a boolean expression
Project(input, expressions): compute new columns
Join(left, right, condition, type): relational join
Aggregate(input, group_by, aggregates): GROUP BY + aggregate functions
Sort(input, keys): sort by keys
Limit(input, n, offset): limit/offset
Union(inputs): combine multiple inputs

These are standard. The interesting additions for us:
VectorSearch(input, column, query_vector, k, distance_metric): find the k nearest neighbors in input based on column distance to query_vector. This is a first-class operator, not a special function. Its input might be a scan, a filter, anything — and the optimizer can reorder it relative to other operators.
TextMatch(input, column, query_expression): filter rows where column matches the text query. First-class operator.
PathTraversal(input, pattern, bindings): traverse the graph along a path pattern, producing rows that satisfy the pattern. First-class operator with its own algebra.
HybridRank(inputs, scoring_function, k): take multiple ranked input streams and fuse them into a single top-k ranked output. First-class operator.
The critical design choice: these are operators, not functions buried in WHERE clauses. This is what lets the optimizer reason about them — reorder them, push them down, pick indexes for them.
Compare to pgvector, where vector similarity is a function inside ORDER BY. The Postgres planner can't reason about it as an operator — it just sees a function it has to compute per row. That's why pgvector queries with filters are slow: the planner can't decide whether to filter-then-vector-search or vector-search-then-filter.
By lifting these to first-class operators, our planner can make those decisions explicitly.
The shape of a logical plan
Example: the hybrid query above. Its logical plan, post-parsing, looks roughly like:
Limit(20)
  └── Sort(score DESC)
       └── Project(id, title, hybrid_score(...) AS score)
            └── PathTraversal(d -[:authored_by]-> a, predicate: a.id = $author_id)
                 └── Filter(created_at > now() - 30 days)
                      └── HybridRank(
                            TextMatch(body MATCH 'derivatives'),
                            VectorSearch(body_embedding, EMBED('hedging strategies'), k=∞)
                          )
                          └── Scan(documents)
This is the naive logical plan straight from parsing. It's not the plan we'd execute — the optimizer will rewrite it. But it shows the shape.
Notice: the HybridRank takes raw text and vector results before any filtering or path traversal. That's obviously wrong (we'd score and rank everything in the table before filtering to 20). The optimizer's job is to fix this.
Logical plan invariants
A few rules the logical plan must obey, because the optimizer relies on them:
Type-correct. Every operator's output schema is well-defined given its inputs. We can verify the plan typechecks before optimization.
Acyclic. It's a tree (or DAG with shared subexpressions in advanced cases). No cycles.
Pure (no side effects). Logical operators are mathematical functions — same inputs, same outputs. This is what allows the optimizer to reorder them.
Statistics-annotated. Each operator carries estimated cardinality (number of output rows) and cost. Initially these are unknown; the optimizer fills them in. We'll cover cost estimation next turn.
The physical plan IR
The physical plan is where we choose how to execute. For each logical operator, the physical planner picks a specific physical operator that knows how to actually compute it.
A logical Scan(documents) becomes one of:

SequentialScan(documents): read every page
IndexScan(documents, btree_idx_created_at, range): use a B-tree
BitmapScan(documents, bitmap_indexes...): combine multiple bitmap indexes

A logical VectorSearch becomes one of:

HNSWSearch(file_id, column, query, k, ef_search): traverse HNSW in each file
FlatScan(input, column, query, k): brute-force distance over all rows in input

A logical TextMatch becomes one of:

InvertedIndexLookup(file_id, column, term): term dictionary + posting list
ScanFilter(input, column, regex): when no text index exists or the predicate isn't index-supportable

A logical PathTraversal becomes one of:

ForwardWalk(source_filter, edge_type, depth): use forward CSR
ReverseWalk(target_filter, edge_type, depth): use reverse CSR with bloom filter pruning
BidirectionalSearch(source_filter, target_filter, edge_type, max_depth): meet-in-the-middle

A logical Join becomes one of:

HashJoin(left, right, condition): build hash from smaller side, probe with larger
MergeJoin(left, right, condition): both sides sorted, merge
NestedLoopJoin(left, right, condition): small inputs or non-equi joins

Standard pattern: logical operator → multiple physical implementations → cost-based choice.
Physical operators specific to our system
The standard set above is augmented with operators that exploit our specific storage:
MultiFileVectorSearch(files, column, query, k): parallel HNSW search across N files, top-k merge at the end. This is the typical vector search across an LSM. Internally:

For each file, in parallel, do HNSW search returning k results
Merge the N×k results, keep top k
Apply MVCC filter (read xmin/xmax for top candidates)
Return final k

This is one operator that internally does file-parallel search. It's the workhorse of vector queries.
PredicatedVectorSearch(files, column, query, k, predicate): vector search with a correlated predicate applied during search, not after. This is the genuinely hard one. Three strategies, each is a separate physical operator:

PreFilteredVectorSearch: evaluate predicate first to get a candidate row set, then do brute-force vector distance on candidates. Right when predicate is very selective.
PostFilteredVectorSearch: do HNSW search returning more than k (say 10k), filter by predicate, return top k of survivors. Right when predicate is non-selective.
IntegratedFilteredHNSWSearch: traverse HNSW but only consider nodes whose predicate is true. Requires the HNSW to support filtered traversal. Right for medium-selectivity predicates.

The optimizer chooses among these three based on estimated predicate selectivity. This decision is the single most important optimization in vector databases under filters, and it's where most systems get it wrong.
MultiFileTextSearch(files, column, query): similar parallel pattern across files, merging posting list results.
GraphReachability(source, target_pattern, max_depth): BFS/DFS traversal with file-aware edge access.
ScoreFusion(streams, fusion_function, k): take multiple top-k streams (vector, text, scalar score) and produce top-k fused. Implementations include RRF (Reciprocal Rank Fusion), CombSUM, weighted linear, learned-to-rank.
The execution model: morsel-driven parallelism
A note that crosses both logical and physical layers. How do operators execute concurrently?
Two paradigms:
Volcano (pull-based, single-threaded). Each operator has next() → row. Execution is a chain of next() calls from the root. Simple, what every textbook teaches. Doesn't scale past one CPU.
Morsel-driven (pull-based but parallel). The TUM/HyPer model. Input data is divided into morsels (small chunks, ~100K rows). A worker thread takes a morsel, runs it through a pipeline of operators, produces output morsels. Many workers operate in parallel on different morsels. Operators that need cross-morsel state (joins, aggregates) coordinate via shared state.
We're going morsel-driven. It's the modern default for analytical systems (DuckDB, Velox, Polars all use variants). It scales to many cores cleanly. It composes well with vectorized execution (each morsel is processed in vectorized batches).
I'll go deeper on morsel mechanics in the execution engine section. For the plan, what matters: the physical plan is a DAG of operators that the executor parallelizes across cores via morsels.
Vectorized vs. tuple-at-a-time execution
Within a morsel, how do operators process data?
Tuple-at-a-time (Volcano). Each operator processes one row at a time. Slow because of per-row overhead.
Vectorized (column-at-a-time). Operators process columns of values in vectorized batches (typically 1024 or 4096 rows). SIMD-friendly. This is what every modern OLAP engine does.
We're going vectorized. Specifically, we use Apache Arrow's in-memory format as the working representation throughout execution. Operators take Arrow RecordBatches in, produce Arrow RecordBatches out. This gives us:

SIMD-friendly columnar data
Standard format that integrates with the ecosystem
Zero-copy when reading from our row-group format (which is Arrow-compatible)

The DataFusion project (Apache, Rust) is a query engine built on Arrow that we can either use directly or take heavy inspiration from. I'd propose: we build on top of DataFusion's execution framework, but with our own logical/physical operators for the four-modal stuff. DataFusion gives us:

A solid execution scheduler
Standard operators (Scan, Filter, Join, Aggregate, Sort, Limit) implemented well
Vectorized execution with Arrow
An optimizer framework we can plug into

We extend it with our novel operators (HNSWSearch, TextMatch, PathTraversal, HybridRank, etc.). This is genuinely smart — we don't reinvent the parts of query execution that are well-understood, and we focus innovation on the parts that are novel.
This is similar to how InfluxDB IOx is built on DataFusion. They proved the pattern works for a non-trivial vertical database.
Predicate pushdown — the optimizer's main weapon
We'll get to the full optimizer next turn, but one pattern is so important it needs to be named now: predicate pushdown.
A query like WHERE created_at > '2024-01-01' ORDER BY embedding <-> q LIMIT 10 has two parts:

A scalar predicate on created_at
A vector similarity on embedding

Naively executed: vector search returns top 10, then filter by date. If most rows are post-2024-01-01, this works fine. If only 1% of rows are, the vector search returned 10 results and most got filtered out — we have <1 actual result.
Correctly executed: push the date predicate down to the vector search itself. The predicate becomes part of the vector search, which now searches only matching vectors. This requires the PredicatedVectorSearch operator.
This is the canonical example of the AI-native database challenge. Pushdown of scalar predicates into vector search is the optimization that most systems fail at. Filtered ANN search is its own research area (Filtered-DiskANN, ACORN, etc.).
The optimizer needs cardinality estimates to decide. If the predicate is highly selective (say <1% selectivity), use PreFilteredVectorSearch (brute-force the candidates). If it's non-selective (>50%), use PostFilteredVectorSearch (search then filter). In between, use IntegratedFilteredHNSWSearch (search with filter callback during traversal).
This is one operator decision among hundreds, but it's representative: every cross-modal decision in the planner has the same shape — selectivity-driven choice among physical implementations.
Plan representation in code
Concretely, both logical and physical plans are Rust enums with operator-specific data:
rustenum LogicalPlan {
    Scan(ScanNode),
    Filter(Box<LogicalPlan>, Expression),
    Project(Box<LogicalPlan>, Vec<Expression>),
    Join(Box<LogicalPlan>, Box<LogicalPlan>, JoinCondition, JoinType),
    Aggregate(Box<LogicalPlan>, Vec<Expression>, Vec<AggregateExpression>),
    Sort(Box<LogicalPlan>, Vec<SortKey>),
    Limit(Box<LogicalPlan>, usize, Option<usize>),
    Union(Vec<LogicalPlan>),
    VectorSearch(Box<LogicalPlan>, VectorSearchSpec),
    TextMatch(Box<LogicalPlan>, TextMatchSpec),
    PathTraversal(Box<LogicalPlan>, PathSpec),
    HybridRank(Vec<LogicalPlan>, ScoringFunction, usize),
}

enum PhysicalPlan {
    SequentialScan(...),
    IndexScan(...),
    HashJoin(...),
    MergeJoin(...),
    MultiFileVectorSearch(...),
    PreFilteredVectorSearch(...),
    PostFilteredVectorSearch(...),
    IntegratedFilteredHNSWSearch(...),
    MultiFileTextSearch(...),
    ForwardWalk(...),
    ReverseWalk(...),
    BidirectionalSearch(...),
    ScoreFusion(...),
    // ... and more
}
Each variant carries the operator's metadata. Estimated cardinality, cost, child plans. The optimizer transforms these.
Honest tradeoffs
SQL as the surface language. SQL has limitations for our use case — graph syntax is ugly, hybrid scoring is awkward, the impedance mismatch with vectors as first-class is real. Specialized graph query languages (Cypher, Gremlin) are cleaner for graph traversal. Some teams might prefer a fluent Rust/Python API. We're picking SQL because adoption matters more than syntactic elegance, but it's a real cost.
DataFusion dependency. Building on DataFusion is leverage but also constraint. We inherit its architecture, its bugs, its release cycle. Some optimizations we want might require modifying upstream. The alternative is building our own execution framework, which is months of work and gives us nothing customers care about. The trade is correct but not free.
First-class hybrid operators vs. functions. I made VectorSearch and TextMatch first-class operators. The alternative (functions in WHERE) is simpler but defeats the optimizer. Reasonable people argue both ways; I think the optimizer benefit dominates for our use case.
Three physical operators for filtered vector search. Having three distinct operators (Pre/Post/Integrated) adds optimizer complexity. The alternative is one operator with a strategy parameter. Functionally equivalent; the multi-operator design makes the cost model clearer and the decision points explicit. Aesthetics call.
Morsel-driven + Arrow + DataFusion. I committed to a specific execution stack. The alternative is custom execution. The committed stack is leverage but locks us into specific representations. Worth it for the time-to-market and ecosystem benefits.
Three places to push on
SQL syntax vs. a domain-specific language. I committed to SQL. If you imagine our users as primarily AI engineers building agentic apps rather than analysts running BI tools, a Python-fluent DSL might actually be better. Lance and many vector databases pursue this. I lean SQL because it scales to non-AI use cases too, but for our likely v1 customer base, SQL might be unnecessary baggage.
DataFusion-based vs. independent execution. I argued for building on DataFusion. The independent path takes 6-12 months longer but gives us full control. For v1 specifically, the time savings of DataFusion dominate. By v3 it might be limiting and we'd consider forking or replacing. Open to disagreement.
Logical vs. physical separation rigor. I described them as distinct layers. Some systems blur this (PostgreSQL has a fuzzy boundary). The rigorous separation pays off in optimizer cleanliness but costs in implementation. I think it's worth it; reasonable to disagree for v1.

Locking the parts of query plan we've covered:

SQL with extensions as the query language; vector (<->), text (MATCH), graph (PATH), and hybrid scoring as syntactic extensions
Logical plan IR with standard operators plus first-class VectorSearch, TextMatch, PathTraversal, HybridRank
Physical plan IR with multiple implementations per logical operator
Three physical operators for filtered vector search: Pre-filtered, Post-filtered, Integrated-filtered HNSW
Morsel-driven parallelism for execution
Vectorized columnar execution using Apache Arrow as the in-memory format
Build on DataFusion for the execution framework; extend with our novel operators
Predicate pushdown as the optimizer's primary technique; selectivity-driven physical operator choice
Plans represented as Rust enums; each variant carries metadata for cost-based optimization


That's the structural foundation. Next turn covers the parts that actually make the system smart: the cost model and the optimizer, including the genuinely novel work on estimating vector search cost under filters, hybrid scoring fusion strategies, and the rule-based + cost-based optimizer architecture.
