What "Python-fluent DSL" actually means
It's not a new language. It's a Python library where you compose queries with method chains, and under the hood it builds the same logical plan we just designed. SQL is a string you parse; a DSL is method calls that construct the plan directly.
The lineage here is real: Pandas, Polars, PySpark, SQLAlchemy Core, Ibis. All of them are "query languages" in Python that compile to a backend. The most relevant for us are Polars and Ibis, because they're modern (post-2020), they target columnar execution, and they handle the impedance mismatch between Python and a query engine cleanly.
Let me show you what our four-modal queries would look like in each style. I'll write the same query — the hybrid one from last turn — three ways.
The SQL version (for reference)
sqlSELECT d.id, d.title,
       hybrid_score(
           text_score(d.body MATCH 'derivatives'),
           vector_distance(d.body_embedding <-> EMBED('hedging strategies'))
       ) AS score
FROM documents d
WHERE d.created_at > now() - INTERVAL '30 days'
  AND EXISTS (PATH (d) -[:authored_by]-> (a) WHERE a.id = :author_id)
ORDER BY score DESC
LIMIT 20;
A Polars-style DSL version
pythonimport ll

docs = ll.table("documents")

result = (
    docs
    .filter(docs.created_at > ll.now() - ll.days(30))
    .filter(docs.path("authored_by").to(author_id).exists())
    .hybrid(
        text=docs.body.match("derivatives"),
        vector=docs.body_embedding.near(
            ll.embed("hedging strategies")
        ),
    )
    .select("id", "title", score="score")
    .sort("score", descending=True)
    .limit(20)
    .collect()
)
The structure: every method returns a new query object (immutable, lazy). Nothing executes until .collect(). Each method corresponds to a logical operator. The chain is the plan.
An Ibis-style version (more SQL-shaped)
pythonimport ll

docs = ll.table("documents")
query_vec = ll.embed("hedging strategies")

expr = docs.filter([
    docs.created_at > ll.now() - ll.interval(days=30),
    docs.has_path("authored_by", to=author_id),
])

scored = expr.mutate(
    score=ll.hybrid_score(
        text=expr.body.match("derivatives"),
        vector=expr.body_embedding.distance(query_vec),
    )
)

result = (
    scored
    .order_by(ll.desc("score"))
    .limit(20)
    .select("id", "title", "score")
    .execute()
)
Subtly different from Polars — Ibis tends to construct relational expressions that look more like a SQL AST. Both are legitimate styles.
Now the honest comparison
This is where I want to be careful, because the choice isn't as one-sided as my "I lean SQL" framing made it sound. Let me lay out what each genuinely buys and costs.
Where the DSL wins, concretely
Composability. In Python you build queries programmatically. You can write a function def recent_documents(days: int) that returns a query fragment and reuse it. In SQL you concatenate strings and pray. Anyone who's built a system that programmatically generates SQL has felt this pain — escaping, quoting, dynamic WHERE clauses. The DSL doesn't have this problem; queries are objects.
pythondef by_author(author_id):
    return ll.table("documents").filter(
        ll.path("authored_by").to(author_id).exists()
    )

def recent(query, days):
    return query.filter(query.created_at > ll.now() - ll.days(days))

# Compose
result = recent(by_author(author_id), days=30).limit(20).collect()
This is impossible to do cleanly in SQL. You'd be string-building.
Type safety. The DSL knows the schema. If you type docs.titel (typo), the IDE catches it. If you call .match() on a non-text column, the DSL rejects it at construction time. SQL queries fail at parse or execution time with a "column does not exist" error.
For someone writing 50 queries a day, this is a genuine productivity gain.
Better integration with embeddings. This is the AI-native case specifically. In SQL you have EMBED('text', 'model_name') as a function call. In Python you can do:
pythonfrom sentence_transformers import SentenceTransformer
model = SentenceTransformer("BAAI/bge-large-en-v1.5")
query_vec = model.encode("hedging strategies")

result = docs.body_embedding.near(query_vec).limit(20).collect()
The embedding happens in the user's Python code with their model of choice. The vector is passed as data. SQL forces you to register every embedding model with the database via DDL, which is server-side complexity to support client-side flexibility.
For users with their own embedding pipelines (which is most AI shops — they have custom fine-tuned models), the DSL is genuinely better.
Natural fit for agentic workflows. If an LLM agent is generating queries, generating Python method chains is meaningfully easier than generating valid SQL. The DSL has fewer "gotchas" — no quoting, no SQL injection concerns, no dialect differences.
Pandas/Polars interop. Result is a DataFrame natively. No conversion layer. Users feed results into ML pipelines, plotting libraries, the broader Python ecosystem — zero friction.
Where SQL wins, concretely
The universal lingua franca. Every engineer knows SQL. Every BI tool speaks SQL. Every ORM compiles to SQL. Every analyst can query the database. This is not a small thing — it's the entire reason SQL has lasted 50 years.
If our customer is a startup with Python-only engineers building AI products, SQL is overhead. If our customer is a mid-sized company where analysts pull dashboards, finance runs reports, and engineers build apps — they all need to query the database, and SQL is the only language all of them speak.
Tooling. Tableau, Metabase, Looker, Superset, Grafana, Datadog, every notebook tool — they connect via SQL over standard protocols (Postgres wire, MySQL wire). A SQL database with the Postgres wire protocol gets dozens of integrations for free. A DSL database integrates with... nothing without writing custom adapters.
ORMs and existing applications. If a team has an existing application using SQLAlchemy, Prisma, Drizzle, ActiveRecord, Hibernate — they already have years of code generating SQL. To use a DSL database, they'd rewrite their data access layer. That's months of work and a non-starter for most existing teams.
Federation and joins to other systems. SQL databases speak to each other. Foreign data wrappers, ETL tools, CDC, replication — all use SQL. Our database with a DSL is islanded.
Ad-hoc queries. Engineer in incident response, 2am, needs to debug a thing. They open psql or a SQL console. They don't open a Python REPL and pip install ll. SQL is the language of incident response.
The "language" question, decomposed
I want to break down what "query language" actually means, because conflating these is where the SQL-vs-DSL debate goes wrong:

Surface syntax — what users type
Wire protocol — how clients talk to the server
Internal IR — what the planner operates on

These are independent decisions. You can have:

SQL surface, Postgres wire, our internal IR (what I proposed)
DSL surface, custom wire, our internal IR
Both SQL and DSL surfaces, multiple wire protocols, one internal IR

The third option is the right answer for us, and I want to revise my earlier recommendation accordingly.
The actual proposal
We build:

The internal IR — what we designed last turn. Logical plan, physical plan, optimizer, executor.
A SQL surface + Postgres wire protocol — primary client interface. This gives us the BI ecosystem, ORM compatibility, analyst access, all of it. Engineers can use the system from any language with any SQL client.
A Python DSL on top of the IR — second-class but well-supported. Same internal plan, different surface. Built for our AI engineering core users.

The DSL doesn't compile to SQL strings — it builds plan objects directly, sending them over the wire as serialized plans (FlatBuffers, or whatever we settle on). Faster than parsing SQL on the server, type-safe, programmatically composable.
This is what Ibis does. Ibis is a Python frontend over many backends (DuckDB, BigQuery, ClickHouse, Postgres, Snowflake). For DuckDB and BigQuery, Ibis builds query plans directly without going through SQL strings. For older backends, it generates SQL. The architecture proves the dual-surface model works.
We support both because they serve different users. SQL is the long tail (analysts, BI, existing apps). The DSL is the power tool for AI engineers writing application code against us.
What this means for v1
Practically:

v1 ships with SQL + Postgres wire as the primary interface. This is mandatory for any usable database in 2026.
v1 ships with a Python client library that uses SQL under the hood (generates SQL strings from method calls). This gives DSL-like ergonomics without building dual frontends.
v2 upgrades the Python client to bypass SQL and build plans directly. Faster, type-safe, fully featured.
v3 might add a Rust/TypeScript fluent client for the same reasons.

In other words: ship SQL first, then build the DSL as a better way to use the same database, then make it bypass SQL for performance. We don't fork the language stack.
Where I was wrong last turn
I framed this as "SQL vs DSL, I pick SQL." That was the wrong framing. The right framing is "SQL is mandatory; DSL is a layer on top that we'll build incrementally." Choosing SQL doesn't mean rejecting the DSL — it means SQL is the foundation and the DSL is added value.
I think this is genuinely the cleanest path. We don't lose adoption because SQL is there. We don't lose AI-engineer ergonomics because the DSL is coming. We don't build two parallel systems because the DSL is a frontend, not a separate engine.
On the specific syntax of an LL Python DSL
If we were to design it carefully, I'd want it to be:
Lazy by default. Method calls build a plan; nothing executes until .collect() or similar. This lets us optimize the whole query, not piece by piece.
Schema-aware. docs.body is a typed column reference. The IDE can autocomplete. Operations on it are typed.
First-class multi-modal. column.near(vector) for vector. column.match(query) for text. column.has_path(...) for graph. These are methods on columns, not awkward function calls.
Composable with Python ecosystem. Vectors are numpy arrays. Results are Polars or Pandas DataFrames. Embeddings come from any model the user has.
Async-friendly. Returns futures by default; .collect() awaits or blocks.
The Polars-style proposal I showed above is a reasonable starting point. Refinement happens through usage.
The genuine cost of dual surfaces
Building two surfaces isn't free. The cost:

Documentation duplicated (every feature explained in SQL and DSL)
Test surface doubled (every feature tested in both)
Bug surface — two parsers, two binding paths, possible behavioral inconsistencies
Decision overhead — for every new feature, "how does this look in both?"

This is real cost. The reason it's worth paying:

SQL is a moat. Without it, we're not a database.
The DSL is differentiation. Without it, we're just another Postgres + vector extension.

Both matter. Pay the cost.

So the revised position: SQL with Postgres wire protocol as primary interface in v1; thin Python client wrapping SQL in v1; native Python DSL building plans directly by v2. This is what we should lock in.
