Fair warning: this turn is dense. The cost model is mathematical in places. I'll try to keep the math grounded in what it actually buys us at execution time.
Why this matters more than people admit
Most vector databases don't have a real cost model. They have hardcoded heuristics: "always use HNSW," "always filter first," "always rerank top 100." These work for the median query and fail catastrophically on the tail.
The catastrophe is usually a filtered query. "Find similar documents where created_at > yesterday." If only 0.1% of documents are from yesterday, HNSW-then-filter returns mostly empty results — you searched the index for the top 100 and 99.9 of them got filtered out. Meanwhile, the right plan is "find rows from yesterday (cheap, B-tree), then brute-force vector distance over them (cheap, only 0.1% of corpus)." That plan is 100x faster and 100x more accurate. But you can only find it if you have a cost model.
Selectivity is the entire game. Every interesting decision in the planner is "which input is smaller, the vector candidates or the filter candidates?"
What a cost model is, concretely
A cost model is a function cost(physical_operator, input_statistics) → estimated_runtime. It estimates how long each candidate physical plan would take. The optimizer enumerates plans and picks the lowest-cost one.
The cost model has two pieces:

Cardinality estimation — given the inputs, how many rows does each operator produce?
Operator cost formulas — given the input cardinality, how expensive is each operator?

Cardinality estimation is where most of the difficulty lives. Operator cost formulas are mostly mechanical once cardinalities are known.
The currency: time, not abstract cost units
Traditional databases use abstract cost units ("page reads" * "cpu cost"). I'd argue we should use actual time estimates (microseconds) calibrated to the actual hardware. Reasoning:

Modern systems mix workloads where I/O and CPU costs vary wildly (HNSW is CPU-heavy, scan is I/O-heavy). Abstract units obscure this.
We need to compare across plans like "scan + filter" vs "HNSW search," and these have very different cost profiles. Same units make this honest.
Cardinality estimates are inherently fuzzy; time estimates are too, but at least they're interpretable.

So cost = estimated microseconds. Calibrated periodically from real measurements.
Cardinality estimation: the standard parts
Standard scalar cardinality estimation is well-understood. We do the textbook things:
Column statistics: per column, we maintain:

Row count (cardinality)
Distinct value count (NDV)
Min/max values
Null count
Sampled histogram (typically equi-height with ~256 buckets)
Most-common-values list (top N values + their frequencies)

These are computed during compaction (which already scans all data) and stored in the catalog. Refresh frequency = compaction frequency, which is good enough for most workloads.
Predicate selectivity: given column op value, estimate the fraction of rows matching:

= predicate: 1/NDV, or histogram lookup
<, >, <=, >=: histogram bucket interpolation
IN (...): sum of per-value selectivities
AND: product of selectivities (assumes independence — wrong in practice but standard)
OR: inclusion-exclusion
LIKE: hardcoded estimate per pattern shape

These are the same formulas Postgres has used for decades. They have known limitations (independence assumption, histogram bin imprecision) but they work well enough in practice.
Join cardinality: classical formulas based on inputs and join column NDVs.
We implement all of this. It's not novel, but it's table stakes. DataFusion has reasonable scalar cardinality estimation; we extend it where needed.
The novel parts: vector, text, graph cardinality
This is where it gets interesting. None of the existing cost models in DataFusion or any open vector database handle these well.
Vector cardinality estimation — the hardest
Given vector_distance(column, query) < threshold, how many rows satisfy?
This is genuinely hard because the distribution of distances depends on:

The query vector's position in the embedding space
The clustering structure of the data
The metric (cosine vs L2 vs dot product)

Standard scalar techniques don't help: a histogram of distances would depend on the query, which is unknown at compaction time.
What we can do, and what I'd propose:
At compaction time: for each file, compute a distance distribution sample. Pick 100 representative query vectors (cluster centroids of the file's vectors, sampled). For each, compute the distribution of distances to all vectors in the file. Store the resulting distribution per centroid.
At query time: given a query vector, find the closest sampled centroid. Use that centroid's distance distribution as the estimate. Selectivity at threshold T is then "fraction of distances below T" from the closest centroid's distribution.
This is approximate but principled. The closer the query is to a sampled centroid, the more accurate the estimate. Errors are bounded.
Storage cost: 100 centroids × distance histogram (~1KB) per file = ~100KB per file. Trivial.
CPU cost at query time: 100 distance computations to find closest centroid = ~100μs. Acceptable for query planning.
This is, to my knowledge, not done by any production vector database. They either use fixed selectivity estimates ("assume 1% of rows match any vector query") or skip cardinality estimation entirely. I think doing it properly is a real differentiator.
For top-K vector queries (no threshold, just ORDER BY distance LIMIT K), the cardinality is just K. Easy. The hard case is when there's a predicate combined with vector search.
Predicated vector cardinality — the critical case
ORDER BY embedding <-> q LIMIT 10 WHERE created_at > '2024-01-01'
We need to know: of the top 10 nearest neighbors, how many would satisfy the predicate?
Two estimates needed:

Predicate selectivity: fraction of all rows matching created_at > '2024-01-01'. Standard scalar estimate.
Conditional probability: given that a row is in the top K nearest, what's the probability it satisfies the predicate?

The independence assumption says these are equal — the predicate selectivity is the same in the top-K as in the full data. This is sometimes wrong (the query vector may correlate with the predicate; e.g., recent documents may be semantically similar to the query) but it's a reasonable starting point.
Under independence: expected top-K survivors = K × predicate_selectivity. If selectivity is 1%, we need to search for ~K/0.01 = 1000 candidates to find K survivors. That's the input to the operator decision:

If we need to search for 1000 candidates and HNSW returns them at cost C_hnsw, then post-filter — total cost = C_hnsw(1000) + filter_cost.
If we instead pre-filter to (1% of N rows) and brute force — total cost = filter_cost + brute_force(0.01 × N).

The optimizer compares these. For low predicate selectivity, pre-filter wins. For high selectivity, post-filter wins. There's a crossover point determined by data size and HNSW parameters.
For very small selectivity (e.g., 0.001%), even pre-filter is bad — you might filter to 100 candidates and brute-force them, but the answer is dominated by whether those 100 candidates are good. If your filter is "only Tuesday entries from 2019" you might find 100 vectors that are semantically far from the query. The cost model handles this case the same way — both plans are cheap; the optimizer picks brute force.
For the IntegratedFilteredHNSWSearch operator (HNSW traversal with predicate evaluation during traversal), cost estimation is more complex. The traversal explores roughly ef_search × adjustment_factor candidates where adjustment_factor depends on predicate selectivity (low selectivity means we visit more nodes to find enough survivors). Research papers like Filtered-DiskANN and ACORN give models for this. We adopt their estimates.
Concretely: for predicate selectivity p, expected work scales as approximately 1/p × log(N) distance computations. Sweet spot is roughly when 0.01 < p < 0.5. Outside this range, pre-filter (p < 0.01) or post-filter (p > 0.5) dominates.
These bounds aren't sharp — they depend on data, parameters, hardware. We calibrate them with measurements.
Text cardinality
Easier than vector. For column MATCH 'term':

We have the FST and the posting list per file
The FST tells us the posting list length without decoding it
Posting list length = number of documents containing the term

Sum across files = total cardinality. Exact, not estimated.
For multi-term queries (AND, OR, NOT):

AND: minimum of individual cardinalities (lower bound), product assuming independence (point estimate)
OR: sum (upper bound), inclusion-exclusion
NOT: total minus matching

These are standard IR estimates.
For phrase queries with positions: harder, because we need to estimate how many of the documents containing both terms have them in adjacent positions. Reasonable estimate: fraction proportional to average document length and term frequencies. Acceptable but imprecise.
For score-based top-K text queries (BM25 ranking, LIMIT K): the score-augmented auxiliary lists we designed help — they give us the top-K candidates per file directly, so the cardinality is just K.
Graph cardinality
Hardest of all. For a path traversal (a) -[:edge_type]-> (b) WHERE ...:

We know average out-degree per node (from compaction-time stats per edge column)
We know total node count
For a single hop from a constrained source: expected = source_cardinality × avg_out_degree
For depth-k: source_cardinality × avg_out_degree^k

This is wrong in practice because real graphs are power-law (degree distribution is heavy-tailed; average is meaningless). High-degree "celebrity" nodes blow up cardinality estimates if they're in the source set.
Better: maintain out-degree distribution histograms per edge column. If the source set is "nodes with id > X," look up the degree distribution for that subset (or estimate it from sampling). Sum expected degrees rather than using average × count.
For multi-hop traversals, cardinality grows multiplicatively but with strong correlation effects (multi-hop neighborhoods overlap). I don't think there's a clean solution here. We use multiplicative estimates with degradation factors and accept that deep graph queries have high planning uncertainty.
The pragmatic answer: cap the optimizer's confidence on graph cardinality. For traversals deeper than 3 hops, planner falls back to conservative defaults rather than trying to estimate. Surface this to users via EXPLAIN output ("estimated cardinality: 10000 (confidence: low — deep traversal)").
Hybrid query cardinality
For a hybrid query combining vector + text + graph + scalar:
The query has multiple "anchors" — operators that produce candidate row sets. The optimizer needs to estimate cardinality of each anchor and decide the order of evaluation.
Example: WHERE body MATCH 'derivatives' AND body_embedding <-> q < 0.3 AND created_at > yesterday
Three predicates, three anchors:

Text: maybe 5000 rows match
Vector: estimate via centroid distribution, maybe 50000 rows match
Date: 0.1% of corpus, maybe 1000 rows match

Most selective wins as the driving operator: start from the date predicate (1000 rows), then evaluate the others as post-filters. This is canonical predicate pushdown logic but applied to multi-modal predicates.
The non-obvious case: when two predicates have similar cardinality but very different evaluation costs. Text match might give us 5000 candidates at cost 1ms (one FST lookup, one posting list scan). Date predicate might give 1000 candidates at cost 10ms (full column scan). The optimizer weighs both cardinality and evaluation cost.
This is where the time-based cost model pays off — we compare microseconds against microseconds, not abstract units.
Operator cost formulas
Given cardinalities, what does each operator cost? I'll sketch the formulas for the novel operators; standard ones (Scan, Filter, Join) are textbook.
MultiFileVectorSearch(files, column, query, k):
cost = sum over files of:
  hnsw_search_cost(file_size, dim, M, ef_search) + 
  page_fetch_cost(estimated_page_reads)
+ merge_cost(num_files * k)
+ rerank_cost(top_K * full_dim_distance)
+ mvcc_filter_cost(top_K)
HNSW search cost: roughly ef_search × distance_cost × log(N) for the descent, plus ef_search × distance_cost for layer-0 beam search. Distance cost is dim × 4ns for int8 quantized SIMD distance, dim × 8ns for full precision.
Page fetch cost: estimate based on buffer pool hit rate for this file. If the file is "hot" (recently accessed), assume 95% hit rate; "cold," 5%. Tracked via cache statistics.
For 30K-vector files with 1536-dim int8 quantized HNSW: ~50μs HNSW search per file, ~10μs page fetch (warm cache), ~100μs rerank. Total ~200μs per file. Across 100 files in parallel, total wall-clock is ~5-10ms.
PreFilteredVectorSearch(files, predicate, column, query, k):
cost = scalar_filter_cost(predicate) +
       brute_force_distance_cost(estimated_filtered_rows, dim) +
       top_k_selection_cost(filtered_rows, k)
Brute force is filtered_rows × dim × 4ns. For 10K filtered rows at 1536 dim: ~60ms. For 1000 filtered rows: ~6ms.
The crossover: PreFiltered wins over MultiFileVectorSearch when filtered_rows × dim × 4ns < hnsw_total_cost. For our numbers (HNSW total ~10ms across all files), crossover is around 1500 filtered rows. Below that, brute force wins. Above, HNSW wins.
IntegratedFilteredHNSWSearch(files, predicate, column, query, k):
cost = hnsw_search_cost × adjustment_factor(predicate_selectivity) +
       predicate_eval_cost × visited_nodes +
       (other components similar to MultiFileVectorSearch)
The adjustment factor is empirical — typically 1.5x-5x base HNSW cost depending on selectivity. Bad for very low selectivity (factor > 10x means PreFiltered wins) or very high (factor ~1x but PostFiltered is even cheaper).
MultiFileTextSearch(files, column, query):
cost = sum over files of:
  fst_lookup_cost(query_terms) +
  posting_list_decode_cost(estimated_doc_freq) +
  intersection_cost(if multi-term) +
  scoring_cost(per_candidate)
FST lookups are microseconds. Decode is ~50ns per posting (SIMD-unpacked PFOR-Delta). For 5000-document term across all files: ~250μs decode, plus scoring.
ForwardWalk and ReverseWalk:
cost = source_lookup_cost +
       sum over depth levels of:
         expected_frontier_size × avg_edge_decode_cost +
         predicate_eval_cost (if filters along path)
Edge decode is fast (PFOR-Delta SIMD), ~10ns per edge. For a 2-hop traversal with frontier sizes 10, 100, the cost is dominated by predicate evaluation if any.
ScoreFusion(streams, function, k):
cost = sum of input stream costs (in parallel) +
       fusion_cost(total_inputs, function)
RRF fusion is O(total_inputs × log k) — cheap. Weighted linear combination is also cheap.
These formulas are approximate but in the right shape. They're calibrated against real measurements during system operation.
Calibration: how the cost model stays honest
Cost formulas have constants (distance_cost_per_dim_per_ns, page_fetch_cost, etc.). These vary with hardware. We don't hardcode them; we calibrate.
On system startup and periodically thereafter, run microbenchmarks:

Distance computation cost: time computing distances on synthetic vectors
Page fetch cost: time loading pages from buffer pool (hit) vs disk (miss)
HNSW traversal: time a query against a known index
B-tree lookup, hash join, scan throughput: standard benchmarks

Store the measured constants in the catalog. The cost model reads them at planning time.
This is what Postgres does (sort of — it has cost constants that need manual tuning). What real engines like ClickHouse and Snowflake do (auto-calibration). It's not optional for a system that wants to plan well on diverse hardware.
The optimizer architecture
Now the actual optimization. Given a logical plan, produce the best physical plan.
There are two main paradigms:
Rule-based optimization (RBO): apply transformation rules in a fixed order. Simple, predictable, fast. Limited by rule order — can't make decisions that require cost comparison.
Cost-based optimization (CBO): enumerate candidate plans, score each via cost model, pick the cheapest. More powerful, slower. Can find plans RBO misses.
Modern systems combine both. The standard architecture:

Rewrite phase (RBO): apply rules that are always good. Predicate pushdown, constant folding, projection pushdown, common subexpression elimination, join reordering for obvious cases.
Physical planning (CBO): for each logical operator, generate candidate physical operators. For each plan candidate, compute cost. Pick cheapest.
Post-optimization (RBO): cleanup, parallelization decisions, batch size tuning.

We follow this. DataFusion has a rule-based framework we extend.
Rules we'd add specifically
The novel rules for our system:
Predicate pushdown into vector search. A logical Filter(VectorSearch(...)) becomes PredicatedVectorSearch(...). The optimizer then chooses among Pre/Post/Integrated based on cost.
Predicate pushdown into text search. Filter(TextMatch(...)) similarly combined.
Index selection for vector columns. Choose between HNSW and brute-force based on column statistics and operator context. If a vector column is small (under 10K rows in a file), brute force may win.
Hybrid query decomposition. A multi-modal query is decomposed into per-modality sub-plans, joined by ScoreFusion. The order and parallelism of sub-plans is cost-driven.
Graph traversal direction. For EXISTS (PATH (a) -[edge]-> (b) WHERE cond_on_b), decide whether to start from a (forward walk) or b (reverse walk) based on which constraint is more selective. ForwardWalk vs ReverseWalk decision.
File pruning. Use file-level statistics (LSN range, zone maps, bloom filters in footer) to skip files entirely. This is the big LSM optimization — most queries touch a small fraction of files.
Plan enumeration: how many plans to consider?
CBO has a combinatorial problem. For a query with N joins and M index choices per scan, the plan space is exponential. Two strategies:
Exhaustive enumeration: try everything. Works for small queries (under 10 operators). Postgres does this with limits.
Heuristic pruning: dynamic programming over join orders (System R style), pruning dominated plans early. Standard for large queries.
We use both: exhaustive for small queries, DP-based for large ones. The crossover is around 8-10 operators (where exhaustive becomes too slow).
For our specific operators (vector, text, graph), the plan space is smaller than for arbitrary joins because there are fewer commutative operations. A VectorSearch can't be reordered with a TextMatch in arbitrary ways — they're applied to specific columns and produce specific outputs. The combinatorics are tamer than they look.
Hybrid scoring: the genuinely novel piece
Last big topic. When a query combines vector, text, and scalar signals, how do we combine scores into a single ranking?
The standard fusion methods:
RRF (Reciprocal Rank Fusion): each input stream produces a ranked list; combined score is sum over streams of 1/(k + rank_in_stream) where k is a constant (60 typical). Simple, parameter-free, robust. Used by Elastic, Vespa.
Weighted linear combination: each stream produces normalized scores; combined score is sum of weight × score. Requires score normalization (z-score, min-max). User specifies weights.
Learned-to-rank: train a model (gradient boosted trees, neural net) to combine signals. Requires training data. Most powerful when you have it.
Borda count: each stream votes for top-K; documents get scores based on votes. Less common but useful in some cases.
Reciprocal rank with score awareness: like RRF but uses actual scores, not just ranks. Multiple variants exist.
What we provide:
RRF as the default — it's robust, parameter-free, works well in practice. Used out of the box for hybrid_score(...).
Weighted linear as the customizable option — users specify weights and normalization. For users who know what they want.
Plug-in interface for custom fusion — users register a Rust function or SQL expression that takes per-stream scores and returns combined score. Lets advanced users do whatever they want.
Learned ranking as a v2 feature — train a fusion model from query logs. Out of scope for v1 but architecturally accommodated.
The fusion function is part of the physical plan. The optimizer treats it as a fixed operator with known cost (cheap — fusion is O(K log K) for top-K).
The bigger question: how does scoring compose with filtering and sorting?
In SQL terms, hybrid_score(...) is just an expression. It returns a numeric value per row. Filtering and sorting use it like any other column.
But there's a subtlety: hybrid scoring is expensive to compute compared to scalar fields. Each row's score requires running vector distance, text scoring, etc. Computing it for the whole table just to sort and limit is wasteful.
The optimization: when the query is ORDER BY hybrid_score(...) LIMIT K, the optimizer recognizes this and uses a top-K combining strategy:

Each input stream produces top-K (vector top-K, text top-K, etc.)
Union the candidates (overlap is fine)
Compute full hybrid score only for the union (small set)
Sort by hybrid score, return top K

This is the "early termination" optimization. It avoids computing scores for the long tail. Works because RRF and most fusion functions are monotonic — a candidate can only be in the final top-K if it's in some input stream's top-K (or close to it).
For mathematical correctness with RRF specifically: we actually need top-K' where K' > K because a doc could rank low in every stream but appear in many. In practice, K' = 2K to 5K gives near-perfect recall. We use K' = 3K as default, tunable.
Honest tradeoffs in the cost model and optimizer
Estimation accuracy. Cardinality estimates are wrong. They're always wrong. The question is how wrong, and what that does to plan quality. We're accepting moderate errors (within 5x) for most queries, larger errors for edge cases (deep graph, predicates correlated with vector). Bad estimates produce bad plans, which produce slow queries. There's no fixing this fundamentally — the goal is to minimize the damage.
Centroid distance distribution at compaction time. The 100KB per file is real cost. The compute to build it (100 centroids × distance to all vectors in file = 30K × 100 distance ops = 3M ops per file) is real. For systems with thousands of files this adds compaction time. Justifiable for the planning improvement.
Calibration drift. Hardware behaves differently under load than at benchmarking time. Calibrated constants drift. We accept some inaccuracy and recalibrate periodically.
Plan caching vs replanning. For repeated queries, we'd want to cache compiled plans. But plans become stale as statistics change. We cache aggressively for prepared statements but always replan when statistics significantly differ. Operational complexity.
Optimizer time itself. Generating and scoring N candidate plans takes time. For simple queries, optimization time can exceed execution time. We bound optimizer time (say, 10ms hard cap for non-prepared queries) and fall back to heuristic plans if exhausted.
Three places to push on
Centroid distance distribution vs simpler heuristics. I argued for the per-file centroid-based vector cardinality estimation. The simpler alternative: fixed selectivity (assume X% of rows match any vector query), with X learned from query logs over time. Less precise but simpler to implement. For v1, the simpler approach might be right and we add centroid distributions in v2. My instinct is centroid distributions from day one because they fix a real problem (filtered ANN is hopelessly broken without them), but it's a complexity I'm imposing on v1.
RRF as default vs requiring user-specified weights. RRF is parameter-free, which is a benefit but also a limitation — users can't tune it. The alternative is requiring users to specify weights and normalization, which is more powerful but adds friction. I leaned RRF for simplicity. Reasonable to disagree if you think AI engineering customers want fine control.
Time-based vs unit-based cost model. I argued for actual microseconds. The argument against: time depends on hardware, load, cache state — it's noisy. Unit-based costs are stable. The argument for time: it's interpretable, comparable across operator types, ties directly to user-perceived latency. I think time wins for our use case but it's an opinionated call.

Locking the cost model and optimizer:

Cost = estimated microseconds, calibrated periodically against real measurements
Standard scalar cardinality estimation (NDV, histograms, MCV lists) computed during compaction
Vector cardinality via per-file centroid distance distributions (100 centroids × distance histogram per file)
Predicated vector cardinality via independence assumption (with explicit acknowledgment that this is wrong in some cases)
Text cardinality exact from FST + posting list lengths
Graph cardinality via out-degree histograms; deep traversal estimates capped at low confidence
Hybrid query cardinality via per-modality estimates + most-selective-first ordering
