That's the cost model and optimizer. We've now designed: storage layer (record, memtable, file format, compaction, buffer pool) + metadata layer (catalog) + query layer (plan IR, physical operators, cost model, optimizer). The bulk of the system architecture.
What's left:

Execution engine — how the physical plan actually runs. Morsel-driven parallelism, vectorized batches, operator state management, cancellation, memory limits.
Embedding lifecycle — the full story for EMBED FROM columns. Inline vs deferred, batching, retries, error handling, model migration with dual-read.
Transactions and isolation — we've sketched MVCC; need to nail down isolation levels, read/write concurrency, lock-free read paths.
Wire protocol and SDK — what clients see. Postgres wire compatibility, native protocol option, the Python DSL layer.
Observability — EXPLAIN output, query telemetry, slow query log, metrics for ops.

My instinct: execution engine next. It's the direct consumer of the physical plan; we've designed plans without fully specifying how they run. Once we have execution sorted, the embedding lifecycle is a relatively self-contained subsystem that depends on it.
