I'll structure this around the layers an executor has to handle: how data flows through operators, how parallelism is organized, how memory is managed, how errors and cancellation work, and what's specific to our multi-modal operators.
The flow model: push vs. pull
First fundamental choice. How does data move through the operator tree?
Pull (Volcano): parent operator calls next() on its child to get the next batch. Demand-driven. Each operator is a coroutine producing rows on request.
Push (data-driven): producer operators push batches to consumers as they become available. Producer-driven. More natural for parallelism.
Hybrid push-pull (morsel-driven): data is divided into morsels at the source; each morsel is pushed through a pipeline by a worker thread; pipelines coordinate via shared state at pipeline boundaries.
We're committing to morsel-driven. It's the modern consensus for analytical engines (DuckDB, Velox, HyPer, Doris, ClickHouse). The reasons:
Pull execution is fundamentally serial without per-operator threading, which adds overhead. Push execution scales but has awkward backpressure semantics — when a consumer is slow, the producer overruns memory.
Morsel-driven solves this: a morsel is a small chunk (typically 100K rows or 1MB) that flows through a pipeline of operators on a single worker thread. Many workers process different morsels in parallel. Pipelines break at "pipeline breakers" — operators like aggregation and sort that need to see all input before producing output. At pipeline boundaries, morsels accumulate in queues; consumer pipelines drain those queues.
Backpressure is natural: if a downstream pipeline is slow, its input queue fills, and upstream pipelines block on queue insertion. Parallelism is natural: spawn N worker threads, each processes morsels independently.
DataFusion supports this paradigm; we build on it.
Morsels in detail
A morsel is a unit of work that flows through one pipeline on one thread. Concretely:

Source: ~100K rows or a fixed batch from a scan or memtable
Format: Arrow RecordBatch (columnar, SIMD-friendly)
Mutable until pipeline completion; immutable thereafter
Carries metadata: source file_id (for predicate pushdown context), row IDs (for MVCC visibility), execution context

A morsel's lifetime: created by a source operator (scan, vector search, text search), passed through filter/project/limit operators (no boundary crossing), eventually either consumed by a sink operator (aggregate, sort, top-K) or returned to the client as result.
Size tuning matters: too small (1K rows) and per-morsel overhead dominates. Too large (1M rows) and parallelism is coarse-grained, late-arriving morsels can't be load-balanced. Sweet spot is 100K rows for most workloads; configurable.
Pipeline structure
A physical plan is broken into pipelines. Each pipeline is a chain of operators that share morsel flow — no buffering between them, just function composition.
Example: Scan → Filter → Project → ... → HashAggregate
This breaks into pipelines at the HashAggregate (which is a pipeline breaker — it accumulates state across all input morsels before producing output). So:

Pipeline 1: Scan → Filter → Project → InsertIntoAggregateHash
Pipeline 2: ReadAggregateHash → ProduceResults

Pipeline 1 has many workers in parallel; each pulls a scan range, runs filter and project, inserts into the shared aggregate hash table. Pipeline 2 starts after pipeline 1 completes; produces results from the hash table.
Pipeline breakers: HashAggregate, Sort, HashJoin (build side), HashSetJoin, top-K. Non-breakers: Filter, Project, MergeJoin (after sort), Limit (in some cases).
This isn't novel — it's standard morsel-driven design. What's specific to us is what happens at the multi-modal pipeline starts.
Vector and text searches as pipeline sources
A logical MultiFileVectorSearch(files, query, k=20) runs as one pipeline. It's not parallelized by morsels in the same way scans are. The parallelism is at the file level:

Spawn N parallel HNSW searches, one per file (or per file group if there are many files)
Each search produces top-k candidates for its file
A merge operator combines results into global top-k
Top-k flows downstream as a morsel

This is a different parallelism unit from row-level morsels. The execution scheduler has to handle both:

Row-level parallelism for scans, filters, joins
File-level parallelism for vector/text/graph index access

Practically: file-level work units are still morsels, just with one row each (the top-K result), and the pipeline that produced them was parallelized across files rather than across row ranges. The scheduler treats them uniformly after that point.
Similarly for MultiFileTextSearch: per-file FST + posting list operations in parallel, merge into global top-K.
For graph traversals, parallelism is by frontier: BFS expansion of N frontier nodes in parallel; each expansion may produce many output nodes; new frontier is the union.
Memory management within execution
Each query has a memory budget. Execution must respect it.
Standard problems:

Hash tables for joins/aggregates grow unbounded if naive
Sort needs to hold all input or spill
Top-K needs only K rows of state (cheap)
Window functions need varying state

The memory model:
Per-query budget: each query is admitted with a memory limit (default: 1GB; configurable; system enforces total across all concurrent queries).
Per-operator allocation: operators allocate from the query's budget via a tracked allocator. Allocations succeed if budget allows; otherwise the operator either spills to disk or fails.
Spillable operators: HashAggregate, Sort, HashJoin support disk spill when memory is exhausted. They partition state into chunks; when memory pressure hits, oldest/largest chunks spill to temporary files; processing continues with remaining state in memory.
Non-spillable operators: top-K, simple filter, project — these have bounded memory by construction, no spill needed.
Global admission control: when total query memory exceeds system memory, new queries wait in admission queue. Existing queries proceed.
Spill is a fall-back, not a goal. Properly sized queries shouldn't spill. The system surfaces spill in observability metrics so operators can tune.
For multi-modal operators specifically:

HNSW search has bounded memory (the candidate set, size ef_search) — no spill needed
Text search posting list scans are streaming — no spill needed
Graph BFS frontier can grow large but is bounded by graph properties; we cap depth and frontier size; queries that would exceed limits fail explicitly

Operator state machines
Each operator is a small state machine:

Init: prepare (allocate buffers, open child operators, look up indexes)
Run: process incoming morsels
Finalize: emit any pending output (top-K materialization, etc.)
Cleanup: release resources

The interface in Rust (sketched):
rusttrait PhysicalOperator: Send + Sync {
    fn init(&mut self, context: &ExecutionContext) -> Result<()>;
    fn process(&mut self, morsel: Morsel) -> Result<Option<Morsel>>;
    fn finalize(&mut self) -> Result<Option<Morsel>>;
    fn cleanup(&mut self) -> Result<()>;
}
Returning Some(morsel) means "here's output." None means "I consumed input but produced nothing." Finalize is called once at end of input.
Stateful operators (HashAggregate, Sort) store state internally. State is partitioned for parallel access; each worker thread has its own state partition, with synchronization at finalize.
This is straightforward operator interfaces — DataFusion provides equivalents that we extend.
Multi-modal operator internals
What's specific to our operators:
HNSW search execution:

Init: open the HNSW section of each target file; cache footer/translation tables; load entry point pages into buffer pool
Process: not used (HNSW is a source, no input morsels)
Finalize: produce top-K morsel
Internal logic: for each file in parallel (using rayon or async tasks): descend HNSW, beam-search at layer 0, return file-local top-K. Then merge across files. Then rerank top global candidates against full-precision vectors. Apply MVCC filter.

This is essentially a complex source operator with internal parallelism. It looks like a single operator from the plan tree but does substantial work.
Text search execution:

Init: open FST and posting list pointers per file
Process: not used
Finalize: per file in parallel, query FST for each term, decode posting lists, intersect/merge as needed, score (BM25), top-K per file. Merge across files. Apply MVCC filter.

Graph traversal execution:

Init: open edge sections per file
Process: takes a morsel of source rows
Finalize: produces morsel of target rows (or path tuples for path queries)
Internal logic: BFS with frontier expansion. Each frontier expansion: for each source row, look up its edges (forward CSR or reverse CSR depending on direction), filter by edge predicates if any, add to next frontier. Continues until depth limit or empty frontier. Bloom filter consultation for reverse traversal across files.

Score fusion execution:

Init: prepare to receive from multiple parallel inputs
Process: collects top-K morsels from each input stream
Finalize: applies fusion function (RRF or custom), produces top-K
Internal logic: stream candidates from each input into a unified priority queue (capped at 3K candidates by default); compute hybrid scores; produce top K.

Cancellation and timeouts
Long-running queries must be cancelable. Standard mechanism:
Each query has a CancelToken (essentially an atomic bool). Operators check it periodically — typically once per morsel, once per HNSW layer descent, once per text posting block.
On cancellation: operators short-circuit, return immediately, no further work. The query coordinator collects the cancellation, releases resources, returns "canceled" to the client.
Timeouts are implemented as cancellation: a timer thread sets the cancel token after the deadline.
This is critical for interactive workloads — a misbehaving query shouldn't block the system. We bake cancellation in everywhere.
Concurrency model
Multiple queries run concurrently. Each has its own:

Memory budget tracker
Cancel token
Worker thread pool (drawn from a global pool)
Buffer pool reference (shared)
Catalog reference (shared, read-only after open)

Worker threads are global pool, not per-query. A query "requests" workers (up to its parallelism limit); workers pick up work from query queues; queries that need more workers wait if pool is exhausted.
Parallelism control: per-query max_parallelism parameter (default: number of cores). System-wide max_workers parameter (default: 2x cores). Total parallelism across queries is bounded by max_workers.
This is essentially what DataFusion and other modern engines do. We piggyback on Rust's tokio or rayon for thread management.
Error handling
Errors fall into categories:

Query errors: invalid plan, type mismatch, missing column, etc. Caught at planning, never reach execution.
Resource errors: out of memory, disk full, query budget exceeded. Cause query failure; operators clean up; report to client.
Data errors: corrupted file (page checksum failure), bad encoding. Causes query failure; system marks file as suspect for ops attention.
Transient errors: network issue (for embedded models calling external APIs), I/O retry-recoverable. Operator retries internally with backoff.

Each operator handles its own errors. Errors propagate up the operator tree; the executor catches them at the root and returns to client. Resources are released in cleanup() regardless of success or failure.
Streaming results
For long result sets, we stream rather than buffer:

Result morsels are sent to the client as they're produced
Client receives them incrementally
If the client is slow, backpressure propagates back through the executor
Connection broken = cancellation triggered

This is how every modern OLAP system works. Standard.
For top-K queries, results are produced at the end (after merge/sort). For non-top-K queries, results stream throughout execution.
Adaptive execution: a v2 feature worth flagging
Modern engines like Spark and Snowflake adapt plans at runtime: if actual cardinalities differ from estimates, the plan adjusts mid-execution.
Example: estimated cardinality of a filter was 1000 rows; actual is 1M rows. The downstream join was planned as hash join with this side as build; now we should switch to merge join.
Adaptive execution is genuinely useful for unpredictable workloads. It's also genuinely complex. We defer it to v2.
For v1, we get the cost model close enough that we don't catastrophically mispick. Calibration helps. Observability helps users catch misestimates manually.
What's novel here vs. stolen
Stolen:

Morsel-driven execution: TUM HyPer paper
Pipeline structure with pipeline breakers: textbook OLAP
Vectorized batches via Arrow: modern OLAP standard
Operator state machines: standard
Memory budgeting with spillable operators: PostgreSQL, Spark
Cancel tokens and timeouts: standard

Specific to our system:

The two-tier parallelism model (row-level morsels + file-level index parallelism) under one scheduler
Multi-modal source operators (HNSW, text, graph) with their internal parallelism and merge logic
Score fusion as a first-class operator with early termination
MVCC visibility filters integrated into source operators rather than as separate filter step

Most of this is well-trodden ground. The interesting bits are how the multi-modal operators fit into the morsel-driven framework cleanly — they look like any other operator from the scheduler's perspective, but their internal parallelism is different.
