Honest tradeoffs
DataFusion as the framework: pays off in speed-to-market, costs in flexibility. Our multi-modal operators are custom; DataFusion's standard operators are reused.
Morsel size hardcoded vs adaptive: 100K rows is right for most workloads; some workloads (very small tables) over-shard, some (very wide rows) under-shard. Operator hints at compile time can help. Adaptive sizing is v2.
Single-tier parallelism for multi-modal sources: we made HNSW/text/graph sources internally parallel rather than fitting them into the row-morsel model. This adds complexity but matches the data access patterns. The alternative — uniform row-morsel parallelism — would be cleaner but slower (HNSW per row doesn't make sense).
No adaptive execution in v1: estimates can be wrong and we can't fix it mid-flight. Pain points show up as slow queries that EXPLAIN ANALYZE shows clearly. Operators can rewrite manually. Real cost but bounded.
Tokio vs custom scheduler: I assumed tokio-based async/await. This is fine but not optimal — tokio is designed for I/O concurrency, not CPU work. For CPU-bound stages, rayon is better. We use both: tokio for I/O coordination (page fetches), rayon for CPU work (distance computations, hash table inserts). Hybrid is more code but right answer.
Three places to push on
Morsel-driven vs DAG-based scheduling. I argued morsel-driven. Some engines (Spark, Trino) use DAG-based with batch processing per stage. DAG is simpler conceptually, morsel is better for streaming and small-batch workloads. For our likely workload (lots of small interactive queries from AI apps), morsel is right. For analytical batch workloads, DAG might be cleaner. Reasonable to disagree.
DataFusion as foundation. Same as before — leverage vs constraint. I committed to it. Real alternatives are forking DataFusion (too much custom work) or building from scratch (months of effort). DataFusion-based is the pragmatic choice.
Memory budgeting granularity. I made it per-query with operator-level tracking. The alternative is system-wide with no per-query limits — simpler but lets one query starve others. Per-query is correct for production systems; adds bookkeeping cost. I think it's right; some teams prefer system-wide simplicity for v1.

Locking the execution engine:

Morsel-driven parallelism, morsels as Arrow RecordBatches (~100K rows default, configurable)
Pipeline structure with pipeline breakers at HashAggregate, Sort, HashJoin (build), top-K
Two-tier parallelism: row-morsel for relational ops, file-level for multi-modal sources
DataFusion as the framework, extended with our novel operators
Tokio for I/O, rayon for CPU-bound work
Per-query memory budget with operator-level tracking; spillable operators (HashAggregate, Sort, HashJoin) support disk spill
Operator state machine interface (init/process/finalize/cleanup)
Cancel tokens for query cancellation and timeouts
Streaming results (no result buffering by default)
Multi-modal operators with internal parallelism: HNSW search per file, text search per file, graph traversal by frontier, score fusion via priority queue
MVCC visibility filtering integrated into source operators
No adaptive execution in v1; deferred to v2


That closes execution. We've now designed: storage (record, memtable, file format, compaction, buffer pool) + catalog + query (plan IR, cost model, optimizer, execution engine). The system architecture is mostly settled.
What's left:

Embedding lifecycle — full story for EMBED FROM columns. Lazy vs eager, batching, retries, model migration with dual-read, embedding cache.
Transactions and isolation — sketched MVCC throughout but never nailed down isolation levels, write coordination, conflict handling.
Wire protocol and SDK — Postgres wire compatibility, native protocol option, Python DSL layer details.
Observability — EXPLAIN output, query telemetry, metrics, slow query log.
Recovery and durability semantics — what actually happens on crash, what guarantees we provide.

My instinct on order: transactions and isolation next. We've been talking about MVCC informally throughout; we should nail down the precise semantics before moving on, because embedding lifecycle decisions (when embeddings become visible, what happens during model migration) depend on transaction semantics being clear.
Transactions next, or different preference?You said: lets do the next partlets do the next part01:39Claude responded: Good.Good. Transactions and isolation. We've been informal about this throughout — "MVCC via xmin/xmax," "snapshot at LSN T" — and it's time to pin down precise semantics. Get this wrong and the system is subtly broken in ways users won't discover until they have a corruption incident.
I want to organize this around the questions that actually matter: what isolation level we offer, how reads and writes coordinate, what conflicts look like, how garbage collection works, and the genuinely novel question of how transactions interact with embedding generation.
The starting point: what we already committed to
Throughout the design we've assumed:

Every record has xmin (LSN at which it was created) and xmax (LSN at which it was deleted, or ∞)
Reads at snapshot LSN T see rows where xmin ≤ T < xmax
One unified WAL with LSNs assigned at write time
Compaction is the GC mechanism for old versions
Active transactions tracked in catalog; min snapshot LSN is the GC barrier

This gives us the mechanism. What we haven't specified is the semantics — which isolation level, what happens on conflicts, what guarantees we make to users.
Isolation level: choosing what we offer
The textbook isolation levels, in order of increasing strictness:
Read Uncommitted: dirty reads allowed. Nobody actually wants this.
Read Committed: reads see only committed data. Each statement gets a fresh snapshot. Most databases' default. Allows non-repeatable reads (same query in same transaction gives different results).
Repeatable Read / Snapshot Isolation: transaction sees a consistent snapshot from its start. All reads use the same snapshot. Doesn't allow non-repeatable reads. Susceptible to write skew anomalies.
Serializable: transactions appear to execute one at a time. Strongest, slowest.
Snapshot Isolation as our primary level. This is what we get naturally from MVCC, and it's the right tradeoff for our workload:

Strong consistency for the common case (read what you wrote, consistent reads within a transaction)
No read locks — reads never block writes, writes never block reads
Composes cleanly with our LSN-based architecture
AI/analytical workloads care about consistent snapshots more than serializability

Postgres calls this "Repeatable Read" though it's actually Snapshot Isolation. Oracle, SQL Server, MySQL all offer it. It's the de facto standard for modern databases.
We also offer Read Committed as a session option for clients that want statement-level fresh snapshots (some workflows prefer this). Implementation: each statement takes a new snapshot LSN; everything else is the same.
We do not offer Serializable in v1. True serializability requires either pessimistic locking (kills throughput) or optimistic conflict detection across reads (Serializable Snapshot Isolation, SSI — adds complexity). v2 consideration.
We do not offer Read Uncommitted because it provides no value and forecloses optimization opportunities (we'd have to expose uncommitted state).
The write path: how transactions actually commit
A write transaction's lifecycle:

Begin: client requests transaction. System assigns txn_id (monotonic counter from catalog). Transaction is "active." No snapshot LSN yet — assigned at first read or commit.
First read (if any): snapshot LSN = current LSN. This snapshot is fixed for the transaction's lifetime under Snapshot Isolation. (Under Read Committed, snapshot is reset per statement.)
Writes: each write produces WAL records with provisional xmin = txn_id (not yet a real LSN). Writes are accumulated in a per-transaction buffer plus speculative entries in the active memtable.
Commit:

System assigns commit LSN (sequential, atomic with WAL write)
Append a COMMIT record to WAL with txn_id and commit_lsn
fsync (with group commit for throughput)
Replace provisional xmin = txn_id with real xmin = commit_lsn in memtable
Transaction is durable; client sees success


Abort (rollback or crash before commit):

Append ABORT record to WAL (or omit COMMIT)
Any provisional writes in memtable are marked dead
On recovery, transactions without COMMIT records are aborted



The key trick: writes happen at LSN-assignment time (when they enter WAL) but their commit_lsn isn't known until commit. We use the txn_id as a placeholder, then resolve to commit_lsn at commit.
Reads in other transactions see writes from txn T iff txn T has committed and commit_lsn ≤ reader's snapshot LSN. This requires reads to check both: is the writer committed? If so, at what LSN?
The Commit Log: how readers know who's committed
Each transaction's commit status lives in the commit log — a system-wide structure mapping txn_id → commit_lsn (or aborted). This is in-memory for active and recent transactions; persistent in WAL for crash recovery.
Reading row with xmin = txn_id:

Look up txn_id in commit log
If committed: replace xmin with commit_lsn, apply visibility check against reader's snapshot
If aborted: row is invisible
If still active: row is invisible to reader (it can't see uncommitted writes)

The commit log is hot — every read checks it. We keep it in memory as a hashmap of recent transactions. Old transactions (commit_lsn < GC barrier) are garbage collected from the commit log; by then their rows in the memtable have had xmin resolved to commit_lsn directly.
Resolving xmin at commit time vs lazily
Two strategies:
Eager resolution: at commit, walk through all of this transaction's writes in the memtable and replace xmin = txn_id with xmin = commit_lsn. Reads after commit see resolved xmin and don't need commit log lookup.
Lazy resolution: leave xmin = txn_id in records; readers check commit log per row. When records flush to disk, resolve xmin at flush time.
Eager is faster for reads (no commit log lookup) but slower for commits (walk through writes). Lazy is faster for commits but slower for reads.
For our workload (read-heavy, want fast reads), I'd go eager resolution. The commit walk is fast because it's localized to one transaction's writes (typically dozens or hundreds of rows), and avoiding per-read commit log lookups on hot data is a real win.
Postgres does lazy resolution (called "hint bits") — but Postgres has different tradeoffs because its xmin is stored per-tuple and any read can update the hint. We don't have that luxury because our data lives in columnar batches that aren't easily updated in-place. Eager at commit time is cleaner for us.
The read path: visibility checks during query execution
Earlier I said "MVCC filter integrated into source operators." Let me make that concrete:
For each row produced by a source operator (Scan, HNSW, text search, graph traversal), apply visibility:

Read xmin, xmax of the row
Visible if: xmin ≤ snapshot_lsn AND (xmax > snapshot_lsn OR xmax = ∞)
Also visible if: row was written by current transaction (xmin == my_txn_id) — read-your-writes

If invisible, drop the row from the result.
For source operators that internally index data (HNSW, FST), visibility happens after the index returns candidates. The index might return invisible rows; we filter them at rerank time (for HNSW) or after posting list intersection (for text).
For columnar reads, xmin and xmax are columns like any other — we read them as part of the row's column set. Compression is good (most rows in a file have similar xmin from when they were written; xmax is sparse).
Read-your-writes within a transaction
A transaction must see its own writes immediately. This is non-negotiable for correctness.
Implementation: when a transaction reads, it checks both:

Committed rows visible at snapshot LSN
Uncommitted rows written by current transaction (xmin == my_txn_id)

The second check requires per-transaction visibility logic. In practice: the transaction has a write buffer (its memtable speculative entries); reads consult this buffer first, then the regular memtable + files.
This is the same as Postgres' "command counter" for sub-statement visibility — we can extend to per-statement granularity if needed, but for v1 transaction-level read-your-writes is sufficient.
Write conflicts: when do transactions fail?
Under Snapshot Isolation, the canonical conflict is the first-committer-wins rule for concurrent updates to the same row.
Scenario: txn A reads row R at version V. Txn B also reads row R at version V. Both compute new values. Both try to UPDATE row R.
In Snapshot Isolation:

The first one to commit succeeds, row goes to new version
The second one to commit detects the conflict: row R has changed since my snapshot
Second transaction aborts with "serialization failure" — client must retry

Implementation: at commit time, for each row this transaction modified, check whether the row's xmin has changed since this transaction's snapshot. If yes (someone else committed an update), abort this transaction.
This requires a write set per transaction (the row IDs modified) and a check at commit:

Acquire row locks for write set (brief, for the check)
For each row in write set: verify row's current xmin ≤ my snapshot LSN
If all pass: commit succeeds, release locks
If any fail: abort

The locks here are short — held only during the check, not during the transaction. This isn't pessimistic locking; it's optimistic conflict detection.
For our system specifically:

Row IDs are stable (file_id, offset), so the write set is just a set of row IDs
Checking xmin is reading metadata from memtable or files — fast
Locks are sharded by row ID hash for parallelism

INSERT conflicts
Two transactions inserting into the same table don't conflict (they produce different row IDs). Exception: if both insert rows with the same primary key, PK uniqueness violation. Detected at insert time via PK index lookup.
DELETE-DELETE conflicts
Two transactions deleting the same row — first wins, second aborts.
INSERT-DELETE conflicts
Transaction A inserts row R with PK 'foo'. Transaction B reads with PK 'foo' (sees nothing), inserts row R' with PK 'foo'. Both try to commit.
If A commits first, B's commit fails because row 'foo' now exists. B's insert lookup happened against B's snapshot which didn't see A; but B's PK uniqueness check at commit time does see A. PK uniqueness is enforced at commit, not at insert, for SI correctness.
Write skew: the unavoidable SI anomaly
The classic SI failure: two transactions read overlapping data, write disjoint data, both succeed, but the combined result violates an invariant.
Example: "at least one of A or B must remain true." Txn 1 reads A=true, B=true, sets A=false. Txn 2 reads A=true, B=true, sets B=false. Both commit. Now A=false and B=false. Invariant violated.
SI doesn't catch this because the transactions don't write to the same row. Their reads would conflict under serializability but SI only checks writes.
For v1: we accept write skew. Users who need stricter guarantees use explicit locking (SELECT ... FOR UPDATE) which we should support.
For v2: SSI (Serializable Snapshot Isolation, from PostgreSQL) detects write skew by tracking read-write dependencies. Adds overhead but provides true serializability.
Long-running transactions and the GC barrier
The min snapshot LSN across active transactions is the GC barrier — compaction can't drop versions newer than this.
A long-running transaction holds the GC barrier back, preventing cleanup of old versions. If a transaction runs for an hour, an hour's worth of dead versions accumulate.
Mitigation:

Surface long-running transactions in observability ("warning: txn 12345 has held a snapshot for 2 hours")
Per-session max transaction duration (default: 1 hour, configurable)
Beyond max duration: transaction aborted automatically with "snapshot too old" error
This is what Oracle does; Postgres has similar mechanisms

The mechanism: a background watcher checks active transaction durations every minute. Transactions exceeding max are signaled to abort. Their snapshot is released; GC can proceed.
Statement-level vs transaction-level snapshots
Under Snapshot Isolation, the snapshot is at transaction start. Under Read Committed, it's at statement start.
For Read Committed: each query takes a fresh snapshot before executing. Within a single transaction, two queries can see different states. This is what most apps use by default (it's MySQL/Postgres default).
We support both. Client opens transaction with BEGIN ISOLATION LEVEL READ COMMITTED or BEGIN ISOLATION LEVEL REPEATABLE READ (we map "repeatable read" to SI per Postgres convention). Default is Read Committed (matches Postgres default; familiar to most users).
The DDL and transactions question
Can DDL be inside a transaction? BEGIN; CREATE TABLE foo (...); INSERT INTO foo VALUES (...); COMMIT;
Postgres allows this. Most other databases don't (DDL auto-commits).
For us:

Schema changes update the catalog (redb-backed)
redb has transactions
We can support transactional DDL by binding catalog updates to query transactions

But: schema changes have cross-cutting effects (compilation cache invalidation, etc.) that are easier to reason about if they're not deferred. And background index builds can't be transactional (they're long-running).
Decision: DDL auto-commits in v1. Each DDL statement is its own transaction. Transactional DDL is v2. This matches the majority of databases.
The novel question: transactions and embedding generation
This is where it gets specific to us. When a row is inserted with an EMBED FROM column, the embedding needs to be computed. When does this happen relative to the transaction?
Three options:
Inline (synchronous): compute embedding before transaction commit. Transaction blocks on embedding API call. Slow but transactional — either you have the embedding or you don't.
Deferred (asynchronous): row commits without embedding; background process generates it. Embedding column is null until generation completes. Fast commits but row is non-queryable for vector search until embedding is ready.
Hybrid: commit immediately with embedding marked as "pending"; background process generates and updates. Vector queries on pending rows fall back to scan or filter them.
For v1, I'd argue deferred with pending marker is right:

Embedding generation is slow (50-500ms for API calls, even for batched local models)
Inline would make every insert dominated by embedding latency
Users care about insert throughput
The pending state is short-lived (seconds for batched generation)

Pending state mechanics:

Row is committed with embedding = NULL and a pending_embed_token set in row metadata
Background embedding worker picks up pending rows in batches
Worker calls embedding service, gets vectors, writes them as updates (new row versions with embedding populated, original null embedding superseded via MVCC)
These updates are atomic per row: embedding update commits, supersedes the null-embedding version

Visibility:

Reads see the row regardless of embedding status (scalar columns work)
Vector search filters out rows where embedding is null (they can't be ranked)
Once embedding is populated, future vector queries find them

Backpressure: the embedding worker has a queue. If the queue grows too large (sustained high write rate exceeding embedding throughput), we surface this as a warning. Insert throttling kicks in only at extreme buildup.
Failure: if embedding fails (API down, rate limit), the worker retries with backoff. After max retries, the row is marked as "embed_failed" — user-visible state. The application can decide to retry, ignore, or delete.
This is a system-level subsystem with its own state. We'll discuss in detail in the embedding lifecycle section. The transaction-level point: embedding generation is outside the user's transaction. Commits are fast; embeddings backfill.
The alternative: configurable per-table
Some tables might want inline embedding (low-volume, high-importance — the user wants the embedding before commit returns). Others want deferred (high-volume, OK with lag).
Schema option: WITH (embedding_mode = 'inline' | 'deferred') at table level. Default deferred. Inline available for users who need it.
Crash recovery
What happens on crash with active transactions?
The WAL has commit records for committed transactions, no commit record for in-flight ones.
Recovery:

Replay WAL from last checkpoint
Rebuild memtable from WAL records
For each transaction with COMMIT in WAL: mark committed in commit log, resolve xmin in memtable
For each transaction without COMMIT: treated as aborted; their rows in rebuilt memtable are filtered (xmin = aborted txn_id is invisible)

Memtable rebuild includes all writes that reached WAL before crash, including from aborted transactions. We don't try to skip them at replay time — easier to include them and filter via visibility.
After recovery, abort records can be eagerly applied to drop aborted rows from memtable — saves space. Not required for correctness but good housekeeping.
Connection model
A client connection holds a session. A session holds transaction state:

Current transaction (if any)
Isolation level
Pending writes
Snapshot LSN
Cursor positions

If a connection drops mid-transaction: the transaction is aborted by the server. Cleanup happens on connection close detection.
Idle transactions are tracked. Sessions idle in transaction for > N minutes get their transactions aborted (forces them to retry but prevents GC barrier hold).
Honest tradeoffs
Snapshot Isolation, not Serializable: we accept write skew. Real but rare. Documented.
Eager xmin resolution at commit: extra commit-time work for fast reads. Right tradeoff for our workload.
Optimistic conflict detection: commits can fail; clients must retry. Standard for SI systems. Adds complexity to client code (retry loops) but allows high concurrency.
Long-running transaction abortion: snapshots can't be held forever; long-analytical-query users get aborted. Mitigation: increase max duration per session if needed.
Deferred embedding generation: inserts return before embedding ready. Vector queries miss recently-inserted rows. For most workloads acceptable; some users need inline mode.
Auto-commit DDL: not Postgres-compatible. Multi-statement DDL transactions don't work. Tradeoff for v1 simplicity.
Three places to push on
SI vs SSI: I argued SI for v1. Some workloads need true serializability and write skew bites them. SSI adds 10-20% overhead but provides safety. Reasonable to argue v1 should ship SSI if we're targeting production OLTP-like workloads. I leaned simpler.
Deferred embedding as default: I argued deferred. The cost: vector queries on freshly-inserted rows miss them. For agentic workflows where an agent inserts then immediately queries, this might be surprising. Inline-by-default with deferred-as-option is the alternative. My instinct says deferred matches typical bulk-ingest workloads but agentic workflows are real and want inline.
Read Committed as default isolation: I made it default for Postgres compatibility. Some teams argue SI should be default because it's stronger and our architecture supports it natively. I leaned Postgres-default for familiarity; reasonable to argue for SI-by-default since we get it for free.

Locking transactions and isolation:

Snapshot Isolation as primary isolation level, Read Committed as session option, Read Committed as default
No Serializable in v1 (SSI deferred to v2)
LSN-based snapshots; commit_lsn assigned at commit; in-memory commit log for active transactions
Eager xmin resolution at commit: write set walked at commit to replace txn_id with commit_lsn
Optimistic concurrency control: first-committer-wins on row conflicts; second commit fails with serialization error
Write set tracked per transaction; commit-time conflict check with sharded row locks
PK uniqueness enforced at commit, not insert
Read-your-writes guaranteed within transaction
Long-running transaction protection: configurable max duration (1hr default), auto-abort
Auto-commit DDL in v1; transactional DDL in v2
Deferred embedding generation by default; inline mode as opt-in
Pending embedding rows committed with null embedding; background worker populates
Connection-bound sessions; transaction abort on connection drop


