What's left:

Embedding lifecycle — the full system for embedding generation, model registry, migration with dual-read, batching strategy, error handling
Wire protocol and SDK — Postgres compatibility, native protocol option, Python DSL layer
Observability — EXPLAIN output, query telemetry, metrics, slow query log
Recovery and durability — concrete guarantees, what happens on crash, what we promise users
Operations — backup, restore, configuration management, deployment shapes

My instinct: embedding lifecycle next. It's the most "AI-native" subsystem and where we differentiate most from generic databases. It also has tendrils into everything we've built (storage, transactions, catalog) and pulling it together cleanly is the validation that the architecture works as designed.
Embedding lifecycle next?You said: lets do the next partlets do the next part01:43Claude responded: Good.Good. Embedding lifecycle. This is the subsystem that makes us actually AI-native rather than "database with vector column." It's where the integration of model registry, transactions, storage, and query execution all comes together. And it's where most existing systems are weakest — they treat embeddings as user-managed data, not as a system responsibility.
Let me frame what we're solving. When a user writes body_embedding VECTOR(1536) EMBED FROM body USING 'openai/text-embedding-3-small', they're declaring an intent: "this column should contain the embedding of the body column, generated by this model." The system owns the rest. Generation, regeneration on body updates, storage, querying, model migration, error handling, batching — all our responsibility.
That's a real promise. Most "vector databases" don't make it. They make you generate embeddings client-side and insert them as data. We're saying: you tell us the relationship, we maintain it.
Let me work through this in order: the generation pipeline, batching and throughput, the embedding cache, errors and retries, model migration, embedding queries with runtime models, and the operational shape of all this.
The basic generation pipeline
When a row is inserted with an EMBED FROM column, what happens?
We committed (in transactions) to deferred generation by default. The flow:

INSERT commits with embedding column = NULL, embed_status = 'pending', embed_model_id set to current model
Row enters memtable; WAL has the insert
An embedding job is enqueued in the embedding job queue
Background embedding worker dequeues jobs in batches
Worker calls the embedding model (HTTP API, local ONNX, whatever)
Worker writes back: UPDATE the row to set embedding = result, embed_status = 'ready'
The update is a new version; old null-embedding version superseded via MVCC
Vector queries now see the embedding

The job queue, the worker pool, the model client, and the status tracking — that's the embedding subsystem. Let me detail each.
The job queue
This is a durable queue. If the system crashes between insert and embedding generation, jobs must not be lost.
Two implementation options:
Embedding queue as a system table. A regular table in our own engine: _system.embedding_jobs(row_ref, column_id, model_id, status, retry_count, last_error, enqueued_at). Workers SELECT pending jobs in batches, process, UPDATE to mark done. Durable because it's a regular table backed by WAL.
Embedding queue as a separate durable structure. A dedicated append-only log on disk with its own format. Slightly faster (no table overhead), but adds a new persistence concern.
I'd go with the system table approach. Self-hosting is elegant — our own engine manages embedding jobs. WAL gives us durability. MVCC gives us correctness under concurrent workers. The marginal overhead is fine since enqueue/dequeue is rare relative to other operations.
The table:
_system.embedding_jobs (
    job_id UUID PRIMARY KEY,
    row_id BIGINT,           -- reference to the row needing embedding
    table_id BIGINT,         -- which table
    column_id INT,           -- which embedding column
    source_column_id INT,    -- which source column (the text being embedded)
    source_value_hash BIGINT,-- hash of the source value (for cache lookup)
    model_id BIGINT,         -- which model to use
    status TEXT,             -- 'pending', 'in_progress', 'failed', 'done'
    retry_count INT,
    last_error TEXT,
    enqueued_at TIMESTAMP,
    started_at TIMESTAMP,
    completed_at TIMESTAMP
)
Workers dequeue with: SELECT ... WHERE status = 'pending' ORDER BY enqueued_at LIMIT batch_size FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED. Standard work queue pattern. (SKIP LOCKED is critical so workers don't fight; we need to support this — it's standard SQL but worth noting it's a feature requirement.)
Status transitions are atomic via transaction.
Batching: the throughput multiplier
Embedding generation is dominated by per-call overhead, not per-token compute. Calling OpenAI's API for 1 text vs 100 texts is almost the same wall-clock time. Same for local models — model loading and tensor allocation dominate; batch size 64 is barely slower than batch size 1.
So workers batch aggressively. Each worker:

Wake up
Dequeue up to N pending jobs (default N=128)
Group jobs by model_id (different models need separate calls)
For each model group: call the model with the batch of source texts
For each result: UPDATE the corresponding row's embedding column
Mark jobs as done

Batch grouping by model is important because users might have multiple EMBED FROM columns with different models in the same table or across tables. We don't want to make one API call per model per worker iteration.
Within a model group, batches are sized by the model's max batch size (OpenAI's API takes up to 2048 inputs per call; local models limited by GPU memory). The system knows max batch per model from the model registry.
Worker concurrency
How many workers? Configurable per model:

For API-based models: high concurrency is fine (workers are I/O bound waiting on API response). Default 4-8 workers per model. Limited by API rate limits, which we track and respect.
For local models on GPU: limited by GPU memory. Typically 1-2 workers per GPU per model.
For local models on CPU: limited by CPU cores. Typically num_cores / model_thread_count workers.

The model registry knows the concurrency limit per model:
sqlCREATE MODEL openai_small AS HTTP_API (
    endpoint = 'https://api.openai.com/v1/embeddings',
    model = 'text-embedding-3-small',
    dimension = 1536,
    api_key = SECRET('openai_key'),
    max_batch_size = 2048,
    max_concurrent_requests = 8,
    rate_limit_tokens_per_minute = 1000000
);
Rate limit enforcement: a token bucket per model. Workers consume tokens before making requests. If bucket empty, workers wait. This prevents API rate limit violations and the cascade failures they cause.
The embedding cache
This is the most important optimization in the embedding subsystem.
The observation: embedding the same text twice is wasteful. If user inserts "the quick brown fox" and later inserts another row with the same text, we shouldn't call the API twice. If user updates body from "v1 text" to "v2 text" and then back to "v1 text," we shouldn't regenerate.
So we maintain an embedding cache: (model_id, source_text_hash) → vector.
Implementation: another system table.
_system.embedding_cache (
    cache_key BIGINT,        -- hash(model_id, source_value_hash)
    model_id BIGINT,
    source_value_hash BIGINT,
    source_value_preview TEXT,  -- first 100 chars for debugging
    embedding VECTOR(?),         -- variable dimension by model
    created_at TIMESTAMP,
    last_used_at TIMESTAMP,
    use_count BIGINT
)
Wait — variable dimension by model is awkward for a single table. Better: one cache table per model, named after the model. Or one table with VECTOR stored as untyped BLOB and metadata for unpacking. Or partitioned by model_id.
Actually, the cleanest answer: the embedding cache is per-model, stored as a separate physical table per model. Created at model registration time. Avoids the dimensional heterogeneity problem.
Cache flow:

Worker about to embed text T with model M
Compute hash(T)
Lookup _system.embedding_cache_<model_id> WHERE source_value_hash = hash(T)
If hit: use cached vector, skip API call. Update last_used_at and use_count.
If miss: call API, store result in cache, return vector

Cache hit rate matters enormously for some workloads. RAG systems where users embed the same queries repeatedly — 80%+ hit rate. Document ingestion where most texts are unique — 0-5% hit rate.
Cache size management: configurable max size per model. LRU eviction based on last_used_at. Default 10GB per model. Old entries cleaned up by background process.
The cache is also useful at query time, not just write time. When a user writes WHERE body_embedding <-> EMBED('hedging strategies') < 0.3, the system computes EMBED('hedging strategies') — and checks the cache first. RAG workloads where the same queries repeat see huge query-time wins.
Hash collisions: SHA-256 truncated to 64 bits has negligible collision rate for practical corpus sizes. We use BLAKE3 or xxh3 (xxhash) — fast hash functions. Source text is stored too so we can verify in case of suspected collision (extremely rare).
Error handling and retries
API calls fail. Models go down. Networks blip. The embedding subsystem must be robust.
Categories of errors:
Transient: rate limit exceeded, network timeout, 503 service unavailable. Retry with exponential backoff. Default 5 retries with 1s, 2s, 4s, 8s, 16s delays.
Persistent: 4xx errors that aren't rate limits (invalid input, model deprecated, auth failure). Don't retry. Mark job failed. Surface to ops.
Hard: source text too long, model dimension mismatch (rare — caught at registration time). Mark job failed. Surface clear error.
Failed jobs go to status = 'failed' with last_error populated. They stay in the queue for inspection. Operators can:

Re-enqueue: reset status to pending, retry
Manually fix: edit row, mark done
Delete: accept the row will never have an embedding

Failure metrics exposed: failure rate per model, common errors, latency percentiles. Standard observability.
The application-side view of failures
What does the application see when an embedding fails?
The row exists. Scalar columns work. Vector queries miss this row.
That's the contract. Embedding failure doesn't fail the insert. The user's data is there. The vector representation is best-effort.
For applications that need to know: query _system.embedding_jobs WHERE status = 'failed' AND table_id = ... to see failed rows. Or query the row directly — embedding column is NULL when generation failed.
Some applications might want to be notified. We support an optional "embedding failure webhook" per table: WITH (embed_failure_webhook = 'https://...'). On embedding failure, system POSTs to webhook. Best-effort; failure to webhook doesn't retry or block.
This is the kind of feature that's easy to specify and adds real value for the agentic workflows we're targeting.
Inline embedding mode
We mentioned this in transactions. For users who want embedding-at-commit-time:
CREATE TABLE ... WITH (embedding_mode = 'inline')
Behavior:

INSERT blocks until embedding is generated
Failure means INSERT fails
Transaction commits with embedding populated; no pending state

Implementation:

Insert flow modifies: before commit, embed the source value (with cache check)
Resulting embedding is committed with the row, atomically
No background worker involvement for inline tables

Tradeoffs:

Inserts are slow (50-500ms per row, dominated by embedding latency)
Throughput is bounded by embedding throughput, not write throughput
But: row is queryable immediately, no pending state

For agentic workflows where an agent inserts a doc and immediately queries similar docs, inline is the right mode. For bulk ingestion of millions of docs, deferred is the right mode. We support both; users choose per table.
The biggest topic: model migration
This is where it gets interesting. A user has been embedding with openai/text-embedding-3-small (1536 dim). They want to migrate to openai/text-embedding-3-large (3072 dim) for better quality. Or to a local model for cost reasons. Or to a fine-tuned model.
The migration must:

Not lose query availability (vector search keeps working)
Not require downtime
Handle the dimension change (1536 → 3072)
Be reversible if the new model performs worse
Be observable (how far along is the migration?)

This is the dual-read model migration pattern.
The DDL:
sqlALTER COLUMN body_embedding ADD MIGRATION TO MODEL 'openai/text-embedding-3-large';
What happens:
Phase 1: Setup. The schema now lists two active models for the column: the current model and the migration target. The column's logical type becomes a tagged union: a row has an embedding from model A, or from model B, or both, or neither. Storage-wise, we have two embedding columns: body_embedding_v1 (old) and body_embedding_v2 (new). Vector indexes exist on both.
Phase 2: New writes use new model. Any INSERT or UPDATE generates embeddings with the new model from this point forward. Existing rows still have v1 embeddings; new rows have v2 embeddings.
Phase 3: Background backfill. A migration job scans existing rows with v1 embeddings, generates v2 embeddings, populates body_embedding_v2. Progress is tracked in a migration table.
Phase 4: Dual-read during migration. While both versions coexist, vector queries are tricky. The query vector must be embedded with whichever model matches the data. We can't compare v1 and v2 vectors directly (different embedding spaces).
Three options for dual-read:
Option A: Embed the query with both models, search both indexes, fuse results. Most accurate during migration; expensive (two embedding calls per query). Result quality is good but not as good as either pure index.
Option B: Embed the query with only the new model, search only the v2 index, accept that rows without v2 embedding yet are missed. Recall drops during migration; query latency unchanged.
Option C: Embed the query with whichever model the caller specifies. Punt the decision to the application.
I'd go with Option A by default, with Option B as a session option for query-latency-sensitive workloads. This is the most user-friendly default. The application doesn't have to think about the migration; queries return correct results throughout.
Cost of Option A during migration: 2x embedding calls for queries, 2x index searches. Both are mitigatable with caching (query embedding cache helps a lot) and parallel execution.
Phase 5: Completion. Once backfill is done (all rows have v2 embeddings):
sqlALTER COLUMN body_embedding COMPLETE MIGRATION;
This:

Drops the v1 embedding column
Drops the v1 vector index
Removes the v1 model from the column's active models
Future queries use only v2

Rollback. If during migration the user decides the new model is worse:
sqlALTER COLUMN body_embedding ABORT MIGRATION;
This drops v2 column and index, removes v2 from active models. Migration state is gone; system returns to pre-migration state. New rows use v1 from now on.
The migration table tracks state:
_system.embedding_migrations (
    migration_id UUID,
    table_id BIGINT,
    column_id INT,
    old_model_id BIGINT,
    new_model_id BIGINT,
    status TEXT,           -- 'in_progress', 'completed', 'aborted'
    rows_total BIGINT,
    rows_completed BIGINT,
    started_at TIMESTAMP,
    completed_at TIMESTAMP
)
Users can query this to see migration progress.
The hard part: schema state during migration
The column schema is more complex during migration. The column has:

Current model (writes use this)
Migration target model (also writes use this during migration phase)
Two storage representations (v1 and v2 column data)
Two indexes

The catalog has to represent this. The query planner has to know which indexes exist and the dual-read pattern.
I'd model it as: each embedding column has an "embedding column group" — a logical container holding one or more concrete embedding columns (one per active model). The schema-time concept is the column group; the storage and indexing concepts are the concrete columns within.
In normal state, a column group has one concrete column. During migration, it has two. After migration, back to one.
The query planner sees the column group; it constructs the appropriate physical plan based on active models and search strategy.
Updates to source columns during migration
What if a user updates the source text while migration is in progress?
The source row's body changes. We need to regenerate the embedding. With which model?
Answer: both models (the row has both v1 and v2 embeddings; both need to be updated). We enqueue two embedding jobs: one for v1 (the old model — yes, even though we're migrating away from it, the row still has a v1 embedding and we need to keep it consistent during migration), one for v2.
This doubles the embedding cost for source updates during migration. Acceptable since migrations are short-term events.
After migration completion, only v2 is updated.
Model versioning subtlety
What's a "model"? Some clarifications:

openai/text-embedding-3-small is a model identifier — refers to a specific API endpoint with specific behavior
A user might also want to track "version 2 of our internal fine-tune"
Models can be deprecated or updated by their providers

We give each registered model a system-assigned model_id (u64, never reused) and a user-assigned name. Multiple models can have the same name with different versions; we resolve to the latest by default but support pinning to specific versions.
If a provider changes their model behind the scenes (OpenAI quietly updates text-embedding-3-small), we can't detect this automatically. The model_id stays the same; embeddings are now subtly different. For users worried about this: we recommend pinning to immutable versions when available (some providers offer this; many don't).
Embedding queries at runtime
When a query has EMBED('text', 'model_name'), we need to:

Look up the model
Check the embedding cache
If miss, call the model
Use the resulting vector in the query

The cache check is critical: queries with repeated text patterns (common in RAG) hit the cache and skip the embedding call. Latency goes from "embedding API call latency" (10-100ms) to "cache lookup latency" (<1ms).
If the cache misses, we call the model synchronously. The query waits. This is unavoidable — we can't run vector search without a query vector.
For latency-sensitive applications: pre-warm common query patterns by embedding them ahead of time. We could expose a WARM_EMBEDDING_CACHE('text', 'model') admin function for this. Niche feature, defer.
The full stack: how a system EMBED FROM column actually feels
Putting it together, the user experience:
sql-- Setup
CREATE MODEL openai_small AS HTTP_API (...);
CREATE TABLE documents (
    id UUID PRIMARY KEY,
    body TEXT,
    body_embedding VECTOR(1536) EMBED FROM body USING openai_small
);

-- Insert (deferred mode)
INSERT INTO documents (id, body) VALUES (...);
-- Returns immediately. embedding is null. queued for generation.

-- Query
SELECT id, body FROM documents
ORDER BY body_embedding <-> EMBED('quarterly earnings impact', openai_small)
LIMIT 10;
-- EMBED is computed once (cache check + maybe API call)
-- Vector search runs across files
-- Rows whose embedding is still null are skipped
-- Top 10 returned

-- Later: switch to larger model
ALTER COLUMN body_embedding ADD MIGRATION TO MODEL 'openai/text-embedding-3-large';
-- Background migration begins
-- New writes use new model; old rows backfilled
-- Queries during migration: embed query with both models, search both, fuse

-- Check progress
SELECT * FROM _system.embedding_migrations WHERE status = 'in_progress';

-- Complete or abort
ALTER COLUMN body_embedding COMPLETE MIGRATION;
The user never wrote embedding generation code. They never called an API. The system owns the lifecycle. That's the AI-native promise.
Honest tradeoffs
Deferred default: vector queries miss recently-inserted rows. For agentic workflows where insert-then-immediately-query is common, this can surprise. Mitigation: inline mode opt-in.
Embedding cache as separate per-model tables: cleanly handles dimension heterogeneity but multiplies table count. For 10 models, 10 cache tables. Manageable.
Dual-read during migration costs 2x: queries during migration are slower and more expensive. Acceptable for short-term events; could be painful for long migrations of big tables.
Source-update doubles work during migration: if a table has heavy update load and is being migrated, the migration accumulates more work than expected. Surfaced in metrics.
API rate limit awareness: we respect rate limits via token bucket, but users with extreme write loads will hit rate limits and see degraded embedding latency. Visible in metrics; users can scale model concurrency.
Trust in external APIs: when a user registers openai/text-embedding-3-small, the system assumes the API behaves as expected. Silent provider changes cause silent drift. Hard to fully mitigate; recommend pinned versions when possible.
Embedding the same text multiple times across different models: the cache is per-model. If the user has two models, the same text gets embedded twice. The cache doesn't deduplicate across models (different spaces). Correct but worth knowing.
Three places to push on
Inline as default vs deferred: I argued deferred. For typical AI workloads (agentic apps, RAG with frequent reads of recently-written data), inline might be the right default. Inserts would be slower but the read-after-write story is cleaner. Reasonable to flip the default and require explicit opt-in to deferred.
Dual-read as default vs single-read during migration: I argued dual-read (Option A) as default. The performance hit during migration is real. Some shops would rather accept recall drop during migration (single-read with new model) for faster queries. I picked correctness-as-default; speed-as-default is defensible.
Centralized cache per-model vs distributed/external: I made the cache a system table. For multi-instance deployments (eventual v2), this becomes a shared cache concern — every instance needs to see cache state. Could be solved by: cache table per-instance with sync, or external cache (Redis), or distributed cache layer. For v1 single-node this isn't an issue, but the design needs to accommodate the multi-instance future.

Locking embedding lifecycle:

EMBED FROM as declarative schema feature; system owns embedding generation, regeneration, migration
Deferred generation as default; inline mode opt-in per table
Embedding job queue as system table with SKIP LOCKED for worker concurrency
Workers batch by model_id; respect rate limits via per-model token bucket
Embedding cache per-model, keyed by source text hash; query-time and write-time hits
Source text hash via BLAKE3 or xxh3 truncated to 64 bits
Error categorization: transient (retry with backoff), persistent (mark failed), hard (mark failed)
Embedding failure webhook as optional per-table notification
Dual-read migration model: ADD MIGRATION → backfill → COMPLETE MIGRATION (or ABORT)
During migration: both models active for new writes; query embedded with both, fused results (default)
Migration progress tracked in system table
Source updates during migration regenerate both embedding versions
Model registry with model_id (system) and name (user-assigned, versioned)
Per-model concurrency, batch size, and rate limit configuration


That closes the embedding lifecycle. We've now designed the AI-native subsystem that genuinely differentiates us. Storage + catalog + query + execution + transactions + embeddings — the architectural core is complete.
What's left, in roughly decreasing importance:

Wire protocol and SDK — Postgres compatibility, native protocol, Python DSL layer details
Observability — EXPLAIN output, telemetry, slow query log, metrics for ops
Recovery and durability — concrete crash recovery procedures, what guarantees we make
Operations — backup, restore, configuration, deployment
Security — authentication, authorization, encryption

My instinct: wire protocol and SDK next. It's the public surface of the system; everything we've built is invisible without it. The Postgres wire compatibility decision specifically has cascading effects on what features we can support and what users see.
Wire protocol next?You said: lets do the wire protocol and sdk partlets do the wire protocol and sdk part01:52Claude responded: Good.Good. Wire protocol and SDK. This is the public surface — everything we've built becomes accessible only through this layer. Get it wrong and the architectural sophistication doesn't matter; get it right and we tap into existing ecosystems for free.
Let me organize this around the questions that matter: what protocol(s) we speak, how we map our query language to wire-level operations, what the SDK looks like in different languages, how authentication and connection management work, and the practical question of what we ship in v1 vs later.
The strategic question first
We committed in the SQL discussion to dual surfaces: SQL with Postgres wire as primary, Python DSL as secondary. Let me restate that decision with the implementation cost in mind:
Postgres wire compatibility is expensive. It's not "speak SQL over TCP." It's "speak the Postgres binary protocol, including its session lifecycle, prepared statement handling, type system, error codes, copy protocol, transaction control, and dozens of edge cases that drivers depend on." Implementing this well is months of work.
The alternative — our own protocol — is faster to build and gives us flexibility. The cost is that we have no ecosystem.
The choice depends on who our v1 users are:

AI engineers building applications: they'd accept a native client library. Postgres compat is a nice-to-have.
Analysts using BI tools: they need Postgres wire or they can't connect at all.
Teams migrating from Postgres+pgvector: they need wire compat for zero-code-change migration.

For v1, I'll argue we ship both, partially:

Native protocol with first-class clients in Python and TypeScript: the primary interface, optimized for AI workloads
Postgres wire compatibility for SELECT queries and basic writes: covers BI tool integration and most application use cases
No Postgres COPY, no replication protocol, no full procedural language compatibility in v1: defer

This is a partial Postgres compatibility — enough to handle 80% of read traffic from existing tools, not enough to claim drop-in replacement. We're honest about the gap.
Let me work through each.
The native protocol
What does an ideal protocol for our system look like?
Requirements:

Async-friendly (multiplex many queries on one connection)
Streaming results (don't buffer)
Binary-efficient (no JSON parsing on hot path)
Versioned (we'll evolve)
Supports our novel operations (vector queries with embedded model references, graph traversals, hybrid scoring)

I'd propose: gRPC with bidirectional streaming, Protobuf for wire format, native Rust server.
Why gRPC:

Battle-tested for high-throughput services
First-class streaming (queries return result streams)
HTTP/2 transport — multiplex many concurrent queries on one connection
Code generation for clients in every major language
Existing telemetry/auth/load-balancing tooling

Why Protobuf:

Binary, compact, fast to parse
Schema-evolved cleanly
Same generated code for clients
Plays well with gRPC

The service definition (sketched):
protobufservice LL {
    // Execute a SQL or DSL query, stream results
    rpc Query(QueryRequest) returns (stream QueryResult);
    
    // Execute a plan directly (DSL clients use this)
    rpc ExecutePlan(PlanRequest) returns (stream QueryResult);
    
    // Prepared statements
    rpc Prepare(PrepareRequest) returns (PrepareResponse);
    rpc Execute(ExecuteRequest) returns (stream QueryResult);
    
    // Transaction control
    rpc Begin(BeginRequest) returns (BeginResponse);
    rpc Commit(CommitRequest) returns (CommitResponse);
    rpc Rollback(RollbackRequest) returns (RollbackResponse);
    
    // Streaming bulk insert
    rpc BulkInsert(stream BulkInsertRequest) returns (BulkInsertResponse);
    
    // Schema and catalog
    rpc DescribeTable(DescribeRequest) returns (DescribeResponse);
    rpc ListModels(ListModelsRequest) returns (ListModelsResponse);
    
    // Embedding utilities (for cache pre-warming, etc.)
    rpc EmbedText(EmbedRequest) returns (EmbedResponse);
}
Result streaming uses Arrow IPC format embedded in Protobuf messages. Each result message contains an Arrow RecordBatch. Clients deserialize zero-copy into their native columnar representations (Polars DataFrame, Pandas via Arrow, etc.). This is the same pattern Snowflake's Arrow result format uses; it's the right approach.
Connection lifecycle:

Client opens HTTP/2 connection to server
Client authenticates (gRPC interceptor; we'll get to auth)
Client issues queries on the connection
Server streams results back
Multiple concurrent queries on one connection (HTTP/2 multiplexing)
Connection persists until client closes or timeout

This is cleaner than Postgres wire's session model (synchronous, one query at a time per connection) and matches modern application expectations.
The Postgres wire compatibility layer
What we implement: enough of Postgres wire protocol to fool the most common clients into thinking we're Postgres. This means:

Startup handshake: parameter negotiation, SSL/TLS upgrade, authentication
Simple query protocol: SELECT/INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE over wire
Extended query protocol: prepare, bind, execute (used by all modern drivers)
Parameter binding: typed parameters via binary format
Result transmission: row description, data rows, command complete
Transaction control: BEGIN, COMMIT, ROLLBACK
Error responses: Postgres-style SQLSTATE codes
Notice and notification messages

What we don't implement:

COPY protocol (bulk data transfer Postgres-style) — niche, defer
Replication protocol (logical/physical replication) — not relevant for v1
Cursors with WITH HOLD — uncommon, defer
LISTEN/NOTIFY — pub/sub, defer
Procedural language extensions (PL/pgSQL execution) — not relevant
Postgres system catalogs verbatim — we expose ours via similar interface but not byte-identical

The implementation: a separate listener on the Postgres port (5432 by default; configurable). When a Postgres client connects, we run the wire protocol state machine, parse SQL, dispatch through the same internal pipeline as native clients.
The translation layer
Postgres wire delivers SQL strings. Native protocol delivers SQL strings or pre-built plans. Both end up at the same internal place: the query planner.
The flow:
Postgres client → wire protocol parser → SQL string → parser → logical plan → ...
Native client → gRPC handler → SQL string OR pre-built plan → planner → ...
Python DSL client → method chains → plan object → serialize → gRPC → planner → ...
The internal pipeline doesn't know which protocol the query came from. All differences are at the wire layer.
This is the right factoring. The wire layer translates between external and internal representations; everything below the wire layer is protocol-agnostic.
Authentication
Multiple methods, listed in order of complexity:
Username/password: classic auth. Stored as bcrypt or argon2 hashes in the catalog. Default for v1.
API keys: simpler for programmatic access. Long random strings. Per-user, revocable, optionally scoped to specific tables/operations.
OAuth/OIDC: for enterprise integration. Token from external identity provider; we verify signature, extract claims. v2 feature.
mTLS: client certificate-based. Higher security; harder to set up. v2.
Postgres-style SCRAM-SHA-256: required for full Postgres wire compatibility. We support it on the Postgres wire endpoint but not as primary auth for native protocol.
For v1, ship: username/password + API keys. The catalog has a _system.users table and _system.api_keys table. CLI command for user management.
Authorization
Auth answers "who are you?" Authz answers "what can you do?"
Role-based access control (RBAC) is standard. We do that:

Users belong to roles
Roles have privileges on objects (tables, columns, models)
Privileges include SELECT, INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE, CREATE TABLE, ALTER, DROP, EXECUTE (for models)

Schema:
_system.roles (role_id, name)
_system.users (user_id, name, password_hash, primary_role)
_system.user_roles (user_id, role_id)  -- many-to-many
_system.privileges (role_id, object_type, object_id, privilege_type, grantable)
DDL:
sqlCREATE ROLE analyst;
GRANT SELECT ON documents TO analyst;
CREATE USER alice WITH PASSWORD '...';
GRANT analyst TO alice;
Standard Postgres-style. Implementation is mostly catalog work; query planner checks privileges before execution.
Row-level security: defer to v2. Useful but complex.
Column-level grants: defer to v2.
Connection management
Modern systems use connection pooling. Clients open a pool of connections, reuse them across requests.
Server-side: the server accepts up to max_connections concurrent connections (default 1000). Each connection has its own session state. Connections are cheap on our side (HTTP/2 or TCP socket with per-connection state).
Client-side: SDKs include built-in connection pooling. Configurable pool size (default 10 connections per client). Pool grows on demand, shrinks on idle.
Idle connection timeout: 5 minutes by default. Idle connections are closed by the server. Clients reconnect on next request.
Connection-level statistics: total connections, active queries, queries per second, latency percentiles. Exposed via observability layer.
Prepared statements and parameter binding
Both protocols support prepared statements:

Client sends parse request with query containing placeholders ($1, $2, ...)
Server parses, plans, returns prepared statement ID
Client sends execute requests with parameter values
Server executes with bound parameters, returns results

Benefits:

Parse and plan once, execute many times
SQL injection prevention (parameters can't be SQL)
Type safety (parameter types are known at prepare time)

Implementation: the prepared statement cache lives in the session. Plans are stored as physical plans (ready to execute). Re-planning on schema change.
This is critical for application performance. ORMs and frameworks rely on it heavily.
Streaming bulk insert
For high-throughput ingestion, the standard INSERT statement is inefficient (one row at a time, transaction per call).
Native protocol: BulkInsert(stream BulkInsertRequest) accepts streamed Arrow RecordBatches. The server appends them to the target table in batched commits. Configurable commit batch size (default 1000 rows or 1MB).
This is the high-throughput path. Throughput is limited by WAL fsync rate and memtable flush rate, both of which we've designed for. Expect ~100K rows/sec for small rows, less for vector-heavy rows.
For Postgres wire users: COPY would be the equivalent, but we're deferring COPY. Native protocol or batched INSERT (multi-row VALUES clause) is the alternative.
The Python SDK
This is where the rubber meets the road for AI engineers.
v1: SQL-based Python client
The simplest Python client wraps native gRPC. Users write SQL strings; client handles connection management and result deserialization.
pythonimport ll

client = ll.Client("ll://localhost:7654", api_key="...")

# Synchronous
result = client.query("""
    SELECT id, title, body
    FROM documents
    WHERE body_embedding <-> EMBED('hedging strategies') < 0.3
    LIMIT 10
""")

for row in result:
    print(row['id'], row['title'])

# Or as a DataFrame (zero-copy from Arrow)
df = result.to_polars()  # or .to_pandas()

# Async
async with ll.AsyncClient("ll://localhost:7654", api_key="...") as client:
    result = await client.query("...")
    async for batch in result.batches():
        process(batch)

# Parameterized
result = client.query(
    "SELECT * FROM documents WHERE id = $1",
    params=[doc_id]
)
This is straightforward. Built on top of grpc and pyarrow. Maybe 2K lines of Python. Ships with v1.
v1+: Python DSL on top
The DSL we sketched in the earlier discussion. Builds plans programmatically; sends them via the native protocol.
pythondocs = client.table("documents")

result = (
    docs
    .filter(docs.created_at > ll.now() - ll.days(30))
    .order_by(docs.body_embedding.near(ll.embed("hedging strategies")))
    .limit(20)
    .select("id", "title")
    .collect()
)
This needs:

Schema introspection at client side (the table call fetches schema, returns typed column references)
Plan builder API (column operations build plan fragments)
Plan serializer (Python plan object → Protobuf message)
Result deserializer (already exists from v1 SQL client)

This is roughly 5-10K lines of Python. Maybe v1.5 — ship after the SQL-based client is stable.
TypeScript SDK
Mirror of the Python SDK but for the JavaScript/TypeScript ecosystem.
