The Rust client is the most efficient (no FFI overhead, direct Arrow handling). Used by Rust applications and as the foundation for other language bindings (Python via PyO3, Node.js via napi-rs).
Actually, this raises a good architectural point: build the Rust client first, wrap it for Python and TypeScript. The wrapping path:

Rust client: pure Rust, async, fast
Python: PyO3 bindings around the Rust client; Arrow conversion via pyarrow
TypeScript: Wasm or napi-rs bindings around the Rust client; Arrow conversion via apache-arrow

This gives us: one set of protocol logic to maintain, optimal performance everywhere, less code to write.
Alternative: hand-write each SDK in its native language using pure gRPC. More idiomatic per language, more code, more places for bugs to hide.
I lean toward the wrap-the-Rust-client approach for SDKs beyond the prototype phase. v1 ships the Rust client and the Python/TS wrappers.
Error model
What does an error look like across protocols?
Categories:

Protocol errors (malformed request, version mismatch)
Authentication errors
Authorization errors
Query errors (parse, plan, execution)
Resource errors (out of memory, disk full)
Conflict errors (transaction serialization failure)
Embedding errors (model unavailable, rate limit)

Each error carries:

A code (machine-readable, stable across versions)
A message (human-readable, may include context)
Optional details (query position for parse errors, conflicting row for serialization errors, etc.)
A category (transient/permanent indicator for retry logic)

On Postgres wire: errors use Postgres SQLSTATE codes. Native protocol: errors use our codes (with mapping to SQLSTATE for shared categories).
Critical for retry logic: clients need to know which errors are transient (retry with backoff) vs permanent (don't retry). We include this metadata in error responses.
Telemetry: query metadata in responses
Every query response includes metadata:

Query ID (UUID, for tracing)
Execution time breakdown (planning, execution, network)
Rows examined, rows returned, rows filtered
Memory peak, spill bytes (if any)
Cache hit rates (buffer pool, embedding cache)
Files accessed
Operators in plan and their costs

This is for observability — clients can log it, monitoring systems can aggregate it. EXPLAIN is the explicit interface; this is the implicit one.
Versioning
The wire protocols and SDKs need versioning.
Approach:

Native gRPC: each service method has a version in the request; server supports last N versions
Postgres wire: implement against PG 15+ protocol version (currently 3.0, stable)
SDKs: semver. Major version bumps for breaking changes (we'll try hard to avoid these).

Compatibility window: support last 3 major versions of clients. After that, force upgrade. This is normal but worth stating.
Honest tradeoffs
Dual-protocol implementation: maintaining both native gRPC and Postgres wire is real work. Probably 2-3 months of dev for v1. The benefit is real (ecosystem access + native efficiency) but it's the kind of thing that competes with other work.
Postgres wire is a leaky abstraction: we'll be "Postgres-compatible" but not bug-for-bug compatible. There will be edge cases where pgvector queries work and ours don't, or where Postgres behaviors differ. Documenting the gaps honestly is important; pretending we're 100% compatible would burn users.
Native protocol means another thing to learn: users have one more API to understand. We mitigate with good SDKs and docs, but it's friction.
Arrow IPC dependency: clients need apache-arrow library (Python, JS, Rust all have it). Adds dependency weight to client SDKs. Acceptable for our target users.
Bulk insert without COPY: Postgres wire users have to use INSERT (slower) or switch to native protocol for bulk loads. Mitigation: clear docs and tooling for migration.
Three places to push on
gRPC vs custom protocol: I argued gRPC. The alternative is a custom protocol — slightly more efficient, slightly less ecosystem. For our scale and use case, gRPC's tooling and language support dominates. But teams who want to optimize hot paths might find gRPC's overhead noticeable; could be argued.
Postgres wire in v1: I argued for partial Postgres wire support. The alternative is skipping it in v1 and adding in v2. Skipping in v1 lets us ship faster but means no BI tool integration at launch. For a database product, that's a real positioning problem. I think we ship it in v1; reasonable to defer.
Build SDKs by wrapping Rust client vs native per language: I argued wrap-the-Rust-client. Native per-language gives better ergonomics and idiom; wrapping gives consistency and less code. For 2-3 languages, wrapping wins. For 10 languages, native might. I lean wrap; reasonable to argue native for the few primary languages.

Locking wire protocol and SDK:

Native protocol: gRPC with bidirectional streaming, Protobuf wire format, Arrow IPC for result data
Postgres wire compatibility: simple + extended query protocols, transaction control, prepared statements, error mapping; defer COPY, replication, full procedural language
Internal pipeline protocol-agnostic; wire layers translate to logical plan/SQL
Authentication: username/password + API keys in v1; OAuth/mTLS in v2
Authorization: RBAC with privileges on tables/columns/models
Connection management: HTTP/2 multiplexing, configurable pools, idle timeouts
Streaming results via Arrow RecordBatches embedded in protocol messages
Prepared statements supported in both protocols
Streaming bulk insert in native protocol; INSERT-based for Postgres wire
SDKs: build Rust client as foundation; wrap for Python (PyO3) and TypeScript (napi-rs)
v1: SQL-based client APIs; DSL fluent API in v1.5+
Error model with stable codes, retry hints, SQLSTATE mapping
Query response metadata for observability
Semver for SDKs; support last 3 major client versions


That closes wire protocol and SDK. We've now covered the full stack: storage + catalog + query + execution + transactions + embeddings + wire protocol/SDK. Everything a user interacts with is designed.
What's left:

Observability — EXPLAIN output, query telemetry, slow query log, metrics, distributed tracing
Recovery and durability — concrete crash recovery procedure, durability semantics, fsync policies
Operations — backup, restore, configuration, deployment shapes, monitoring
Security — authentication details, authorization, encryption-at-rest, audit logging
Distribution — the big one we've deferred throughout

My instinct: observability next. It's small but essential — a production database without good observability is unsupportable. After that, recovery/durability + operations together form the "running it in production" story.
Distribution is a major architectural topic we've been deferring; I'd argue we tackle it last, after all the single-node design is locked.
