What's there that matters:

Operator name: which physical operator
Cost estimate vs actual: explicit comparison so users see misestimates
Strategy choice (for operators with variants): which Pre/Post/Integrated variant chose
Selectivity estimate vs actual: critical for diagnosing planner misjudgments
File pruning stats: how many files we skipped, how many we touched
Cache behavior: buffer pool hit rates per operator
Distance computation count: for vector ops, the dominant cost
Memory and timing: standard

What I want to highlight: showing estimated vs actual side by side for cardinality and selectivity is what makes EXPLAIN useful. Most databases show one or the other; the diff is the diagnostic signal. If estimate was 1000 and actual was 100000, the planner picked the wrong plan and that's the place to focus.
We surface the strategy choice for operators that have multiple physical implementations. "I chose PredicatedVectorSearch with strategy=IntegratedFilteredHNSW because selectivity was estimated at 3.4%." This is the kind of transparency that makes the system tunable rather than mystical.
