Telemetry overhead
How much does observability cost?

Per-query metadata collection: ~5-10μs overhead per query. Negligible.
Metrics histogram updates: ~1μs per update.
Trace span creation (when sampled): ~10μs per span.
Slow query log write: I/O cost only when triggered (rare).
System table inserts (audit log, query history): small WAL overhead.

Total: low single-digit percent of execution time. Acceptable.
For high-throughput workloads, we let users tune sampling and detail levels. Production-grade defaults are conservative (1% trace sampling, query history with hourly aggregation, etc.).
Honest tradeoffs
System table queries through main engine: introspection queries compete with user queries for resources. For a database under load, querying _system.recent_queries might be slow. Standard problem; not specific to us. Mitigation: dedicated thread pool for system queries (small) or recommend running heavy introspection during off-hours.
Telemetry storage growth: query log files, audit log, query stats accumulate. We rotate and compress, but operators need retention policies. Default: 30 days query log, 1 year audit log, query stats aggregated indefinitely. Configurable.
Cardinality of metrics: per-table per-user metrics multiply quickly. We have to be careful what we expose with what labels. Prometheus best practices: keep label cardinality bounded. We follow them.
OTel maturity: OpenTelemetry has had ecosystem churn over the years. The standards are settled now but library quality varies. We use the official Rust OTel libraries; expect occasional friction.
EXPLAIN complexity: rich EXPLAIN output is useful but can be overwhelming. Default to short form; require ANALYZE/VERBOSE for detail. Don't drown beginners.
Three places to push on
All-system-tables vs separate observability backend: I argued everything queryable as system tables. The alternative: ship observability data to a separate system (TSDB for metrics, log aggregator for logs). Pros of separate: doesn't load the primary engine. Cons: more infrastructure, harder to correlate. For v1 single-node, system tables is cleaner. For distributed v2, separate backend might be necessary. Reasonable to argue either way.
Trace sampling default of 1%: I went conservative. AI/observability tools often push higher (10-100%) for richer data. The cost-benefit depends on workload volume. Configurable, so it's fine, but the default sends a message about expected production behavior.
Audit log scope: I included it in v1. Some teams argue audit is a v2 feature — adds complexity, needed only for regulated customers. I lean ship-from-day-one because retrofitting auditing is painful and customers want it for compliance. Reasonable to defer.

