Recovery and durability next?You said: yes lets do ityes lets do it01:58Claude responded: Good.Good. Recovery and durability. This is one of those areas where the difference between a database and a "data store that mostly works" lives in the details. We've been informal throughout — "WAL fsync on commit," "memtable rebuilds from WAL on recovery" — and now we make it precise. The promises we make to users are made here.
Let me work through this in order: what we actually promise (durability semantics), how we achieve it (fsync discipline), what crash recovery looks like in detail, what edge cases break things, and finally backup/restore mechanics which are recovery's natural extension.
The promise: what durability means for us
A database's durability guarantee is a contract: "if I returned commit success and the data isn't here, that's a bug, not user error." Setting this precisely matters.
Our contract:
Acknowledged writes survive process crashes. If we returned commit success and the process is killed (kill -9, OOM kill, segfault), the write is recoverable on restart.
Acknowledged writes survive power loss. If we returned commit success and the machine loses power, the write is recoverable on restart, assuming the underlying storage hardware honors fsync.
Acknowledged writes survive single-disk corruption. If a sector goes bad after a successful write, our checksums detect it; recovery surfaces the failure clearly rather than silently corrupting.
Acknowledged writes do not survive: catastrophic hardware failure (disk destruction), multiple simultaneous failures, lying storage hardware that ignores fsync, filesystem corruption beneath us. These require backup/restore or replication (v2).
This is the standard single-node durability story. Postgres makes the same promises. We honor the same constraints.
The fsync discipline
fsync() is the system call that asks the OS to flush data to durable storage. Without it, writes might live only in OS cache — fast, but lost on crash.
Where we fsync:
WAL writes at commit: this is the durability moment. A commit returns success only after the WAL fsync completes. Everything else can be lazy.
File close after flush: when a memtable flushes to a new file, the file is fsynced before we update the catalog to reference it. Otherwise a crash could leave a catalog pointer to a half-written file.
Catalog updates: redb handles its own fsync internally. We trust redb's transaction model.
Compaction output: same as memtable flush — fsync the new files before swapping the catalog.
Where we don't fsync:
Memtable updates: lives in RAM; durability comes from the WAL, not the memtable itself.
Buffer pool dirty pages: trick question — buffer pool pages are read-only (immutable files), no dirty pages exist.
Reads: never trigger fsync.
The WAL fsync is the only hot-path fsync. Everything else is background and amortized.
Group commit revisited
We mentioned this in the WAL discussion; let me make the mechanism precise because it's the throughput bottleneck.
A naive WAL fsync per commit limits throughput to 1 / fsync_latency. On modern NVMe, fsync is ~100μs; that's 10K commits/sec max. Not enough for high-throughput workloads.
Group commit:

Commits write their WAL records to an in-memory buffer
Commits join the "current commit group" — a list of pending commits
A dedicated WAL writer thread:

Picks up the current group (taking a batch of commits)
Writes all their WAL records to disk
Issues one fsync
On fsync completion, notifies all commits in the group that they're durable


Each commit in the group sees its commit return after the group's fsync completes

The math: with 100 concurrent commits batched together, we do 1 fsync for 100 commits. Throughput becomes 100 / fsync_latency = 1M commits/sec on NVMe.
Latency per commit: slightly higher than 1 fsync because of group formation wait (typically <1ms). For high-throughput workloads this is a clear win.
The implementation: lock-free MPSC queue (multi-producer, single-consumer) where commits push their WAL records; the WAL writer thread is the consumer. Commits block on a per-commit completion notification until the WAL writer signals them.
Tuning: group size has a sweet spot. Too small (1-2 commits) and no batching benefit. Too large (10000 commits) and group formation time exceeds the savings. We adaptive: WAL writer waits up to N microseconds for more commits (configurable, default 100μs); commits the group either when N microseconds pass or when group fills (configurable max group size).
The WAL file structure
WAL is a sequence of records appended to one or more files:
[file header: magic, version, base_lsn]
[record: lsn, txn_id, type, payload, crc32]
[record: ...]
...
[file footer: written at fsync; last_record_lsn]
Records are variable length, prefixed with a length header. CRC32 per record for corruption detection.
Files rotate at size threshold (default 64MB). New writes go to a new file; old files retained until checkpointed (we'll get to checkpoints).
The file naming: monotonically increasing IDs, wal_000001.log, wal_000002.log, etc. Easy to reason about, easy to enumerate, easy to clean up.
The WAL record types
Logical logging means WAL records describe what changed, not how it changed at the page level. Record types:
INSERT_ROW { table_id, row_id, column_values }
UPDATE_ROW { table_id, row_id, column_values, prev_xmax_lsn }
DELETE_ROW { table_id, row_id, prev_xmax_lsn }
COMMIT { txn_id, commit_lsn }
ABORT { txn_id }
SCHEMA_CHANGE { details }
CHECKPOINT { lsn, memtable_state, file_list_snapshot }
COMPACTION_COMMIT { input_files, output_files }
FILE_REGISTER { file_id, table_id, lsn_range }
FILE_DELETE { file_id }
The high-frequency types are INSERT_ROW, UPDATE_ROW, DELETE_ROW, COMMIT. Others are rare administrative events.
Each record carries the LSN assigned at write time. LSNs are monotonically increasing across all records (one global sequence per database).
Checkpoints: making recovery bounded
Without checkpoints, recovery has to replay the entire WAL from the beginning of time. As the WAL grows, recovery time grows. Unacceptable.
A checkpoint marks a point at which all data before it is fully durable in files (not just in WAL). After a checkpoint, WAL records before the checkpoint LSN can be discarded.
Checkpoint procedure:

Mark checkpoint start LSN
Force flush all memtables to files (write rows, build indexes, fsync files)
Update catalog to reference new files
Mark checkpoint complete LSN
Write CHECKPOINT record to WAL with the checkpoint LSN
fsync the WAL
WAL files containing only records before the checkpoint can now be deleted

Recovery only needs to replay from the most recent CHECKPOINT record forward.
Checkpoint frequency tradeoffs:

Frequent (every few minutes): short recovery time, more flush overhead
Infrequent (every hour): less overhead, longer recovery time

Default: checkpoint every 5 minutes or when WAL exceeds 1GB, whichever comes first. Configurable.
Checkpoint is a background operation. It doesn't block writes — new writes during checkpoint go to a new memtable.
The full crash recovery procedure
What actually happens when the process restarts after a crash?
Phase 1: Open the catalog.

Open the redb catalog file
Read table list, file list, model registry, etc. into memory
Check for consistency (catalog file integrity)

Phase 2: Find the recovery starting point.

Scan the WAL directory for files
Read the most recent CHECKPOINT record from WAL
Recovery starts at the CHECKPOINT's start_lsn

Phase 3: Identify active transactions at crash time.

Scan from checkpoint forward
Build a set of all transactions seen
For each, track whether we saw a COMMIT or ABORT record
Transactions with COMMIT: committed; their writes are durable
Transactions with ABORT or no completion record: aborted; their writes are dead

Phase 4: Replay the WAL.

For each record from checkpoint forward:

If it's a write (INSERT_ROW, UPDATE_ROW, DELETE_ROW):

Look up the transaction's status (committed or aborted)
If aborted: skip (don't apply)
If committed: replay into the memtable, including resolving xmin to the commit_lsn
If still active at end of WAL: treat as aborted (won't have a commit record)


If it's a schema change, file register, compaction commit: apply to catalog


Build the memtable as if the writes happened normally

Phase 5: Verify file integrity.

For each file referenced in catalog: open it, verify footer checksum
Files that fail verification: mark as suspect, surface error
Suspect files can be recovered from backup or manually intervened with

Phase 6: Resume operations.

Open network listeners
Begin accepting connections
Background processes (compaction, embedding workers) start

Total recovery time: dominated by Phase 4 (WAL replay). For 1GB of WAL containing 1M records, replay is ~5-10 seconds on modern hardware. For 10GB of WAL, ~1 minute. Bounded by checkpoint frequency.
The torn-write problem
Disk hardware writes in sectors (typically 512B or 4KB). If a write is in progress and power is lost, the sector may be partially updated — "torn." Half the new data, half the old data, corruption.
Two situations to handle:
WAL records crossing sector boundaries. A 1KB WAL record may span two sectors. If only one is written before crash, recovery has half a record.
Our defense: CRC32 per record. On replay, if CRC doesn't match, the record is corrupt. We stop replay at the first corrupt record (it must be the last record, since all previous ones were checksummed successfully). All transactions after the corrupt record are aborted.
This is correct: we never partially apply a corrupted record. Standard pattern (Postgres uses CRC32, RocksDB uses CRC32C, etc.).
Data file pages with torn writes. A page in a column chunk file is, say, 16KB. The disk writes it in 4 sectors. If power fails partway through, the page is torn.
We've designed files to be immutable — once written, never modified. The only torn-write risk is during the initial write of a file. Our procedure:

Write the file to a temporary name (e.g., file_abc.tmp)
Fsync the file
Rename to the final name (atomic on POSIX filesystems)
Update catalog to reference the new file
Fsync the directory

If a crash happens between steps 1-3, the temporary file is incomplete; we delete it on startup. If between 3-4, the file exists but isn't catalog-referenced; we delete it on startup (or alternatively, we could detect it via metadata and recover, but simpler to just clean up).
After step 4, the file is committed and torn writes within it can't happen because we never modify it again.
This works on filesystems that honor atomic rename (most POSIX filesystems do). On filesystems that don't (like some older NFS variants), we'd need additional protection. We document the requirement.
Lying storage hardware
Some hardware lies about fsync. Cheap consumer drives sometimes return success on fsync before the data actually hits the platters; if power fails in the window, data is lost despite our fsync.
This is fundamentally outside our protection. Our promise is "if fsync truly succeeds, the data is durable." If the hardware lies, we can't help.
Mitigation:

We document that we assume honest fsync
We recommend enterprise SSDs with power-loss protection (capacitors that flush volatile cache on power loss)
We test against known-honest storage in our CI
We provide a diagnostic tool that tests whether the user's storage honors fsync

This is the same situation Postgres and every other database is in. There's no magic.
Recovery on partial corruption
What if a file's checksums don't match — say, a single bad sector developed after the file was successfully written?
Our checksums detect it. Now what?
For a single corrupt page in an otherwise intact file:

The page is unreadable; we can't serve queries that need it
The system marks the file as suspect, surfaces the error
Other pages in the file are still usable

Operators can:

Restore from backup
Manually delete the file and accept data loss for affected rows
Trigger compaction to rewrite (if other replicas exist via v2 replication)

For an unreadable footer (the worst case):

The file is unusable entirely
We can't tell what's in it
Same options as above

The system never silently returns corrupt data. We always surface the error.
The fsync flexibility for users who don't need full durability
Some users have weaker durability requirements:

Analytics workloads where occasional data loss is acceptable
Caches that can be rebuilt
Dev/test environments

For these, we offer durability levels:
durability = full (default): WAL fsync per group commit. Acknowledged writes survive crashes.
durability = sync (looser): WAL written but fsync deferred to a background flusher every N ms. Acknowledged writes survive process crashes but might not survive OS crashes.
durability = async (loosest): WAL written to memory buffer, flushed periodically. Acknowledged writes are not durable until flush completes.
durability = none (no WAL): writes go straight to memtable, no WAL. On crash, all uncommitted data is lost. Useful for ephemeral testing only.
This is a per-session or per-table setting. Most users use the default. Users who explicitly opt out know what they're trading.
This is the same model as MongoDB (write concern), Cassandra (consistency levels), and the ecosystem generally. We're following convention.
Backup
Two backup strategies, addressing different needs:
Logical backup: dump all data as SQL or a wire-format file. Slow, large, but portable across database versions and machines. Good for archival and migration.
Physical backup: copy the data files and catalog directly. Fast, exact, but version-specific. Good for routine operational backups.
For v1, we ship physical backup with two modes:
Cold backup: shut down the database, copy all files (data directory, WAL, catalog), restart. Simple, safe, but requires downtime.
Hot backup: while running, snapshot consistently:

Trigger a checkpoint
Note the checkpoint LSN
Copy all files referenced in the catalog at the checkpoint LSN
Copy WAL files from the checkpoint LSN onward
Backup is complete; the running system continues normally

The copy itself is filesystem-level (cp -a, rsync, snapshot if filesystem supports it like ZFS or btrfs). We don't implement our own copy protocol.
The hot backup procedure works because our files are immutable. Once a file is in the catalog, it never changes; copying it is safe. The catalog itself is a small file (redb); copying it captures a consistent snapshot.
For an incremental backup: on subsequent backups, only files added since last backup need copying. Since files are immutable, "incremental" is straightforward — just track which files have already been backed up.
Restore
Restore is straightforward when backup is consistent:

Stop the database (if running)
Restore the data directory from backup
Start the database
WAL replay completes recovery from the backup's LSN

For point-in-time recovery (PITR): if WAL files are retained, we can recover to any LSN between the backup time and now. Restore the backup, then replay WAL only up to the target LSN.
PITR requires WAL retention beyond the checkpoint-driven defaults. Configurable retention duration (default: 7 days, configurable).
Backup verification
A backup that doesn't restore is no backup. We ship a verification tool: given a backup directory, runs a test restore in a sandbox and validates queries against expected results.
Best practice: run verification periodically. Users who skip this discover their backup is bad only when they need it.
Honest tradeoffs
Group commit latency cost: individual commits wait up to 100μs for group formation. For very-low-latency workloads, this is annoying. Mitigation: configurable wait time; users can tune.
WAL retention for PITR: keeping 7 days of WAL on disk costs storage (for a busy database, gigabytes per day). Users who don't need PITR can configure shorter retention.
Checkpoint flush overhead: forcing memtable flushes for checkpoints adds I/O bursts. Frequent checkpoints = more bursts. Tunable but tradeoff exists.
Backup of large databases: physical backup of a multi-TB database is slow even with snapshot-aware filesystems. For huge systems, ZFS/btrfs snapshots are essentially free; for ext4/xfs, full copy is required. Document this.
Single-disk durability: we assume one disk. RAID for redundancy, multi-disk striping for performance, replication for high availability — all handled at deployment layer or in v2. Single-disk corruption that survives our checksums (extremely rare) is a possible vector.
Recovery time bound by WAL size: a long-running database with high write rate generates lots of WAL. Recovery can take minutes for big systems. Mitigated by checkpoints but not eliminated.
Three places to push on
Group commit defaults: I chose 100μs max group wait. This balances throughput and latency. Some workloads benefit from longer waits (e.g., 1ms for batch ingestion); others need shorter (e.g., 10μs for ultra-low-latency apps). Default is a guess; tuning is per-workload.
Logical backup deferred: I argued physical backup for v1, logical for v2. The cost: physical backups aren't portable across versions. Users upgrading the system can't restore old backups easily. Workaround: keep the old version's binary around for restores. Reasonable to argue logical should ship v1.
Aggressive checkpointing default: every 5 minutes is conservative. Some systems checkpoint hourly. Frequent checkpoints reduce recovery time at the cost of write overhead. The right answer depends on workload. We default conservative; users who care can tune.

Locking recovery and durability:

Durability contract: acknowledged writes survive process crashes and power loss assuming honest hardware fsync; checksums detect corruption
One WAL with logical record types: INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE_ROW, COMMIT, ABORT, CHECKPOINT, schema/file metadata events
LSN is the global monotonic ordering primitive; CRC32 per WAL record
WAL fsync at group commit boundary; configurable wait up to 100μs default
Files written via temp-then-rename; immutable after rename; corruption detection via three-layer checksums (file, section, page)
Checkpoint procedure: flush memtables, fsync files, write CHECKPOINT record; bounds recovery time
Default checkpoint trigger: every 5 minutes or 1GB of WAL
Recovery: open catalog → identify transactions → replay WAL from checkpoint → verify file integrity → resume
Configurable durability levels: full (default) / sync / async / none
Hot backup procedure: checkpoint, copy data files + WAL since checkpoint, no downtime
WAL retention configurable for PITR (default 7 days)
Backup verification tool ships with system
Document hardware assumptions: requires honest fsync (recommend enterprise SSDs with PLP)


That closes recovery and durability. Combined with the storage and transaction work, the system now makes precise promises about data safety.
