What we ship
Concretely, what does a v1 release of LL look like as artifacts?
A single static binary. The Rust compile target. Statically linked where possible (musl on Linux for true static, or dynamically linked against glibc with versioned deps as fallback). Sizes around 80-120MB. Contains the database engine, the SQL parser, the wire protocol servers, the embedding pipeline, everything internal.
The principle: one binary, one process. No microservices, no sidecar containers, no internal RPC. Operations is dramatically simpler when there's one thing to deploy, one log to read, one process to monitor.
This is Postgres's pattern, ClickHouse's pattern, every modern self-hostable database. We follow it.
Platform builds. Linux x86_64 (the workhorse), Linux aarch64 (Graviton, Ampere — common in cloud now), macOS (Apple Silicon — for developer machines). Windows: not v1. Server-side databases on Windows are uncommon enough that we punt.
Container images. Official Docker images on Docker Hub and GitHub Container Registry. Minimal base (distroless or Alpine-with-musl). Multi-arch manifests.
Kubernetes manifests. Helm chart with reasonable defaults. We'll get to deployment patterns shortly.
CLI tool. Same binary, different entry points: ll server, ll client, ll admin. The CLI handles backups, restores, schema dumps, configuration validation, diagnostics.
Language SDKs. Python (PyPI), TypeScript (npm), Rust (crates.io) per our wire protocol discussion. Build matrices for the wrapper SDKs target every supported platform.
Documentation site. Static site, versioned, searchable. Postgres-quality is the standard; nobody adopts a database with bad docs.
Helm chart and Terraform modules. For Kubernetes and AWS/GCP/Azure deployments.
The deployment shapes
Three legitimate ways to run LL, each for different audiences:
Single-process embedded (like SQLite, DuckDB). The application links the LL library directly; no separate server. For: edge applications, mobile, single-tenant tools, agentic apps with embedded knowledge stores.
Cost: most users don't want this; it forces them to manage the data lifecycle in-process.
We support this in principle — our Rust library can be embedded — but it's not the primary v1 deployment. We document it; we don't optimize for it. Maybe 5% of users.
Single-node server (like Postgres on one box). The LL binary runs as a daemon, applications connect via wire protocol. For: most production deployments below the scale ceiling, dev/staging environments, smaller production workloads.
Single-node is what we've been designing. It's the v1 sweet spot. Everything we've discussed assumes single-node.
Capacity ceiling: limited by the hardware. We support up to ~5-10TB of data per single node comfortably; up to ~50TB with appropriate hardware (large SSDs, lots of RAM). Beyond that, distribution becomes necessary.
For most v1 customers this ceiling is irrelevant. They're well below it.
Single-node with read replicas (v2 architecture). Primary node accepts writes; replicas accept reads. Sync via WAL streaming. For: scaling read throughput, geographic read locality, high availability.
This isn't full distribution — there's still one writer. But it's a critical step toward scale and HA without the complexity of distributed consensus. Many production databases run for years in this configuration without ever needing full distribution.
Deferred to v2 but architecturally accommodated. We'll discuss in the distribution conversation.
Hardware sizing
What do we recommend for production?
Storage: NVMe SSD is mandatory. Spinning disks make compactions painful and HNSW page reads catastrophic. Cloud equivalent: gp3/io2 on AWS, pd-ssd/pd-extreme on GCP. For high write throughput: local NVMe (instance store) gives best fsync latency.
RAM: scale with data size and concurrent query count. Rules of thumb:

Buffer pool target: 30-50% of total data size, capped at available RAM
Plus 1-4GB per concurrent heavy query (memory budgets)
Plus ~10% headroom for OS and other processes
For an 100GB dataset with 20 concurrent queries: ~50GB buffer pool + 40GB query memory + 10GB headroom = 100GB RAM minimum

CPU: scale with query concurrency and embedding workload. Vector distance computation is the hottest path for AI workloads. Modern CPUs with AVX-512 give 2x speedup over AVX2. For GPU-accelerated embedding models: separate GPU resources, allocated to embedding workers specifically.
Network: for clients querying the server, modest bandwidth (1-10Gbps typical). For replicas (v2): high bandwidth for WAL streaming.
We publish sizing guidance per common workload (RAG, semantic search, hybrid queries). Users plug in their data size and QPS, get a recommended instance type.
Configuration management
Every database has a configuration nightmare. Postgres has hundreds of GUCs. We aim for fewer but it's not zero.
Configuration sources, in order of precedence:

Runtime SET commands (highest)
Per-user settings in catalog
Per-table settings in catalog
Configuration file (ll.conf in TOML format)
Environment variables (mostly for secrets)
Compiled-in defaults (lowest)

The configuration file:
