The principle: every setting has a sensible default; configuration is for tuning, not for making the system work.
We ship ll admin config validate which checks the config file for typos, conflicting settings, and obvious mistakes. We ship ll admin config show which prints current effective config with provenance (where each value came from).
Hot reload of configuration
Most settings can be changed via SET without restart. Some require restart:

Listen addresses
Data directories
Number of compaction workers
Buffer pool size (could theoretically resize but complexity isn't worth it for v1)

Settings that require restart are clearly marked in documentation and in ll admin config show.
For SET commands, we maintain a hot-reload protocol where the running system picks up the new value within a few seconds. Per-session SET is immediate; per-system SET propagates.
Upgrades
The painful operation. How do we make upgrades safe?
Patch versions (x.y.Z bumps): bug fixes only, fully backward compatible.

File format: unchanged
Wire protocol: unchanged
Configuration: unchanged or additive only
Procedure: stop, replace binary, start. Should work without coordination.

Minor versions (x.Y.z bumps): new features, backward compatible.

File format: extended additively (new sections in footers, new optional fields)
Wire protocol: extended additively
Configuration: new options with defaults, no breaking changes
Procedure: same as patch.

Major versions (X.y.z bumps): breaking changes possible.

File format may have changed; we provide migration
Wire protocol may have changed; we maintain at least one prior version
Configuration may require changes; migration tool helps
Procedure: backup, run migration tool, upgrade, restart. Documented per-version.

The discipline: major versions are rare (once every 1-2 years). Most upgrades are minor or patch and should be trivial.
For file format migration: shipped as an admin command (ll admin migrate) that converts old files to new format in-place or in-parallel. Compaction can do this automatically too as files get rewritten.
For zero-downtime upgrades (v2 territory with replicas): rolling upgrade through replicas, then primary failover. Single-node necessarily has downtime during binary swap.
