The CLI is the operator's interface. It's stable, well-documented, gracefully handles errors, and outputs JSON when piped (for scripting).
Monitoring patterns
What do operators monitor?
The standard golden signals:

Latency (query latency p50/p95/p99)
Traffic (queries per second)
Errors (error rate, error types)
Saturation (CPU, memory, buffer pool hit rate, disk space, connection count)

The LL-specific signals:

Compaction queue depth and rate
Embedding pipeline queue depth and processing rate per model
Memtable count and pressure
WAL fsync latency
File count per tier (a leading indicator of compaction lag)

We ship Grafana dashboards (JSON definitions) for these. Plug into a Prometheus instance scraping our metrics endpoint; the dashboards work out of the box.
We ship alerting rules (Prometheus AlertManager rules) with sensible defaults:

Critical: WAL fsync p99 > 100ms (storage problem), buffer pool hit rate < 50% (undersized), connection count > 90% capacity
Warning: compaction lag > 1 hour, embedding queue depth > 10K, disk usage > 80%

Users adapt these to their needs. The defaults catch most production issues.
Logs
Three log streams:
Server log: operational events from the engine. Startup, shutdown, configuration loaded, compactions, errors. Structured JSON with timestamp, severity, component, message, key-value details.
Slow query log: per the observability section, queries exceeding threshold. JSON lines.
Audit log: security/compliance events. JSON lines, append-only.
All logs go to stdout/stderr by default (12-factor app principle), with optional file destinations. Standard log levels (debug, info, warn, error, fatal). Configurable per-component log level.
Log shipping: we don't ship a log shipper. We expect users to use whatever they prefer (Vector, Fluent Bit, Filebeat, Logstash). Our log format is structured JSON that works with any of them.
Capacity planning
Operators need to predict when they'll outgrow their setup.
We help by surfacing growth indicators:

Data growth rate (bytes/day, files/day)
Query latency trend (is p99 increasing?)
Memory pressure trend (is buffer pool hit rate decreasing?)
Compaction backlog trend

These are visible in the standard dashboards. Forecasting (linear projection to capacity limit) is in ll admin diagnose.
The conversation: "Based on current growth, you'll hit your buffer pool ceiling in 6 weeks. Consider scaling up RAM or sharding."
The Kubernetes deployment story
For teams running on Kubernetes (most cloud-native deployments), we ship a Helm chart.
The deployment unit: a StatefulSet with one or more replicas. v1 is single-instance (one StatefulSet pod). v2 adds read replicas (additional pods).
Persistent volumes for: data directory, WAL directory (often separate for performance — WAL on fast local SSD, data on network-attached SSD).
The Helm chart parameterizes everything reasonable:

Storage class for each volume
Resource requests/limits
Configuration via values.yaml
Secrets via Kubernetes Secrets
Service definitions for native and Postgres protocols
Optional monitoring sidecar (Prometheus exporter is in-process, but pod-level annotations for scraping)

We document common patterns (using GP3 in EKS, using node-local NVMe for WAL, hot-standby with Patroni-style coordinator) in the operations guide.
The cloud deployment story
For teams using cloud providers, we provide Terraform modules:

AWS: EC2 with EBS, RDS-style managed deployment (eventually)
GCP: GCE with persistent disks
Azure: similar

The modules handle: instance sizing, disk provisioning, networking, security groups, IAM roles, backup to object storage.
We're explicitly not building a managed service in v1. The Terraform modules let users run on their cloud with minimal config; a fully managed service is a future business decision.
Failure modes that aren't crashes
Recovery handles crashes. What about other failure modes?
Disk filling up. The system detects when the data directory is at 90% capacity. Behavior:

Switch writes to throttle mode (every commit waits an increasing duration)
Log warnings prominently
Trigger emergency compaction (clear up tombstones, drop garbage)
At 95%: stop accepting writes, reads continue
At 99%: read-only mode

This gives operators time to add storage or clean up.
Memory pressure. When buffer pool can't satisfy demand and OS starts swapping:

Detect via slow read latencies
Surface warning
If queries start failing OOM: reject new queries, allow current to finish
Recommend remedies in error messages (resize buffer pool, add RAM)

We don't auto-resize buffer pool. Explicit operator action.
Embedding model unavailable. If the embedding API stops responding:

Workers retry with backoff
After max retries, jobs marked failed
New inserts: embedding column null; row succeeds
Operator notification via metrics/logs
When API returns: workers automatically resume

The principle: the database stays up even when external dependencies fail. Quality of service degrades, but the system keeps serving.
Slow disk. If fsync latency exceeds threshold (say 100ms p99), operators see metric alerts. The cause is usually disk degradation; remediation is hardware-level.
Query of death. If a query crashes the process repeatedly:

We track recent crash patterns
If a specific query hash is associated with crashes, we refuse to execute it (with clear error explaining why)
Operators can override after investigation

This isn't elegant but it prevents repeated crashes destroying availability while a bug is diagnosed.
Schema corruption. If the catalog file (redb) gets corrupted somehow:

We detect on open
We attempt to recover from redb's internal consistency mechanisms
If unrecoverable: refuse to start, surface clear error, point to backup restoration

The catalog is small and easily backed up; restore is usually feasible.
Honest tradeoffs
Single-binary, single-process simplicity: this is a strength for operations, but limits some architectural choices. A modular plugin system (different storage engines, different model providers as plugins) would be useful but adds complexity. We trade flexibility for operability.
Configuration surface: I sketched ~40 settings. Real systems have more. We'll grow this; the principle is hide complexity behind sensible defaults, expose tunables for people who need them.
No managed service in v1: customers who want zero ops can't get it. The Terraform modules and Helm chart help but aren't a managed product. Decision: defer managed service to product strategy; ship self-hosted v1.
Limited Windows support: server-side. We're betting on Linux/macOS being enough. If a customer needs Windows, they can run in WSL or container.
Manual scaling: operators decide when to scale, what to scale, how. No auto-scaling. Auto-scaling would require either v2 distribution or hooks into cloud provider APIs. v1 punt.
Three places to push on
Single-process vs. modular architecture: I argued single binary. A more modular approach (storage engine as a separate process, query engine separate, embedding workers separate) would be more cloud-native but harder to operate. I think for v1 monolithic wins; reasonable to disagree for users targeting Kubernetes-native architectures.
Helm chart and Terraform as v1: takes real engineering effort to ship and maintain. The alternative is documenting how to run on each platform and letting users build their own. We invest in the tooling because adoption depends on easy deployment. Cost is real but justified.
Auto-throttle on disk pressure: I argued for graceful degradation. The alternative is hard failure (just stop accepting writes). Hard failure is simpler and signals problems clearly; graceful degradation hides problems for a while. I lean graceful because it gives operators time to react; reasonable to argue for fail-fast.

Locking operations:

