The threat model
Before discussing defenses, what are we actually defending?
Threats we defend against:

Unauthorized network access: someone reaching the database from outside should fail authentication
Privilege escalation: an authenticated user with low privileges shouldn't be able to read data they don't have access to
Data theft via storage: someone with disk access (cloud provider, lost backup) shouldn't read plaintext data
Network eavesdropping: someone on the network shouldn't read query traffic
Credential leakage: API keys and passwords stored in our system shouldn't be retrievable in cleartext
Audit trail tampering: actions should be logged in a way that's hard to silently erase
Injection attacks: SQL/parameter injection should be prevented at the protocol layer

Threats we don't defend against:

Root access on the server: if someone has root on the LL host, the game is over. Our process can be inspected, memory dumped, keys extracted. This is the standard limit.
Compromised hardware: TPM-rooted security, attestation, etc. Out of scope; that's confidential computing territory.
DDOS: rate limiting helps, but a determined attacker can overwhelm any single-node system. Mitigation lives at the network edge.
Side-channel attacks: timing attacks on query latency, etc. Possible to mount; expensive to fully mitigate. Standard databases also don't.

This sets reasonable expectations. We're a database, not a fortress.
Authentication
Who is connecting? We need to know.
We outlined this in the wire protocol section. To recap and deepen:
Password-based authentication. Default for v1. Username and password sent over the wire (must be over TLS — see encryption in transit). Server validates against _system.users.password_hash.
Hash function: Argon2id with sensible parameters (memory=64MB, iterations=3, parallelism=4). Argon2 won the Password Hashing Competition for good reason; it's the modern standard, resistant to GPU/ASIC attacks, takes time to compute (deliberately).
Password storage: only the hash. Never plaintext, even briefly in memory beyond the verification window.
Password requirements: configurable policy (minimum length, complexity, etc.). Default: minimum 12 characters, no specific character class requirements (NIST guidance now favors length over complexity).
SCRAM-SHA-256 authentication. For Postgres wire compatibility. Implements the SCRAM challenge-response protocol so passwords don't traverse the wire as plaintext. The same password hash can support both methods (different KDF wrapping; details).
API key authentication. Long random strings (256 bits of entropy, encoded as base64url). Sent in connection headers or as the password field with a sentinel username (like __api_key__).
API keys stored as hashes, not plaintext. Just like passwords. The actual key is shown to the user only at creation time; the system stores hash(key). Verification re-hashes the presented key and compares.
API keys can have:

Scopes: which tables/operations they can access
Expiration: optional time limit
Revocation: instant invalidation by deleting the key record

Use case: programmatic access from applications, scripts, services. Preferred over passwords for non-human users.
OAuth/OIDC. Deferred to v2. The mechanics: the system validates JWT tokens from an external identity provider (Auth0, Okta, Google Workspace, etc.). Tokens carry claims; we map claims to users and roles.
mTLS (mutual TLS). Also deferred to v2. Client certificates authenticate the client; their subject DN maps to a user.
Service-to-service tokens. For LL components talking to each other (catalog, workers) and to LL talking to embedding models. Internal tokens, rotated periodically.
Authentication context
Once authenticated, the session carries a context:

User ID and name
Role memberships
Granted privileges (cached at session start, refreshed periodically)
Source IP (for audit)
Authentication method used (for audit and policy decisions)

This context flows through every query the session executes.
Authorization: the RBAC model
We outlined the basic RBAC in the wire protocol section. Let me deepen it.
The hierarchy:
User → Roles → Privileges → Objects
A user belongs to one or more roles. A role has privileges. A privilege is an action (SELECT, INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE, CREATE, ALTER, DROP, EXECUTE) on an object (table, column, model, role).
Roles can inherit from other roles, forming a DAG. The admin role typically has all privileges; the readonly role has SELECT on everything; custom roles fill the middle ground.
The DDL we've already sketched:
sqlCREATE ROLE analyst;
GRANT SELECT ON documents TO analyst;
GRANT EXECUTE ON MODEL openai_small TO analyst;
GRANT analyst TO alice;
The DML enforcement: every query, before execution, the planner checks privileges. Reading from a table requires SELECT on that table. Inserting requires INSERT. Using an embedding model in a query (EMBED('text', 'model_name')) requires EXECUTE on that model.
This privilege check is at planning time, not execution time. If you don't have privilege, the query is rejected before any work happens.
Column-level access control. Some columns are more sensitive than others. We support GRANT SELECT (id, title) ON documents TO analyst — analyst can read id and title but not body. The planner enforces by either rejecting queries that read forbidden columns, or by projecting them to NULL.
For v1: we support column-level SELECT grants. Column-level INSERT/UPDATE (which columns can be written) is more complex; deferred to v2.
Row-level security (RLS). Some users should only see certain rows. Example: tenant isolation in a multi-tenant SaaS — user alice belongs to tenant X and can only see rows where tenant_id = X.
The mechanism: a policy expression attached to a table:
sqlALTER TABLE documents ENABLE ROW LEVEL SECURITY;
CREATE POLICY tenant_isolation ON documents
    FOR SELECT, INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE
    USING (tenant_id = current_user_tenant());
The planner injects the policy expression as an additional predicate into every query against the table. Users can't bypass it because it's added at planning, not the user's query.
RLS is genuinely useful and a critical feature for multi-tenant applications. Postgres has it; we should too. I'd argue v1 should ship RLS because retrofitting it is painful and customers asking about multi-tenancy will need it.
Privilege on schema operations. CREATE TABLE requires CREATE on the database. DROP TABLE requires OWNER or DROP privilege. CREATE MODEL requires CREATE on the model namespace. These follow standard SQL-style privileges.
The owner concept. Each object has an owner (the user who created it). The owner has full privileges by default and can grant them to others.
Privilege evaluation performance
Authorization checks happen on every query. If they're slow, the system is slow.
Optimization: per-session, the user's effective privileges are computed once at session start and cached. Query planning checks the cache, not the catalog tables. Cache invalidated on GRANT/REVOKE — affected sessions reload their privileges.
For RLS, policy expressions are compiled into plan fragments and inserted; the runtime cost is the same as any predicate.
Total auth cost per query: <100 microseconds. Acceptable.
Encryption at rest
This is where we deferred a hard decision earlier. Now we tackle it.
Three levels of disk encryption:
Filesystem-level (LUKS, dm-crypt, FileVault, BitLocker). The operator encrypts the storage volume; LL writes plaintext to a filesystem that encrypts everything transparently. Pros: simple, transparent, no LL-side code. Cons: doesn't protect against attacker with kernel access; key management is the operator's problem.
Cloud provider-level (EBS encryption, GCS CMEK, Azure SSE). The cloud provider encrypts the underlying storage. Same pros/cons as filesystem-level but managed by cloud provider.
Application-level (LL encrypts data before writing). LL knows what's plaintext vs. ciphertext. Can support column-level encryption, key rotation, key-per-table. Pros: granular, defense against more attack surfaces. Cons: serious complexity, performance cost, harder to reason about.
For v1: rely on filesystem/cloud-provider encryption, not application-level.
Reasoning:

Application-level encryption is genuinely hard. Doing it right (KMS integration, key rotation, performance optimization) is months of work.
Most cloud deployments already encrypt at rest at the provider level.
The marginal security benefit of application-level over filesystem encryption is small for typical threat models.
v2 can add application-level for customers who need it (financial, healthcare, etc.).

We document this clearly: "LL relies on filesystem/cloud encryption for data at rest. Application-level encryption is on the roadmap for v2."
This is similar to Postgres's stance (TDE is offered by enterprise distributions but not core).
The exception: secrets in the catalog.
The catalog stores secrets (API keys for embedding models, etc.). These need application-level encryption even if the disk is otherwise plaintext.
Approach:

A master encryption key (MEK) is derived from a passphrase or read from an external KMS at startup
Secrets in the catalog are encrypted with the MEK before storage
The MEK never goes to disk; it lives only in memory during operation
On restart, MEK must be provided (passphrase prompt, KMS integration, etc.)

Implementation: AES-256-GCM for the actual encryption. Standard, fast, authenticated.
The catalog schema for secrets:
sql_system.secrets (
    secret_id UUID PRIMARY KEY,
    name TEXT,
    encrypted_value BYTEA,    -- AES-256-GCM ciphertext
    nonce BYTEA,
    created_at TIMESTAMP,
    created_by UUID
)
When a model is registered with api_key = SECRET('openai_key'), the catalog stores a reference to a secret named openai_key. The value is encrypted; only authorized code paths can decrypt and use it.
The MEK passphrase: at startup, the operator provides it via:

Interactive prompt (ll server start --ask-passphrase)
Environment variable (LL_MASTER_PASSPHRASE)
File (ll server start --passphrase-file /run/secrets/ll-mek)
AWS KMS, GCP KMS, HashiCorp Vault integration (ll server start --kms aws://...)

The last option is the production recommendation. The MEK is derived from a KMS-managed key; the KMS handles key custody.
Encryption in transit
Wire protocols must support TLS. For v1:
Native protocol (gRPC): TLS via standard gRPC TLS configuration. Server certificate, optional client certificate (mTLS, v2). Strong cipher suites enforced (TLS 1.3 only, or TLS 1.2 with vetted ciphers as fallback).
Postgres wire: standard Postgres SSL/TLS startup negotiation. We support TLS 1.2+ on the Postgres wire. Server certificate; client cert optional (deferred to v2).
Telemetry/observability endpoints: TLS-protected. Authentication required for the metrics endpoint by default (some users prefer cleartext metrics on private networks; configurable).
Configuration:
toml[security]
require_tls = true                       # reject non-TLS connections
tls_cert_file = "/etc/ll/tls/server.crt"
tls_key_file = "/etc/ll/tls/server.key"
tls_ca_file = "/etc/ll/tls/ca.crt"       # for mTLS
tls_min_version = "1.2"                  # 1.2 or 1.3
Default for production: require TLS. Default for development (running on localhost): TLS optional.
Certificate management
Operators need certificates. We don't bundle a CA. Standard options:

Self-signed certificates (dev/internal only)
Let's Encrypt (for publicly-accessible deployments)
Internal corporate CAs
Cloud-provider-managed certificates (ACM, Cloud Load Balancing certs)

We document the patterns; we don't impose one.
For internal microservices accessing LL: typically mTLS with internal CA, or service mesh handles transit security and we accept connections from the mesh.
Secrets management beyond the MEK
Secrets that LL stores:

Embedding model API keys (OpenAI, Cohere, etc.)
Webhook URLs and authentication
External system credentials (if we add features like change data capture targets)
Backup destination credentials (S3 keys, GCS credentials)

All stored in the encrypted secrets table, accessed via the SECRET() reference in DDL.
Secrets that LL needs but doesn't store:

The master encryption key (operator-provided per startup)
TLS private keys (on disk, protected by filesystem permissions)
Database user passwords (stored as Argon2 hashes, never decryptable)

The secrets management subsystem:
sql-- Create a secret
CREATE SECRET openai_key WITH VALUE 'sk-...';

-- Reference a secret in DDL
CREATE MODEL openai_small AS HTTP_API (
    endpoint = 'https://api.openai.com/v1/embeddings',
    api_key = SECRET('openai_key'),
    ...
);

-- Rotate a secret
UPDATE SECRET openai_key WITH VALUE 'sk-new-...';

-- Delete (if not referenced)
DROP SECRET openai_key;
Secrets can't be SELECTed back — there's no way to retrieve the decrypted value via SQL. The DDL writes them; only internal code paths reading them for their declared use can decrypt.
The AI-native security concerns
This is where our system differs meaningfully from generic databases.
Embedding pipeline talks to external services. The embedding workers call APIs (OpenAI, etc.) with potentially sensitive text data. This is a data leak if the embedding service is compromised or if API requests are intercepted.
Defenses:

TLS for all external API calls (standard)
API key rotation (operators can rotate easily)
Per-model audit logging (every embedding call logged with caller, model, timestamp — but not the actual text, to avoid the audit log itself being a data exfiltration target)
Option to mark certain columns as "do not embed externally" — they must use local models

The last point is genuinely useful for sensitive data. Healthcare/financial data shouldn't go to OpenAI's API. We support:
sqlCREATE TABLE patient_records (
    ...
    notes TEXT,
    notes_embedding VECTOR(1024) EMBED FROM notes USING local_model_only
);
The system enforces that notes_embedding can only use models marked as local = true in the model registry. Any attempt to migrate to a non-local model is rejected.
Vector queries leak query intent. When a user queries EMBED('confidential project Alpha'), the query text reaches the embedding service. This is information leakage even without storing the data there.
The defense is the same — use local models for sensitive workloads. The embedding cache also helps; cache hits don't generate external traffic.
Model registry as attack surface. If an attacker can register a malicious model, they can intercept queries (model receives query text) or write data (model returns chosen vectors).
Defense: CREATE MODEL requires elevated privileges (typically only admins). The model is registered with a specific endpoint URL; this can't be changed without dropping and recreating. Audit log tracks model lifecycle.
For paranoid environments: model registration restricted to a whitelist of approved providers; admin must explicitly approve new model URLs.
Indexed text and vector data is still data. If sensitive text is indexed in our text index, an attacker with query access can extract terms via probing queries. Same for vector queries — attackers can probe the embedding space.
This is the side-channel problem. Defense: row-level security restricts what data is accessible; column-level encryption (v2) protects text/vectors from being indexed in cleartext.
Audit logging in depth
We mentioned audit log in observability. For security purposes, the audit log captures:

Authentication events (login, logout, failed attempts)
Authorization events (privilege grants, revocations)
Schema changes (DDL operations)
Privilege escalations (changes to admin roles)
Model lifecycle (CREATE, DROP MODEL)
Secret lifecycle (without values)
Data access (configurable per-table: log SELECTs on sensitive tables; not all SELECTs by default)
Configuration changes

The audit log is append-only. It can be queried but not modified through normal DDL/DML.
For tamper resistance: optionally, audit log entries can be hash-chained — each entry includes a hash of the previous entry, making silent deletion detectable. v2 feature.
Audit log retention: configurable (default 1 year). Logs older than retention are archived to external storage if configured.
Audit log shipping: can be shipped to external SIEM systems (Splunk, Datadog, etc.) via OpenTelemetry or syslog.
Compliance considerations
Various regulations affect database deployments:

GDPR (EU): right to erasure (must be able to delete user data on request), data residency (data must stay in EU), consent tracking
HIPAA (US healthcare): encryption at rest and in transit (required), access logging, business associate agreements
SOC 2: access controls, audit logging, encryption, monitoring
PCI-DSS: cardholder data protection (we don't typically store this, but if a user does, certain controls apply)

We don't claim compliance certifications; we provide the building blocks customers need to be compliant:

Encryption at rest: filesystem-level documented as v1; application-level on v2 roadmap
Encryption in transit: TLS supported
Access controls: RBAC + RLS supported
Audit logging: comprehensive
Right to erasure: DELETE works; physical deletion via compaction or FORCE REWRITE
Backup/restore: supports point-in-time recovery

Customers running compliance-sensitive workloads use this combination, plus their own deployment hardening, to achieve compliance.
Honest tradeoffs
Encryption at rest deferred to v2: customers in regulated industries need application-level encryption now. They'll have to live with filesystem-level or wait for v2. Real cost to those customers; calculated trade.
Argon2id is slow: authentication takes ~100ms per attempt (intentionally). For interactive logins this is fine; for high-frequency programmatic access, prefer API keys (which can use a fast hash since they have high entropy).
RBAC complexity: granular permissions are powerful but operators must configure them. Wrong configuration is a real risk. Default to closed (deny everything not explicitly granted); document patterns clearly.
RLS in v1: I argued for shipping RLS in v1 despite the complexity. Retrofitting is painful. The cost is more code and more edge cases to test. Worth it.
External embedding API security: we can't fully secure data sent to external services. Defense in depth (TLS, key rotation, local-model option) helps but doesn't eliminate the risk. Customers must understand this; it's not specific to us but worth documenting.
No hardware security module integration: real production security at scale uses HSMs for the MEK. We support reading from external KMS (which can use HSMs internally) but don't directly integrate. Sufficient for v1; v2 might add explicit HSM support.
Three places to push on
Encryption at rest in v1 vs v2: I deferred. The argument for v1: it's a deal-breaker for some segments. The argument for v2: it's months of careful work and would delay other features. I lean v2 because the operational reality is that most customers use cloud-provider encryption anyway, and we can ship v1 sooner. Reasonable to argue v1 must have it.
RLS in v1: I argued ship it. The counter: RLS is genuinely complex (policy expressions, performance implications, write-side handling). Could defer to v2 if v1 simplicity matters more. I think the multi-tenancy use case is too important to defer.
Default require_tls = true vs false: I made it default off for development convenience. The counter: making TLS default-on is the secure default; users opt out for development. Default-off is a foot-gun for production. Reasonable to flip the default and require explicit opt-out for development.

Locking security:

Authentication: Argon2id-hashed passwords, API keys (also hashed), SCRAM-SHA-256 for Postgres wire; OAuth/mTLS deferred to v2
Authorization: RBAC with role inheritance, column-level grants for SELECT, row-level security with policy expressions
Privilege checks at planning time, cached per session
Encryption at rest: filesystem/cloud-provider level for v1; application-level deferred to v2
Catalog secrets encrypted with master encryption key (MEK), AES-256-GCM
MEK provided at startup via passphrase/env/file/KMS integration (KMS recommended for production)
Encryption in transit: TLS 1.2+ on both protocols; configurable cipher suites
Secrets management via CREATE SECRET / SECRET() reference; values never readable back
AI-native security: per-model audit logging, local-model-only constraint per column, model registry protection
Comprehensive audit log: authentication, schema, privilege, model, configuration changes
Audit log retention configurable; shipping to external SIEM supported
Hash-chained audit log deferred to v2
Compliance: we provide building blocks (RBAC, RLS, encryption, audit), not certifications


