I want to set expectations first: this conversation is about the v2+ architectural arc. We're not implementing this for v1. But the design decisions we make here constrain v1 — some choices are reversible later, others aren't. The point is to know which is which.
I'll work through this in order: why we deferred distribution (and what that bought us), the distribution layers and what each provides, the read-replica architecture (v2), sharding for write scale (v3), consensus and replication mechanics, multi-region considerations, and the things we deliberately won't do.
Why we deferred distribution: revisiting the decision
The early decision was single-node-first. The reasoning, restated honestly:
Distribution multiplies engineering cost by ~5x. Every layer we designed gets harder: storage gets sharding keys, the WAL becomes distributed, transactions become 2PC or Paxos, the query planner must reason about network costs, recovery becomes leader election, the catalog becomes a distributed object.
Most use cases don't need it. A single modern node can comfortably hold 5-10TB of data, serve thousands of QPS, and handle hundreds of concurrent connections. That covers the median database workload for years. The percentage of teams that genuinely need >10TB of vector data or >100K writes/sec is a small minority.
We made the right call. Single-node v1 lets us ship faster, debug easier, and reach the majority of customers. Distribution is the path for the customers who grow beyond v1's ceiling.
The downside: we accept that some customers will eventually want distribution and we'll need to deliver. The design space for that is large; we need to commit early to a direction so v1 doesn't make distribution impossible.
Layered distribution: what each layer provides
There's a tendency to think "distributed system" as one binary choice. It's actually a spectrum of capabilities, each addressing different needs:
Read replicas (v2): copies of the database that handle read queries, syncing from a primary. Adds read scale, geographic read locality, and high availability. Writes still go through a single primary. Most production databases run this way for years without ever needing more.
Active-standby (v2): a hot standby that takes over if the primary fails. Doesn't add throughput but adds availability. Often combined with read replicas.
Sharded writes (v3): data partitioned across nodes; each shard owns its slice. Each shard is essentially a single-node LL with its own primary. Writes scale linearly with shards. Cross-shard operations are expensive.
Distributed consensus (v3+): each shard has multiple replicas using Raft/Paxos for consistency. No single point of failure even within a shard. The most complex; what Spanner-like systems do.
Multi-region (v4+): shards spanning geographic regions. Adds latency and consistency tradeoffs. Conflict resolution becomes essential.
Each layer is additive on the previous. We don't commit to all of them — we commit to the path through them.
Our distribution path:
v1: Single-node only.
v2: Single-primary + read replicas + standby for HA.
v3: Sharded writes; each shard is single-primary + replicas.
v4+: Multi-region with explicit consistency models.
This is the conservative path. It mirrors how Postgres scales (with PostgreSQL+Patroni for v2 territory, with Citus or YugabyteDB for v3, with custom solutions for v4). Most teams stop at v2 and that's fine.
The radical alternative is jumping straight to distributed consensus from day one (like CockroachDB, FoundationDB). The cost: dramatically harder to build, harder to operate, harder to reason about performance. The benefit: scales to anything. For our user base (AI engineers building applications, not hyperscale infrastructure teams), the conservative path is right.
Read replicas (v2): the architecture
The simplest distribution layer. Most useful per unit of complexity.
The model:

One primary node accepts writes
One or more replica nodes copy data from the primary
Replicas serve reads
Optionally, one replica is designated standby (ready for failover)

What replicates:

Every WAL record from primary is shipped to replicas
Replicas apply WAL records to their own memtable and storage
Files: replicas can either receive files directly from primary or rebuild from WAL
Catalog updates: shipped via WAL (we extended WAL with catalog events earlier)

What doesn't replicate (different per replica):

Buffer pool state
Query cache
Embedding job queue (each node processes its own; primary owns the canonical queue)

WAL streaming protocol
The primary streams WAL records to replicas in real-time:

Primary's WAL writer appends records to its WAL files
Primary's replication thread reads new records and pushes them over the network to replicas
Replicas append received records to their local WAL
Replicas apply records to their state (memtable updates, catalog changes)
Replicas acknowledge: "I've durably received up to LSN N"

The acknowledgment is critical: it tells the primary the replica is keeping up.
Three streaming modes:
Asynchronous: primary doesn't wait for replica acks. Lowest latency for writes; replicas can fall behind. On primary failure, recent writes may be lost from replicas.
Synchronous: primary waits for at least one replica to acknowledge before commit returns. Adds network round-trip to write latency (~1ms intra-region, ~50-100ms inter-region). Guarantees no data loss on failover.
Quorum-based: primary waits for K of N replicas to acknowledge. Balances latency and durability.
For v2 we ship both sync and async modes, configurable per-cluster. Default is async (matches Postgres default). Sync available for users who prioritize data safety.
Replica consistency model
Replicas are eventually consistent with the primary by default. Reads from replicas see a snapshot of the primary as-of some LSN ≤ the primary's current LSN.
This means: read-your-writes is NOT guaranteed across primary/replica. If you write to primary and immediately read from a replica, the replica might not have the write yet.
Two options for clients that need read-your-writes:
Read from primary: always. Sacrifices replica read scale. Simplest.
Read with LSN target: client tracks the LSN at which their write committed. When reading, the client passes "read at LSN >= N." The replica waits until its applied LSN >= N before serving the read. Adds latency proportional to replication lag.
We support both via session settings:
sqlSET read_preference = 'primary';  -- always primary
SET read_preference = 'replica';  -- eventually consistent
SET read_preference = 'replica_with_lsn'; -- wait for specific LSN
This is the same pattern MongoDB uses (read preferences). It's the right amount of flexibility.
Failover
If the primary fails, one of the replicas becomes the new primary. How:

Replicas detect primary failure (heartbeat timeout, ~5 seconds)
Replicas coordinate to elect a new primary (typically the most up-to-date one)
The elected replica promotes itself: stops being a replica, accepts writes
Other replicas reconfigure to follow the new primary
Old primary, if it returns, becomes a replica of the new primary (after possible WAL truncation if it has divergent writes)

This requires a coordination layer. Options:
External coordinator: an external service (etcd, ZooKeeper, Consul) handles election. Replicas register with the coordinator; the coordinator decides who's primary. This is what Patroni does for Postgres. Simple, well-understood, requires running another system.
Built-in consensus: replicas elect among themselves via Raft or similar. No external dependency. More complex to implement; more self-contained.
For v2 I'd argue external coordinator via etcd or similar. Reasoning: teams running production databases already have an orchestrator (Kubernetes, Nomad) which provides coordination primitives. Adding etcd is incremental; building consensus into the database itself is months of careful work.
The integration: LL nodes participate in a coordinated cluster. The coordinator tracks "who's primary, who's replica, what's their LSN status." Failover decisions go through the coordinator.
We ship reference deployment for Kubernetes (using its etcd) and for standalone (running etcd alongside LL).
Split-brain protection
The classic distributed system failure: a network partition isolates the primary from replicas. Replicas elect a new primary. Then the network heals and the old primary is still accepting writes. Now you have two primaries; data conflicts; chaos.
Defense:

Fencing: the coordinator tells the old primary "you're no longer primary" via a STONITH-style mechanism. Old primary must give up writes or be killed.
Lease-based: primaries hold a lease from the coordinator that expires periodically. If they can't renew (due to partition), they step down before the lease expires. New primary can't be elected until old lease expires.

Lease-based is cleaner. We use it. The lease duration is configurable; default is 10 seconds. Trade-off: shorter lease = faster failover but more sensitive to network blips.
Replica catch-up
A replica that's been offline must catch up. Two cases:
Brief outage (replica's WAL is reasonably up to date): replay missing WAL records from primary. Limited only by WAL retention on primary.
Long outage (replica is far behind, primary WAL has been truncated): the replica needs to bootstrap. Primary sends a checkpoint (set of files at a known LSN), then WAL from that LSN forward. This is essentially "physical backup + WAL replay" performed live.
For very long outages or new replicas being added: ship the latest backup, then catch up via WAL. We provide tooling for this.
Operational mode of a v2 cluster
What does a typical v2 deployment look like?
Coordinator (etcd, 3 nodes)
    |
    +-- LL Primary (writes, some reads)
    |       |
    |       +-- WAL Stream --> LL Replica 1 (reads)
    |       +-- WAL Stream --> LL Replica 2 (reads, standby)
    |
    +-- Optional: LL Replica 3 (geographically distant, async)
Three LL nodes plus three etcd nodes. Clients connect through a load balancer that knows the cluster topology — writes to primary, reads to any node.
The load balancer can be:

A real load balancer (HAProxy, nginx, cloud LB)
A client-side router (our SDK can be cluster-aware)
A proxy layer (pgbouncer-style)

We support all three. The client SDK gets cluster-awareness in v2.
Sharding for write scale (v3)
When v2 hits the write throughput ceiling (single primary can't keep up), sharding partitions writes across multiple primaries.
The model:

Data is divided into N shards based on a sharding key (typically PK hash or range)
Each shard is essentially a v2 cluster (primary + replicas)
The cluster has a coordinator that routes queries to the right shard
Cross-shard queries are coordinated (more on this below)

What changes:

Catalog now tracks shard assignments
Query planner becomes shard-aware
Some queries are "single-shard" (fast); some are "cross-shard" (slower); some are "all-shards" (scan-based, expensive)
Transactions are bounded by shard for performance; cross-shard transactions exist but are expensive

Sharding key selection
The first design question. The sharding key determines:

How data is distributed
Which queries are single-shard (fast) vs cross-shard (slow)
Rebalancing strategy if shards become uneven

Common approaches:
Hash sharding: shard_id = hash(pk) mod N. Evenly distributed. Range queries on PK are cross-shard. Most queries that filter by PK are single-shard.
Range sharding: each shard owns a range of PKs. Range queries are single-shard or few-shard. Hot shards possible if data isn't uniformly distributed across ranges. Rebalancing is harder.
Composite sharding: hash by one column, then range within. Used by some systems for time-series data.
Geo-sharding: shard by user/tenant location. Good for geographic locality.
For LL, I'd propose hash sharding by PK as default, with explicit sharding key as an option for advanced users:
sqlCREATE TABLE documents (
    id UUID PRIMARY KEY,
    tenant_id UUID,
    ...
) DISTRIBUTED BY HASH (tenant_id);  -- explicit override
For multi-tenant systems, sharding by tenant_id ensures each tenant's data is co-located. Critical for performance.
Cross-shard query challenges
Some queries are inherently cross-shard:
Joins across shards: if documents is sharded by doc_id and users by user_id, joining them is expensive. The planner has to coordinate data movement.
Vector search across shards: a top-K vector query needs to find the K nearest across the entire corpus, not just one shard. This means querying every shard, getting per-shard top-K, merging globally.
Text search across shards: similar — query every shard, merge results.
Graph traversal across shards: edges may cross shard boundaries. Multi-hop traversals become distributed graph queries.
The planning rules:
Vector search: always all-shards (query every shard in parallel, merge top-K). Cost scales with number of shards but acceptable since each shard is smaller.
Text search: same as vector.
Graph traversal: starts on the shard containing the source node. Each hop may cross to other shards via the edge target's shard assignment. Expensive but bounded by traversal depth.
Single-shard queries: when the WHERE clause includes the sharding key, only the relevant shard is queried. Fast.
For v3, we expose EXPLAIN output showing which shards a query touches. Operators tune sharding keys to maximize single-shard queries.
Sharding the indexes
This is where it gets interesting. We have four index types; each behaves differently under sharding:
B-tree on sharding key: trivially shardable. Each shard has its own B-tree covering its data.
Vector index (HNSW): each shard builds its own HNSW. Query merges top-K across shards. Quality decreases slightly because HNSW quality is logarithmic in vector count; smaller shards have proportionally less quality.
Text index (FST + posting lists): each shard has its own. Query intersects across shards.
Graph index: this is the hard one. Edges can cross shards. The "source" of an edge is in one shard; the "target" might be in another. Storage of edges follows the source shard convention (each edge stored once, at the source's shard). Reverse traversal across shards requires querying all shards' reverse indexes.
Cross-shard edges add complexity. We accept this as a cost of sharded graphs. Real graph databases (TigerGraph, JanusGraph) handle this; the techniques are known.
Cross-shard transactions
The expensive operation. A transaction touching multiple shards must commit atomically — either all shards see it or none do.
Options:
Two-phase commit (2PC): classic protocol. Coordinator prepares all shards; once all are prepared, coordinator commits. If any shard fails to prepare, all roll back. Latency: 2 network round trips. Failure modes: coordinator failure mid-commit is the famous "in-doubt transaction" problem.
Paxos/Raft commit: more failure-resilient. The "commit" itself is a Paxos value agreed upon by participants.
Avoid cross-shard transactions: design data such that transactions naturally fit within one shard (tenant_id-based sharding helps). Cross-shard transactions become a rare exception.
For v3, we use 2PC with retry on coordinator failure. Pragmatic choice — well-understood, "good enough" for the vast majority of workloads. The minority of users with strict requirements can choose to enforce single-shard transactions only.
Distributed consensus per shard (v3+)
Within a shard, we still want HA. v3 ships shards with replication (primary + replicas, like v2). But for v3+, we can move to consensus-based replication where each shard is a Raft group.
The benefit: no single point of failure within a shard. Any minority of replicas can be down without affecting availability or data safety. Compared to primary/replica where the primary is a single point of failure.
The cost: more complex implementation, slightly higher latency per operation, requires majority of replicas to be up.
We make this a configuration option in v3+: shards can be primary/replica (simpler) or Raft groups (more resilient). Users pick based on their HA requirements.
Multi-region (v4+)
Beyond v3, multi-region distribution becomes useful for:

Geographic locality (low-latency reads near users)
Disaster recovery
Compliance (data residency)

The architectural choice:
Single primary across regions, replicas in other regions (async): writes go to one region (high latency from other regions); reads are geographically local. Simplest. Limited write scale.
Multi-primary with conflict resolution: writes can happen in any region; conflicts resolved via timestamps or application logic. Complex; appropriate for some workloads (high write loads tolerant of conflicts).
Sharded with primaries distributed across regions: each shard's primary is in a specific region; data follows the user. Complex sharding key design.
For LL's likely v4 customers (large AI applications with global users), I'd lean toward shards distributed by region with replicas in nearby regions. Each shard's primary is in the region where its data is most accessed; replicas in nearby regions for HA and read locality.
But honestly, v4 is years away and the right design depends on what customers actually need. We design v1-v3 to not foreclose options.
What we deliberately won't do
Some distributed system features we're rejecting:
Eventual consistency by default: some distributed databases (Cassandra, DynamoDB) default to eventual consistency for everything. We don't. Snapshot isolation is our default; eventual consistency is opt-in via replicas. This is what most users actually want.
Tunable per-row consistency: some systems (DynamoDB) let you choose consistency per request. We don't expose this complexity; cluster-level settings.
Distributed transactions across arbitrary tables and shards: 2PC across many shards has poor latency and reliability. We support it but discourage it. Schema design should minimize cross-shard transactions.
Hot data movement: moving data between shards while live. Useful but complex. v4+ if at all.
Geo-replication of full data: replicating every row to every region. Storage cost is enormous. We do this only when explicitly configured per-table.
The constraints from v1
What v1 decisions constrain or enable distribution?
Things that don't change with distribution:

File format (each node's files are local to that node)
HNSW page layout (per-file, per-node)
Buffer pool (per-node)
Compaction strategy (per-node)
Query planner internals (extended for distribution but not rewritten)
Embedding pipeline (per-node, or coordinated for cross-node consistency)

Things that change:

WAL: needs streaming protocol for replicas
Catalog: needs distributed coordination for schema changes (in v2, primary owns catalog; replicas follow)
Query planner: gets shard-aware optimization
Transaction commit: gets distributed coordination
Recovery: gets cluster-level recovery (failover protocols)
Configuration: gets cluster-level vs node-level settings

Things v1 must preserve to enable distribution:

Stable row IDs (file_id, offset) — these can't change semantically when distribution adds cluster context
Logical WAL records — they need to be replayable on a different node
Independent file format — each file must be readable without external state
LSN-based MVCC — this needs to compose with cluster-wide LSN coordination

I believe v1 preserves these. The design has been distribution-friendly from the start despite being single-node-first. This is the payoff for being deliberate.
Migration paths between versions
How do users move from v1 to v2 to v3?
v1 → v2: just add replicas. Configure WAL streaming. Existing files remain valid. No data migration. Downtime: minimal (replicas can sync while primary serves traffic).
v2 → v3: sharding requires resharding. We provide a tool that reads from v2 cluster, redistributes by sharding key, writes to v3 cluster. Live migration possible with brief downtime; full migration is hours-to-days for large datasets.
These migrations are real engineering efforts but well-defined. The architectural decisions preserve them.
Honest tradeoffs
Conservative path means slower scale-out for ambitious customers: customers with extreme scale needs may outgrow us before v3 is ready. They'll go to CockroachDB or similar. Real cost.
External coordinator dependency: requiring etcd (or similar) adds another system to operate. Teams used to self-contained databases see this as a regression. The alternative is implementing consensus ourselves, which is worse for v2 timeline.
Eventual consistency on replicas is a footgun: customers don't always understand the implications. Replica reads can see stale data. Read-your-writes from replicas requires explicit LSN handling. Real source of bugs in customer apps. We document heavily; some customers still trip on it.
Cross-shard query complexity in v3: queries that span shards are inherently slower. Users will write queries that look fast in v2 (single-node) and become slow in v3. We need clear documentation and operator tools.
Sharding key as a forever decision: once chosen, changing it requires full data migration. Users might not pick well at v3 launch and regret it. We document patterns and pitfalls; some customers still pick poorly.
Multi-region complexity ramps fast: v4 is where the design becomes truly hard. Different customers want different consistency models. We can't ship one design that fits all. Real risk of over-promising and under-delivering.
Three places to push on
Conservative path vs. distributed-from-day-one: I argued conservative. The aggressive path (distributed consensus from v1, like CockroachDB) is harder to build but scales better. For our user base (AI engineers building applications), conservative is right. For hyperscale-aspiring customers, conservative is a limitation. Reasonable to argue we should aim higher from the start.
External coordinator vs. built-in consensus: I argued external. The cost of external is operational complexity (running etcd). The cost of built-in is engineering effort (writing Raft). For v2 specifically, external wins on timeline. For mature systems, built-in is often better. Defensible either way.
Sharding granularity: I implied sharding by table-level keys. Some systems shard at finer granularity (by row ranges within a table dynamically). Finer granularity gives better rebalancing but more complexity. I lean coarser (table-level sharding key, fixed shards) for simplicity; reasonable to argue for finer.

Locking distribution:

Conservative scaling path: v1 single-node → v2 primary+replicas → v3 sharded → v4 multi-region
v2: WAL streaming for replication, sync/async/quorum modes; external coordinator (etcd) for failover
Read replicas with eventually-consistent reads; explicit LSN-based read targeting available
Lease-based primary fencing to prevent split-brain
v3: hash sharding by configurable key (default PK); per-shard primary/replica structure
Cross-shard queries always supported, performance is honest with operator
Vector/text searches naturally distributable as per-shard top-K with merge
Graph storage by source shard; cross-shard reverse traversal accepted cost
2PC for cross-shard transactions; encourage single-shard transaction design
Optional Raft-based replication per shard for v3+ (configurable per-cluster)
v4+ multi-region: shards distributed by region, replicas for HA and read locality
Reject: default eventual consistency, per-row consistency tuning, hot data movement (v3 era)
v1 preserves the structural primitives (row IDs, logical WAL, file independence, LSN-based MVCC) that enable distribution
Migration tooling for v1→v2 (trivial) and v2→v3 (resharding)


That closes distribution and that closes the architecture.
Let me step back. What have we actually built?
We've designed a database from physical bits to deployment topology. The full stack:
Storage layer: typed versioned tuples as physical records, row groups as PAX-layout files, embedded per-file indexes (HNSW, FST, CSR graph, B-tree), one unified WAL, LSM-shaped writes with memtable → flush → compaction, immutable files with three-tier checksums.
Metadata layer: catalog in redb with full in-memory caching, schema-evolution semantics, model registry as first-class objects.
Query layer: SQL with extensions plus Python DSL on top, logical plan IR with multi-modal operators as first-class, physical plan with cost-based selection, time-based cost model with multi-modal cardinality estimation including per-file vector centroid distributions.
Execution layer: morsel-driven parallelism via DataFusion+Arrow, per-query memory budgets with spillable operators, multi-modal operators with internal parallelism, MVCC visibility filtering integrated.
Transaction layer: Snapshot Isolation default, Read Committed available, LSN-based snapshots, optimistic concurrency with first-committer-wins.
Embedding subsystem: deferred-by-default with inline option, system table job queue, per-model caches and pipelines, dual-read migration model, model registry with audit trails.
Wire/SDK layer: gRPC native protocol with Arrow IPC results, partial Postgres wire for ecosystem access, Rust client as foundation for Python (PyO3) and TypeScript (napi-rs).
Observability: rich EXPLAIN with estimated-vs-actual, comprehensive per-query telemetry, system tables for every internal state, Prometheus metrics, OpenTelemetry tracing.
Recovery/durability: precise durability contract, group commit, checkpoints bounding recovery time, configurable durability levels, hot backup with point-in-time recovery.
Operations: single static binary, hardware-agnostic deployment, comprehensive CLI, Helm/Terraform tooling, graceful degradation on resource pressure.
Security: Argon2id-hashed credentials, RBAC with row-level security, encryption-in-transit by default, AES-256-GCM for secrets, KMS-rooted master key, comprehensive audit log, AI-native security model.
Distribution roadmap: v1 single-node, v2 read replicas, v3 sharded writes, v4+ multi-region; v1 architecture preserves the primitives needed for the full path.
What's genuinely novel about LL is not any single piece — every individual technique has prior art somewhere. What's novel is the integration:
A unified physical record format that supports relational, vector, text, and graph access patterns in one storage substrate with one WAL, one transaction model, one MVCC mechanism, one cost-based optimizer reasoning across all four. The architecture refuses the "federation of subsystems" trap and treats AI-native concerns (embeddings, model lifecycle, hybrid scoring) as first-class rather than bolt-on.
That's what we set out to design when you said "this needs a completely different approach." We did the work. The design is coherent end-to-end.

If you ship this, you've shipped something real.
